Barack Obama will be the fourth U.S. president to receive the Nobel Peace Prize
U.S. President Barack Obama will fly to Oslo in Norway, to spend 26 hours there and be the fourth U.S. president in history to receive the Nobel Peace Prize.
The US$1.4 million degree, medal and cheque will be presented to him, among other things, for his exceptional efforts to strengthen global diplomacy and collaboration among nations.
The head of the White House and his wife Michelle will land in the morning in the Norwegian metropolis and will have a busy schedule.
He will first visit the Nobel Institute, where he will meet for the very first time the five members of the Committee who chose him in October among 172 people and 33 organizations.
The schedule of the presidential couple then plans an interview with the King of Norway Harald V and Queen Sonja.
The visit will get his pinnacle in the afternoon at a solemn ceremony where Obama will receive the prestigious award.
He will be the fourth U.S. president to receive it, but only the third who will be awarded the prize in the Committee's own compound.
The White House informed in advance that Obama would talk about the war in Afghanistan during the award ceremony.
The president does not want to avoid this subject because he is aware that he is receiving this award by being a president who is waging a war in two countries right now.
He stepped up the war effort just a few days ago by sending additional units to Afghanistan, which none of his critics will forget to highlight.
During the ceremony, Obama will also receive a gold medal, a diploma and a check of ten million Swedish krorons (about 1.1 million euros).
He intends to donate them to charities, but he has not yet concretely decided which.
His busy program will then take him along with his wife to a banquet including, in addition to the king and queen of Norway, the head of government and 250 other guests.
Obama was quite reluctant at this price at the beginning.
For example, he mentioned the feeling that he did not fully deserve it.
He has also repeated several times that this is not a price for himself alone, but for all those who defend the same values as him.
As soon as the announcement, he said it would be an "invitation to act for him".
A defender of nature accused of blackmail
Litoměřice police charged the president of the Society of Protectors of the Nature of Litoměřice with blackmail.
On several occasions last year he appealed for several construction site procedures and demanded money from investors for his personal profit in exchange for withdrawing his call, said Alena Romová, spokesman for the Litoměřice police.
The president of the Society for the Protectors of Nature of Litoměřice is Lubomír Studnička.
He is currently in detention and faces up to three years in prison.
Waste sorting dumpsters aren't scented in Brno
In Prague everyone can sort its beverage packages, while in the villages of Southern Moravia you come across sorting dumpsters on every street corner.
What about Brno?
There, you would have to take several minutes before you find the right dumpsters.
I don't mean we wouldn't sort the trash in Brno at all.
But I have the impression that the municipal councillors of our city, the country's second largest city, are not sufficiently interested in this problem.
Brno is in the Stone Age of sorting waste not only because citizens can only sort paper, glass and PVC, but above all given the number of dumpsters dedicated to this waste.
Have you ever tried throwing a plastic bottle into the city centre?
It's a whole science, which mostly requires time and nerves of steel.
The only place I've found right now is Moravia Square in front of Potrefena Husa.
That Mr. Onderka and his colleagues don't blame me, but it doesn't seem like much.
Not to mention the fact that in other parts of the city it's not a sinecure either.
Finally, many of my friends don’t sort their trash.
By laziness.
It makes them a trot to get to the dumpsters.
I'm not surprised about them.
Myself, after a party, I offered to throw away a few bottles of glass and PVC.
Except I didn't see any Kounicova-street dumpsters.
Luckily I found the right place by taking the tram.
That being said, the waste was overflowing.
It's after all the center of Brno, who's supposed to best represent the city, right?!
It is probably that ecology and so-called aestheticism do not interest the socialists of Brno too much.
They prefer to buy the city a place in the first hockey league that shames the city all over the country, instead of buying a few extra dumpsters and offering the sorting of more raw materials for a cleaner Brno.
I'm happy to have been able to confirm my impression with official statistics after hanging out on the internet for a while.
According to data from EKO-KOM, Brno is really the worst in the entire southern Moravia region.
Paroubek's budget has taken away money from pensions and sick leave
After the left has increased, despite the opinion of the government, farmers or public servants it may lack money for pensions, sick leave or housing savings.
But also for interest on state debt or international legal proceedings.
This will increase the problems of those who will govern in the second half of next year, says Finance Minister Eduard Janota.
When the Social Democrats and Communists imposed the increase for certain classes, they were based on the fact that the government would make loans for sensitive expenses, pensions or sick leave. "It's just putting the problems back to later, undermining the ground," Janota said.
It should be known that on the money borrowed the interest to be paid is higher and that it will have to be returned sooner or later.
The state doesn't really have the choice to know where to draw money.
If he does not cut social assistance or his expenses, he will have to raise taxes.
To all those that the left has currently increased: firefighters, teachers, farmers.
And afterwards, to their children.
People will touch them and do not have to fear for their pensions or health insurance, of which 1.8 billion have disappeared.
But at the expense of a greater debt of the state - next year it will not lack 163 billion in the coffers, but more.
Despite the efforts of Minister Janota the debt will thus grow more and more quickly and with it, the expenses related to interest.
The state is going to need money to repay it and it will no longer be enough to slightly increase the VAT or reduce the maternity leave as the government did in its anti-deficit package.
He will also miss five billion to complete the construction of roads and railways because of the transfer of money to a few groups of voters.
These are mainly sections that have already been decided and started, the projects of which cannot be modified to possibly reduce costs.
At most keep them, which isn't free either.
This means delaying or stopping thirty major projects, and the withdrawal of European funds is also in danger, according to the warning of Karel Hanzelka, spokesman for the Ministry of Transport.
The ČSSD has proposed to resolve the financial deficit very skillfully - it will draw on the dividends of the ČEZ (supplier and distributor of electrical energy), which the State has for the most part and will supply the transport fund.
The concern is that the amount of dividends is never certain and that it is in no way a well without funds.
Last year, 18 billion were sailed from the ČEZ to the budget.
Every year, dividends are used to pay the removal of ecological damage, to supplement the pension accounts or serve as a reserve for the pension reform that is being prepared.
In addition, this trick increases the actual budget deficit by 5.7 per cent of GNP according to EU rules.
And it's far from the end of the transfer carnival.
The budget for various compensation to the victims of communism and crimes, or even housing savings, has decreased by almost three billion.
It is not certain that the state can act without this money.
That's a lottery.
If we lose an international procedure, we will have to pay for it anyway, says Janota.
The minister will then have to save on the side of the department or, in the worst cases, touch the government’s budget reserve.
There is also a shortage of one hundred million in the two-billion-dollar State Fund project, which must make it possible to track the expenditure of each administration online.
The first step will begin in January.
If the Minister of Finance did not find money elsewhere, the project will have to be halted and sanctions would take place, Janota warned.
Your next smartphone will master two operating systems
In the future, U.S. people are considering a mobile phone that will allow the user to switch from one operating system to another by pressing a single button.
Projects that have been revealed look promising.
Just one button and you switch from Windows Mobile to Android in a few seconds.
It is precisely this possibility which is the objective of the American company VMware, which deals mainly with the development of virtualization software for computers.
We’re going to allow you to manage two user profiles at the same time on your mobile phone.
You can alternate between them, you can differentiate them into staff and professional
Both will work at the same time, Srinivas Krishnamurti said in an interview for Computerworld magazine.
It was presented in November of last year and it was made a first demonstration a few days ago.
It will be on the market in 2012.
Virtualization on smartphones does not belong to fiction.
VMware has already successfully presented a test version to reporters of a smartphone equipped with two operating systems.
It is a modified Nokia N800 with 128 MB RAM, which simultaneously executed the mentioned Windows Mobile and Android systems.
The development of new technologies for mobile phones is booming.
VMware is now working with European and U.S. carriers to test virtualization on smartphones, and customers are expected to have access to devices in 2012.
The shortage of snow in the mountains bothers hoteliers
Deserted slopes are not only a problem for lift operators.
Lack of snow deters people from booking their ski stays in hotels and guesthouses.
As a result, it is always possible to find free rooms for all dates in winter, including Christmas and New Year.
We have a lot of visits to our website.
Internet users go through offers, ask for prices but don't dare to book at the moment.
And if they call us, they immediately ask if there will be snow or mud at Christmas,” said Martin Jandura, operator of the Spindl.Info news portal.
If we want to spend the New Year of his dreams in Špindlerův Mlýn, it is better not to hesitate too much to reserve.
If we give priority to the savings and the quality of the accommodation comes only afterwards, then we can try to wait a few more days.
Špindler hoteliers currently only offer one-week New Year stays.
“I think they’re going to try for a while to place them again, but they’ll also give in and accept shorter periods.
And then the number of free rooms will quickly decrease,” Jandura said.
Vrchlabí's Ingtours tourist agency also still offers free beds for all winter periods.
The week of Christmas interests us the most for the moment, it is not completely sold.
There's still free, let's say, half of the rooms in our offer.
That’s why we’ve already prepared a few cheaper last-minute Christmas breaks,” Ingtours director Petr Schiefert said.
Vrchlabí's tourist agency can also offer the New Year in Krkonoše, but there are fewer free places.
The first fourteen days of February leave just as well, the remaining winter stays are sold moderately.
This year, interest in winter stays has been negatively influenced by the fact that there is still no snow in Krkonoše.
People are waiting for her to fall.
No one wants to spend the winter in the mountains without snow.
If the tracks overlap during the week, Christmas too will be full,” Schiefert said.
Conversely, the occupancy of Hotel Horizont in Pec pod Sněžkou is slightly greater than last year.
We've got an improvement of about five percent.
We still have a few last seats for the New Year as for Christmas.
“There’s a lot of interest during the January Polish holidays and February is selling well too,” said Karel Rada, director of the best hotel in Pec.
Hotel Omnia near Jánské Lázně's central car park is also eighty percent during the New Year.
On the contrary, half of the beds remain empty for Christmas in the most recent hotel in Jánské Lázně.
This year, we were pleasantly surprised by the month of January, where we already have sixty per cent occupancies at the moment.
February is a little weaker for the moment but as soon as the snow falls, interest in stays in the Krkonoše will certainly grow according to Hotel Omnia manager Erik Sporysch.
Krkonoše hoteliers, however, do not have to expect more snow.
Thursday it will be cooler and if there is precipitation, they should be snowy in the mountains.
But afterwards, the weather would have to heat up, informs Jiří Jakubský of the Hradec station of the Czech hydrometeorological institute.
United States: Repetition Is Mother of Wisdom
He even becomes a comic, the assiduous effort with which Barack Obama, president with the reputation of sage, reproduces his predecessor's strategy on the Afghan question, which he himself considered very simple.
When he ended up allowing his long-awaited doctrine to sweat, it turned out that he had copied it to carbon paper from Bush’s Iraqi scenario three years ago.
Not content to use in the text even several times the expression "as in Iraq", he did not hesitate, moreover, to use the title of Bush's speech of January 2007 as the title of his own discourse: "A new road forward"
At the same time, he personally criticized Bush’s idea of sending new reinforcements to an Iraq consumed by the civil war as “irresponsible and a catastrophic decision in its consequences.”
The following months, however, showed that Bush’s strategy, emanating from the recommendations of campaign commanders, was the only one possible and successful to such an extent that the newspapers currently do not talk about Iraq at all.
This did not improve Bush’s reputation, that said, objectively speaking, he left his successor with a much more favorable final situation than one could have hoped for.
Barack Obama hopes that in Afghanistan the miracle will happen again
It was once again the campaign commanders that demanded an increase in the military contingent, including the commander of the Afghan operation, General Stanley McCristal, who even harshly criticized the reluctances of the White House despite habits of subordination.
He demanded 40 000 men in order to make the situation more favorable.
The president hesitated for three months but without a better option, he at least defrauded the general of 10 000 soldiers.
He managed to extirpate the promise of an additional 5 000 from Europe, even though he originally counted on 10 000.
What brings the most trouble into Obama’s entire strategy seems to be his intention to withdraw the armies from Afghanistan within 18 months and to complete this entire process within three years. All of Obama’s doctrine raises more questions than it provides answers.
Many doubt that an increase of half will bring the same fruits as the progression in Iraq.
Despite the fratricidal wars of recent years, Iraqi society is relatively well-organized and its leaders are honoring agreements.
The Afghan situation stands out by the exact opposite - we cannot count on anyone and no treaty is respected.
In these conditions, it is difficult to prepare Afghan security forces, accustomed to changing leaders on a day-to-day basis depending on what suits them.
Political institutions, although officially founded, will remain only powder in the eyes behind which patriarchal ethnic relations will proliferate.
It is singularly complex to balance the interests of the various ethnic groups in view of the lack of clarity on the reports, which are still complicated by Pakistani and Iranian influences.
Even the foundation of a "prompt-to-act state", which is the basic goal of the operation, does not seem to be very real.
The time horizon of Obama’s strategy is perpelled by his voluntarism.
In the last eight years, the situation in Afghanistan has only worsened and it is currently on the verge of chaos.
Only a purely romantic and out-of-reality heart can be persuaded that the little soldiers sent in the tumult of the war in view of their "prompt return home" will bring there a real miracle.
From a political point of view, defining a date for the withdrawal of troops is purely irresponsibly since the Taliban will consider it the indirect admission of American defeat.
The latest NATO summit has shown that the willingness of Europeans to participate in military operations in Afghanistan is moving towards the negative.
European politicians don't know or want to explain to their voters how security in Germany or Italy is linked to a war in the Hindu Kush mountains.
Another factor capable of radically altering the situation is the evolution of events related to Iran.
If the situation continues to deteriorate and the reinforced sanctions have no effect on the Iranians, the Americans will find themselves faced with the very difficult question of resolving the problem through the use of military force.
We will have to answer it for example at a time when, according to Obama, America will come to the victorious withdrawal of troops from Afghanistan.
However, each such solution will lead to a dramatic reversal of the Afghan situation.
The only rustine for Obama is the fact that three years remain until the presidential elections and therefore there is still enough time for him to work on a new strategy, in case it turns out to be a total fiasco.
Goldman Sachs executives will no longer receive cash premiums
High-ranking executive employees of the U.S. Goldman Sach Bank will no longer receive cash bonuses this year.
The company has announced it to respond to strong criticism of its wage practices.
The group of 30 senior executives will receive shares instead of money, which they won’t be able to resell for five years.
Actions can be taken from leaders in case they take too many risks.
According to Reuters, Goldman Sachs is at the forefront of the effort to link retributions with long-term performances on Wall Street.
“I suppose Wall Street is well aware of the overall meaning to take,” said former investment banker Douglas Elliott of JPMorgan
There's the details, he added.
Goldman Sach Bank has become the target of criticism after setting itself aside almost US$17 billion for premiums in the first three quarters of this year.
For the current year, the total amount of premiums will likely exceed US$20 billion despite the measure announced today, according to Reuters.
With the arrival of the financial crisis, the strong premiums of the banking sector have become a sensitive political issue in many countries.
Britain announced on Wednesday that it plans to allocate a single fifty-percent tax on banker bonuses exceeding 25,000 pounds.
France is preparing similar measures.
This year the women won Nobel Prizes in all fields except physics
These are five women, a record number, who received today in Stockholm at the hands of King of Sweden Charles XVI Gustav the Nobel Prizes in technical disciplines and literature.
In addition to the four scientists there was also the German writer of Romanian origin Herta Müller.
Two American biologists, Elizabeth Blackburn and Carol Greider and their compatriot Jack Szostak received the Nobel Prize in Medicine for their research in the field of chromosomes.
The chemistry prize was awarded to the Israelian Ada Jonath with the Americans Venkatraman Ramakrishnan and Thomas Steitz for having clarified the structure and function of ribosomes
The last laureate received the Nobel Prize in Economics, the American Elinor Ostrom with his compatriot Oliver Williamson, for their analysis of economic governance.
The only technical discipline that didn't win this year was physics.
This award was presented today to scientists Charles Kao for his research in the field of fiber optics and George Smith with Willard Boyle for their invention of CCD chips, which are currently the basis of all digital cameras, fax machines or astronomical glasses.
Each laureate received the diploma, the Nobel Medal and the certificate of acquisition of the monetary award.
It reaches ten million Swedish kroron in each category. (approximately 1.1 million euros)
If one of them has several winners, they will share the reward with each other.
The highly anticipated Nobel Peace Prize has already been awarded to US President Barack Obama this afternoon in Oslo, Norway.
The latter, in his speech, acknowledged the controversial nature of his reward since he is only at the beginning of his journey and he is also at the head of a country engaged in two wars - in Iraq and Afghanistan.
To the defense of his policy he added that in certain situations war is essential to achieve peace, even if the price is high.
Minister Janota plans to resign. Klaus invited him to the Castle
President Václav Klaus will receive Finance Minister Eduard Janota on Friday morning, who ponders his stay in the government because of his disagreement with the state budget for next year as voted by the Chamber of Deputies
It is estimated that the budget and perhaps also Janota's position in the cabinet will be on the agenda of their Friday appointment.
Klaus also criticises the budget.
According to him, the deficit is too great, which further deepens the public finance crisis.
Presidential spokesman Radim Ochvat briefed on the meeting.
Janota then wants to discuss his position on Monday with Prime Minister Jan Fischer.
According to the legislation on the budget that will be subject to the signing of Klaus, it is a deficit of 163 billion kroron that the Czech Republic should manage.
Nevertheless, the Chamber of Deputies on Wednesday approved the proposals of the left regarding the transfers of money and increased the remuneration of civil servants, the functioning of social services and direct payments to farmers.
The government criticised the proposals of the Social Democrats that they would indirectly increase the budget deficit.
For this reason, a violent disagreement arose between the left on the one hand, the government, Klaus and the right on the other hand, which calls into question the future of Fischer’s cabinet which had been formed by agreement between the ODS, the ČSSD and the Green Party.
Critics say that the budget, as voted by MPs, removes the package of savings measures passed by Fischer's government, which he said must stem the state's indebtedness.
Klaus, who can ratify the state budget law but doesn't compel, said today at a breakfast of discussions with entrepreneurs that politicians have long neglected the "serious problem" of the "irrefrenant lack" of public finances.
According to him, there is no favourable solution.
Only a strong government could make an improvement, if it could build on a broader consensus of political parties, according to the president.
ODS President Mirek Topolánek has indicated that the budget is appalling and that the government should question its subsequent existence.
ODS Vice-President Petr Nečas told ČTK that the idea of an administrative firm supported by ČSSD, the ODS and the Greens clearly no longer works.
In his reaction, Prime Minister Fischer called such words "drastic verdicts."
ČSSD leader Jiří Paroubek reacted to Topolánek, saying that the budget deficit prepared for this year by the former Topolánek government is appalling and will exceed 200 billion crowns.
ČSSD already on Wednesday described the ratification of the state’s budget for the coming year as successful.
The People's Party was also satisfied.
Morgan Freeman will star Mandela in Clint Eastwood’s new film
Despite criticism of the fact that the main roles are played by U.S. actors, South Africans claim that the new Hollywood film Invictus - Undefeated will expose to the world many things about the country, its fights and victories.
History is about sport, relationships between races and Nelson Mandela.
Morgan Freeman, an Oscar-winning American actor, plays the film about the life of Nelson Mandela, a tireless fight against the racist apartheid regime in South Africa and the first black president.
Freeman said, that it was a ninety-old Mandela who asked him to embody him in the film.
Matt Damon plays another important role.
I told him, "If I'm supposed to play you, I should be able to see you personally.
The seventy-two-year-old actor who was awarded an award for his role in Clint Eastwood’s Milion Dollar Baby drama in the middle of boxing has, for example, embodied the leader of a movement against slavagism that has become himself a slave, a fictitious U.S. president, or even God in the film Evan Almighty but never anyone still alive
The former South African statesman has been jailed 27 years for his hard work in supporting the end of apartheid in South Africa.
He was released in 1990 and became the country’s president for four years.
In 1993 he was awarded the Nobel Peace Prize.
Invictus is an undefeated Latin word and is the title of a poem by the English author William Ernest Henley published in 1875.
The challenge was to talk like him.
The film tells the true story of the joint efforts of Nelson Mandela and the captain of the South African rugby team François to unify the country. The newly elected President Mandela knows that his country still remains and still divided racially and economically after apartheid.
He is persuaded to be able to unite his people through sport and decides to unite the rugby players who are among the outsiders.
The South African team will eventually advance to the World Cup in 1995.
Freeman worked for a few years to bring Mandela’s story to the big screen.
“I didn’t have any other purpose except to play that role in a way that brought him closest to reality,” Freeman said.
The biggest challenge for me, of course, was to talk like him
The actor said that if he and the international politician were in the same region, he would have tried to meet Mandela, to dine with him, to be behind the scenes before his speech.
The most important thing for him was to shake his hand.
I realized that when I shake your hand, I recover from your energy, it is transmitted and I had the impression of knowing how you feel, he said.
It's important for me to try to become someone else.
Matt Damon stars in the film François Pienaar, the captain of a national rugby team in which there are a majority of white players.
The actor said he had six months to prepare for a role in the tough rugby world.
For him, his first meeting with Pienaar at his home was a big surprise.
I remember when I knocked on the door of the house, he opened me and the first thing I said to Francois Pienaar was, “I look much bigger in the movie.”
Despite the differences between actors and real models, Invictus under the leadership of director Eastwood has received positive reviews and we are even talking about ambitions for the Oscars.
Film critic Todd McCarthy of the Daily Variety newspaper summarized his appreciation in these words: “It’s a very good story, a very well-crafted film.”
In the film evaluations on rottentomatoes.com, Invictus received 76 percent positive reviews.
The mystery of the aura in Norway is solved: the Russians tested a rocket
We could observe a strange luminous phenomenon of unknown origin above Norway.
The Russian Defense Ministry only admitted yesterday that it tested an intercontinental ballistic rocket not far from the Norwegian borders.
The launch, however, was again a failure and the "Bulava" missile which was originally intended to be the pride of the Russian military gradually became the nightmare of Russian generals and Prime Minister Vladimir Putin.
Its repeated presence during rocket tests did not have the desired effect on the success of the test flights.
Most of the time, either Bulava didn’t fly, or she damaged in mid-air.
The Russian newspapers speak of "Bulava" as "missile that does not fly"
A missile that doesn't fly but shines
This 13th trial took place at the beginning normally.
It's only at the end of the theft that there's damage to the equipment again.
It is obviously the engine of the third stage of the rocket that exploded.
This time, "Bulava" flew from the nuclear submarine Dmitrij Donskij, which was navigating under the surface of the White Sea.
This type of missile can be set on fire from a moving and submerged boat
This is the reason why the mysterious light above Norway caused by an unidentified flying object was presumably caused by a faulty "Bulava".
Moreover, the Norwegians did not doubt for a moment that it is a Russian rocket.
The generals argue that this time we can't talk absolutely about failure.
The first two stages of the rocket resisted and the damage only hit the third.
In the preceding cases, the motors experienced failures on the first stage.
Even though none of the test launches went smoothly, the Defense Ministry management considers that only six out of thirteen have been failures.
This success rate of almost fifty percent therefore fills the generals with optimism and they therefore still claim that one day "Bulava" will fly without problems and that it will take even ten hypersonic nuclear nucles with a total weight of 1.15 tons.
The legendary Fetissov has signed a one-year contract with CSKA Moscow at the age of 51
Legendary defender Viacheslav Fetissov will once again present himself in a professional match at 51.
Former world champion, Olympic champion and Stanley Cup holder will lend his CSKA Moscow team in need and will probably make his entry already during Friday’s game against Petrograd.
Fetissov, who ended his career at the age of forty in 1998, is currently the president of CSKA.
After Denis Kuliash's injury we urgently need another defender.
Fetissov trains regularly and agrees to help the team.
We still have to settle a few details on the form, says the coach of the club Sergey Nyemchinov, quoted by the AP agency.
It has not been clarified if the comeback of the legendary hockey player will concern only one game.
“I think Fetissov is one of those who are able to strengthen the mind of the other players,” Nyemchinov said.
According to AP, in the case of his return to the ice, Fetissov becomes the oldest Russian professional hockey player.
In the NHL, legendary forward Gordie Howe played for Hartford for a whole season and had 15 goals and 26 assists.
The oldest of the famous quintet Fetissov, Kassatonov - Makarov, Larionov, Krutov is one of the best hockey players and among those with the most success.
Under the colors of the Soviet Union he won gold twice at the Olympic Games, seven world championship titles, he triumphed at the Canada Cup as well as at the World Junior Championship.
In the second part of his career, he also received tributes in NHL where twice he held the Stanley Cup.
He has been a member of the Hockey Hall of Fame since 2001.
Hyundai listened to unions and cancelled all overtime
The management of Nošovice's Hyundai plant has agreed with the unions to cancel all overtime.
It is precisely because of the frequent overtime that the unions declared a state of emergency strike Monday.
Spokesman Petr Vaněk of the Hyundai plant confirmed this.
However, the management wants to further agree with the unions on a Saturday worked again this year.
We can’t give up our production plan, it’s essential, that’s why voluntary overtime was offered on Saturday, December 19 knowing that there would be two teams, Vaněk said.
Sedmička.cz indicated that the company has offered a bonus 400 Czech crowns to employees who will be part of Saturday's team.
It will also be awarded a posteriori for the day worked on 28 November.
The company, according to Vaněk, has decided to change the way of motivating its employees in exchange for overtime.
Either they receive the supplement for the extra hours, or they can lay a replacement leave on December 28 and thus extend their year-end holidays told Vaněk.
In addition to this, all employees present on Saturday will receive compensation for transport costs and for their lunch.
Given the fact that overtime was the most urgent and hottest point in the comments and demands of the unions as well as employees who spontaneously ceased production last week, the management took the immediate decision to cancel all overtime for December, Vaněk said.
Until last week, employees of the car factory were forced to work almost an additional two hours daily in addition to their eight hours.
The unions intend to cancel the state of the strike after signing the agreement.
The next negotiation between the management of the car factory and the unions is scheduled to take place on Friday afternoon.
Trade union leader Petr Kuchař said on Wednesday that they were ready to cease the strike in the event of an agreement on their demands and the signing of a joint document.
The situation in the plant culminated last Wednesday when nearly 400 employees spontaneously went on strike due to relentless overtime.
They were also protesting against poor wage conditions and so-called hazing.
The management defended itself with the fact that employees had to work extra to meet the imperatives in terms of car production.
During the announcement of the strike on Monday, the unions also asked that employees who stopped production should not be subject to any sanctions.
Unions are calling on society to reduce overtime to a minimum.
In addition, they want this year a retribution of 5 000 krorons (209 € approximately) for them.
Nošovice's Hyundai car factory currently employs 2 000 people.
It started mass production last year in November.
It manufactured about 80,000 vehicles until September of this year, having a production capacity of 200,000 cars per year.
Czech discovery : a substance that works on "enduring" HIV viruses too
A team of Czech and German scientists is testing new combinations that can slow the proliferation of the HIV virus in the body.
The main advantage of the improved substance is that it also acts on viruses that have already developed immunity to drugs.
This reality takes, in some cases, the top of the fact that the combination does not act as intensely on the common variants of the virus as well as some drugs already available.
It is not possible to cure AIDS at this time.
However, we can radically prolong the lives of patients through a mixture of drugs that prevent the multiplication of the HIV virus in the body.
However, their consumption leads to a series of side effects.
In addition, the repression of this multiplication has the effect the development of so-called resistant viruses on which drugs no longer have any effect.
The work of experts from three institutions, the Academy of Sciences of the Czech Republic, the VŠCHT in Prague and the University of Heidelberg paves the way for a way to cope with the resistance of the virus.
They have shown that substances called metalocarboranes act on the protein responsible for multiplying the HIV virus.
Metalocarboranes are an assembly of boron, hydrogen, carbon and cobalt.
This combination blocks the development of the virus in a different way from all the drugs used so far and that is why they could overcome the problem of resistance.
The work of the researchers leads them to discover "improved" new substances, on the basis of their knowledge of the molecular mechanics of their bindings on the proteins of the virus.
Metalocarborans have a single three-dimensional structure: two multi-walled cages are formed by boron, hydrogen and carbon atoms and connected by a metallic atom, in this case cobalt.
A treacherous and resistant virus.
HIV protease is an HIV protein that is essential to the life cycle of the virus.
Without the division of the HIV virus caused by HIV protease there would be no mature infectious particles of the virus.
If the HIV protease is discontinued, the spread of the virus in the patient's body will also be stopped, the researchers said in a press release issued by the Institute of Organic Chemistry and Biochemistry of the Academy of Sciences of the Czech Republic.
In their work published in the scientific journal Journal of Medicinal Chemistry, scientists describe a series of combinations where two pairs of cages (see table) are linked by a short organic link, which is then systematically modified.
A little weaker but more reliable.
The effectiveness of this series of substances on HIV proteases has been tested in test tubes as well as on its resistant variants, obtained from HIV-contaminated patients.
On the current version of the enzyme, the effect of metalocarboranes is not as strong as on clinical drugs, however it does not falter on resistant versions against which common drugs are often ineffective.
Metalocarboranes, with their unique mechanism of action and other properties such as their biological and chemical stability, their low toxicity and the possibility of other chemical modifications are therefore interesting combinations to be examined to move towards the development of effective HIV drugs, says Pavlína Řezáčová, director of the structural biology laboratory of the Institute of Organic Chemistry and Biochemistry
Argument around the budget. The ODS attacks Fischer, who defends himself
ODS (Civic Democratic Party), Mirek Topolánek, described as "scary" the budget ratified for the following year and according to him, the government of Prime Minister Jan Fischer should question its existence.
The Prime Minister called the ODS remarks a drastic verdict.
He says he's taking some time to analyze.
ODS spokesman Petr Nečas said the idea of an administrative cabinet backed by ČSSD, the ODS and the Green Party clearly no longer worked.
According to Nečas, the head of government should decide whether he will rely in the Chamber of Deputies on the minority who are concerned to fight the growing deficit, or whether he is concerned to remain in office with the help of the parliamentary left.
Nečas warned that the positions of the ODS and the social democrats in relation to the problem of public deficits are diametrically opposed.
The Chamber of Deputies ratified Wednesday the budget for the year 2010, with, however, the proposals for amendments up to 12 billion kronor included by the left.
The Chamber has increased money for civil servants' salaries, the operation of social services and direct payments to farmers.
The government's objection to the proposals of the Social Democrats was the fact that they indirectly increased the budget deficit.
The Czech Republic is expected to manage a deficit of 163 billion kroron.
The ODS left the room ahead of the vote, obeying the government's wish that an amended budget is a less bad variant than an acting budget.
Disappointment and uselessness
Finance Minister Eduard Janota spoke of disappointment and a sense of uselessness, Prime Minister Jan Fischer indicated that the government will still reflect on the current situation.
Nečas is convinced that if Janota threatened to leave his duties in the event of ratification of the proposed amendments, the situation might be different.
According to Czech Television, Janota made an appointment Monday with the Prime Minister after his visit to the government:
At the same time, he indicated that he was wondering if he would remain in office.
His disappointment with the negotiations around the budget is the reason for this.
If Fischer decides to continue to fight the budget deficit, Nečas believes that ČSSD's representatives, who imposed the changes in the budget, have no place in the government.
The Prime Minister should also make it clear that he will resign if the House ratifies other deficit-increasing laws, such as those affecting health insurance, pensions, or the reform of the services law.
Topolánek: the ministers appointed by the ODS are not involved.
This is the business of Prime Minister Fischer and the entire government.
Ministers appointed by the ODS have done nothing harmful, Topolánek said in a textual statement from the United States.
He recalled that the deputy ministers of some ministries call for TOP 09, which voted against the budget.
Nečas pointed out that if Fischer decides to resign in the face of leading a fight against the amount of the fiscal deficit, "it is quite logical and correct that it is based on the left-wing majority which is currently in the House."
That said, he should not expect the support and tolerance of the Democrats.
“I haven’t thought about it and once it’s on the agenda so I’ll dedicate myself to it,” Defense Minister Martin Barták, elected to the government by the democrats from the House.
Prime Minister Fischer flew in the afternoon for a European Council debate in Brussels.
The SDO backtracked at the request of the Prime Minister.
Nečas stressed at the same time that the SDO would probably have voted on Wednesday against the amended budget if it had not expressly asked the Democratic deputies to allow the vote on the budget.
We don't have the feeling of being orphans, we're not small children," he said, referring to the question of whether the ODS hadn't been blamed by the Party president in the House or in the country when negotiating the budget.
I did not notice that my vote would have failed.
I already knew at the moment when I renounced my mandate that what would follow in the following months would be a Bolshevization of the House and a destruction of everything that was positive, reacted Topolánek in person.
According to ČD's new schedule there will be less trains for identical fares.
The Czech Railways will remove or limit from 13 December certain less loaded express or regional trains and, on the contrary, will strengthen some others.
Total transport coverage will decrease on the day of entry into force of the new schedules by two percent compared to today.
Most rates won’t change, including base rates and reduced rates.
For example, the inhabitants of the Ostrava region will have more connections to the capital, the Railways will add a pendolino train.
On the contrary, an express will stop serving Bratislava.
There will also be less fast trains on the Prague - Písek - České Budějovice route, the Prague - Letohrad direct line will be radically reduced.
At the regional level, the biggest decrease in transport concerns the Hradec Králové region by eight percent in total, on the other hand Prague and its surroundings can welcome an increase.
Two night trains on the Prague - Tábor - České Budějovice route are also canceled and the circulation of a few express trains on the Prague-Písek - České Budějovice route is reduced to just a few days in the week.
Among the biggest transformations, we can see the introduction of a new direct line from Milovice to Prague: for the moment the inhabitants of Milovice have to change in Lysá nad Labem.
This is the fifteenth line of the Esko suburban system, the opening of which is possible thanks to the completion of the electrification of the line between Lysá nad Labem and Milovice.
Lines to the capital will have their final destination at Prague’s main train station (Praha Hlavní nádraží).
The ČD will introduce more SC Pendolino lines between Prague and Ostrava and trains to Šumperk/Jeseník will depart in intervals of two hours.
Guests will no longer be able to take the Pendolino to Bratislava from December 13.
The invoice this year for the regional railways: 200 million more crowns.
Express trains are ordered and paid by the Ministry of Transport and will pay the Railways next year in a total of four billion crowns to settle the visible losses, the same amount as this year. The regional trains are ordered by the regions which will pay for them by a total of eight billion next year, knowing that the state will participate to the tune of almost three billion.
Regional trains this year are 200 million more expensive.
The railways have newly signed a contract for ten years, so far the duration was still one year.
The Railways appreciates the system, which also explains why they did not resort to a price increase in the vast majority of transport tickets.
Changes will only affect ČD Net one-day tickets and eLiška discounted internet tickets.
For travelers with no discount cards, the price of ČD Net will increase from 450 to 600 krones.
The price of eLiška will newly depend on the route travelled, while until then it was valid between cities in a region of your choice and was at a single fare of 160 krona.
Filipino rebels keep more than 50 people, children have been released
Rebels today abducted 75 people in the southern Philippines, including a few primary school students and their teachers.
It was the AFP agency that informed, but which originally spoke of only 65 hostages.
All 17 children were released after eight hours, as well as one of the teachers.
The hostage-taking took place in Agusan del Sur province.
It is reported that about 19 rebels employed the hostages as human shields during a flight from the police.
Local negotiators are now trying to reach an agreement for the release of the remaining hostages.
According to the AFP agency, the rebels belong to the New People's Army (NPA), which is the armed wing of the Communist Party of the Philippines (CPP).
According to the AFP news agency, the police first prosecuted the captors following violence caused during a settling of scores between two family clans.
Martial law has been declared in Maguindanao province in the southern Philippines because of a recent massacre in which 57 people were killed.
Among the victims of the November 23 massacre linked to the elections of the governor's administration there were 30 journalists.
The governor of the southern Philippine province has been arrested on suspicion of involvement in the massacre, as well as his father Andal Ampatuan, Patriarch of the Influencing Ampatuan Lineage.
Mindanao Island also includes active Muslim separatists.
However, they renewed peace talks with the Philippine government on Tuesday.
South Africa's history affects everyone, according to Morgan Freeman alias Mandela
Clint Eastwood’s new Hollywood film arrives in U.S. theaters in the coming days, the title is Invictus (the Czech official title is Invictus: Neporažený), and narrates part of the life of former South African President Nelson Mandela.
The role of the world-renowned fighter against apartheid returned to Morgan Freeman, who was chosen years ago by Mandela himself to embody him.
The men’s supporting role for the legendary South African rugby champion has been awarded to Matt Damon.
With the arrival of the film in theaters, the feedback from critics and the public is heard.
Nelson Mandela
Nelson Rolihlahla Mandela was born on 18 June 1918 in Mvezu, South Africa.
In 1988 he won the Sakharov Prize for His Freedom of Wit, in 1993 he was awarded the Nobel Peace Prize alongside Klerk.
He served as President of South Africa from 1994 to 1999.
The film contains great expectations since it is presented at a time when on the one hand South Africa is in full preparations for the Football World Cup, and on the other hand because the media are agitated by disturbing news about the increase in racial conflicts and discrimination of the inhabitants on the part of the black majority in power.
According to the AP agency, the reactions to the new film are currently rather positive in South Africa, despite the frequent reservations expressed towards the main roles when "strike" Hollywood stars.
Morgan Freeman is nevertheless, repeating his own words, proud of the film.
I've dreamed about this role for many years and I've been well prepared.
“I’ve also met Nelson Mandela personally many times to be able – as they say – to recover from his energy, he said at the film’s official premiere in Los Angeles.
I think it’s a good thing if you have the opportunity to remember the personality and message of Mandela via the film.
This is the history of the Republic of South Africa, but it concerns every individual added.
Wearing the National Rugby Team
The film based on the history of the novel of the British writer John Carlin being freely translated. In the role of the enemy: Nelson Mandela and the play that gave its creation to the people depicts the South African defender of human rights during his tenure in the presidency.
Morgan Freeman tries during the film to reglue two racial groups of a long divided population as well as Nelson Mandela in 1995 he entered in the national jersey of the South African team on the lawn of the World Cup, which South Africa had then won.
For having shown himself on the lawn in a jersey, which is considered in South Africa as something sacred, Mandela had been long ovated fourteen years ago mainly by the white public and was recognized by white populations.
Let’s hope that the football championship will succeed in uniting people again into one nation, said rugby player Chester Williams, who in 1995 was the only “black” of the national team.
It remains to be seen whether the tense South African situation will improve.
South Africa’s whites, however, are skeptical as recently said in an interview with LN Christopher Dawson, a rugby fan who fled to Britain.
The championship starts on June 11, 2010.
A transformer worth tens of millions of burning crowns in the Louny region
A fire engulfed in the morning a transformer of the Čeps company of the distribution center near the municipality of Výškov na Lounsku.
According to Lukáš Marvan, spokesman for Ústí firefighters, there are six units operating on the fire.
They originally intended to allow the transformer to consume themselves, but they reviewed their opinion and extinguished the fire with a mixture of water, moss and a special substance.
The cause is a technical defect, an internal short-circuit of the transformer.
No one has been injured and, according to company spokeswoman Pavla Mandátova, the incident does not endanger the safe operation of the transmission assembly.
The damage is estimated at a few tens of millions of crowns, the exact figure can be known once the fire is extinguished.
According to Mandátova, the destroyed transformer will have to be replaced by a new one.
She adds that in ten years of activity, Čeps has not experienced anything similar and that incidents of this type are isolated.
Electric power supplies have not been halted, says ČEZ spokeswoman for North Bohemia Soňa Holingerova Hendrychova, the operation of homes, administrations and industries, was not influenced by the fire of the processing station.
A.s. Čeps is the exclusive operator in the Czech territory of 400 kV and 220 kV transmission assemblies and power lines.
It maintains, renovates and develops 39 distribution stations with 67 transformers transferring electrical energy from the transmission assemblies to the distribution assemblies and line layouts with a length of 4339 kilometers.
Jančura to file defamation complaint against three prefects of ČSSD
Radim Jančura, owner of the Student Agency, will today file a defamation complaint against three democratic social prefects.
And concretely against Michal Hašek of the Southern Moravia region, against Radko Martínka of the Pardubice region and against the prefect of the Olomouc Martin Tesařík region.
Jančura indicated it in Radiožurnál's Dvacet minut.
The complaint relates to the company's attempt to operate regional railways in certain regions.
However, all regions ended up signing long-term contracts with the Czech Railways.
All three attacked us and say that we make the thin mouths, while we only made an offer for what they wanted themselves.
They claim that we are more expensive than the Czech Railways which is not true because we know the prices of the Czech Railways that were signed last week.
All we have to do is defend ourselves.
This is in exchange for slanders on Student Agency, Jančura told Radiožurnál.
Similarly, he indicated that he had evidence that no region has proceeded in accordance with European law.
For this, we are preparing a complaint for the European Commission.
Hašek considers that Mr. Jančura’s filing of a complaint represents the continuation of his media show, which serves him as free publicity for his company and his person.
Of course, I am ready to defend my name and the name of my region and I call on the other prefects to do the same.
I suppose this will be an opportunity to make it clear that it is precisely Mr Jančura who manipulates public opinion and resorts to false and false arguments about regional rail transport, Hašek said
Jančura has already informed last week that he will invest a few million crowns in an advertisement that will mention the unfavourable steps of the regions.
He told Radiožurn�ál that he will interrupt the campaign after Christmas and that he intends to take it back after the New Year.
He clarified that the campaign will target corruption in the broad sense.
Jančura began its campaign after the regions agreed to entrust the Czech Railways with the operation of railway links for an order of about 150 billion kroons, without bidding.
According to Jančura, such a way of acting is illegal and contrary to the position of the European Court of Justice.
According to him, his criticism of rail transport in the regions has even brought to Jančura threats from an ČSSD deputy.
The only threat was that if we didn't stop, we could lose our bus licenses.
He introduced himself as Member of Parliament for ČSSD, I don't know who it's about, but I won't say his name," Jančura told Radiožurn�ál today.
Vaccination against pneumococci will be free and optional
Vaccination against pneumococci will reverse old habits as early as January.
She will be the first to be free and at the same time optional for families.
There are already voices, saying that compulsory vaccination would be more effective.
Preparations for the introduction of free pneumococcal vaccination culminate.
The pharmaceutical institute already defines for vaccines the maximum prices that health insurance will pay.
Specialists also see clarity in the vaccination process.
Another vaccination.
Free vaccines for children in August.
Since January, children born last year from August 2 can be vaccinated free of charge against pneumococci.
The condition is that they have not yet been vaccinated.
If we started vaccination last year or earlier, we will have to pay the following vaccinations yourself.
The first dose of vaccine should be administered between the 3rd and 5th month of the child.
Last year already a quarter of the families voluntarily vaccinated their children against pneumococci.
However, the parents paid 1600 krones or more for a dose.
Vaccination of the youngest requires four doses.
Specialists estimate that health insurance spends 300 to 450 000 krona in doses of vaccine each year.
However, they save on medical care that unvaccinated children would require.
Until now, the State spent about 500 000 krorons per year on all the vaccination of children.
The reimbursed vaccination was introduced with the "Janota package".
Pneumococci are the cause of inflammations of the middle ear but also serious infections including inflammation of the meninges and blood poisoning.
According to hospital investigations, there are up to 28 deaths of children under the age of 10 caused by pneumococcal infections.
Apart from the Czech Republic, about 40 other countries have introduced reimbursed vaccination.
Will vaccination be mandatory?
There are opinions about a possible immunization obligation in the future.
Starting in January, it must be regulated by insurance and optional.
For example the president of the Nahlas Rudolf Kalovský association indicated that compulsory vaccination would be more positive.
His association has long promoted vaccination against pneumococci.
At an international conference in which our association participated, the specialists were not really satisfied with the fact that we have an optional vaccination.
This isn't ideal.
On the other hand, we are happy to have been able to impose at least this.
I think this is a direction that must now be followed, said Kalovský.
However, the Ministry of Health does not want to change the immunization process against pneumococci.
We absolutely do not envisage it at the moment, his spokesman Vlastimil Sršeň said.
Optional vaccination has its disadvantages, say doctors.
Hana Cabrnochová, president of the specialized Society of Pediatricians, acknowledges that a vaccination based on volunteerism will have its shortcomings.
If all the children were vaccinated, we could consider a decrease in the renewals of vaccinations "In the situation where the vaccine costs such a sum I understand the decision making the vaccination optional," says Cabrnochová however.
Specialists predict that as early as next year, more than 80 percent of new parents will vaccinate their infants.
Vaccination against pneumococcal disease also marks a rupture of the fact that it is the first vaccination of children that is financed by insurance and not the state.
Cabrnochová explains that insurance should free up funds for prevention, saving on possible future medical care.
Doctors will have to purchase their products themselves
This, however, is related to the concerns of pediatrician practitioners, which concern the purchase of vaccines.
The original intention was to have the vaccines purchased by insurance, with doctors receiving them in the same way as other state-provided vaccine products.
However, some MPs feared that this would allow insurance to "deduce the sums of health insurance".
Thus, doctors will have to buy the products and wait for the insurance companies to reimburse them.
Pediatricians are estimated to have to invest nearly 40 000 krorons per month in vaccines, which is a significant amount.
It'll be far from easy, according to Cabrnochová.
The son of an American artist involved in a "Museume Theft"
Frank Frazetta’s son, a well-known illustrator, has been accused of trying to fly paintings worth US$20 million (GBP$12 million) in his father’s museum.
In Pennsylvania, police said Alfonso Frank Frazetta had been arrested as he loaded 90 paintings into his vehicle and trailer.
According to her, Mr. Frazetta and another man used a mechanical shovel to force the entrance to the museum in the Pocono Mountains area.
Mr. Frazetta Senior, 81, is famous for his drawings of characters such as Conan the Barbarian and Tarzan.
At the time of the incident, the artist was in Florida, the Associated Press (AP) news agency reported.
AP cited a police official that the young Mr. Frazetta may have been motivated by a family feud.
According to a policing statement, the 52-year-old Frazetta Jr. claims to have been allowed by his father to "enter the museum, by any means, to transport the paintings to a storage facility," the agency reported.
Mr. Frazetta Senior denies having given such authorization, the agency said.
Labour defends the increase of the budgetary tax.
The ministers defended the increase in the tax and the reductions in spending announced in the pre-Budget report, despite criticism from the opposition, companies and trade unions.
The Tories claimed that Alistair Darling "missed" an opportunity to show that he was serious about reducing the deficit by postponing his decisions until after the elections.
The Chancellor has also attracted lightness by attacking low wages and average wages.
Among its main proposals, we can see an increase of 0.5% in Social Insurance and a ceiling rate of 1% on public wages from 2011.
Anger of Social Insurance
Unions have protested that low wages are penalized by a recession they are not responsible for and have warned that "problems" are announced.
The decline in Social Insurance – which will make it possible to obtain about 3 billion GBP per year – has angered the world of companies, because according to him, it is a tax on jobs while the goal should be a revival of the economy.
The increase, reserved for those earning more than 20,000 GBP per year, will affect about 10 million workers.
MAIN POINTS OF THE PRE-BUDGET REPORT
Social Insurance will increase further by 0.5% from April 2011
The economy will fall even more than the 4.75% expected this year
New 50% tax on banker bonuses
The increase in corporate income tax for small companies has been abandoned
Rebates for electric cars and wind turbines
Retirement increase of 2.5%
According to Treasury estimates, one person earning 30,000 GBP will lose 90 GBP per year and a person earning 40,000 GBP will lose 190 GBP, while one person earning 10,000 GBP per year will earn an additional GBP.
The ministers said their goal of halving the deficit before 2013 led to "difficult decisions" but insisted that 60% of the burden of additional taxes would weigh on the top 5% of employees.
Stephen Timms, the Secretary of the Treasury, refuted an increase in the tax and pointed out that a decrease in spending was a "drop of water in the ocean" compared to what was needed.
"These figures are important. They will allow us to halve the deficit, which will be absolutely essential over the next four years," he told the BBC.
Timms said he was confident that the growth of the economy would restart at the end of the year.
However, Labour's forecasts for economic growth - which will influence the amount it will have to borrow and how quickly the deficit will be met - have been questioned after the Chancellor has been forced to revise his first figures.
In his speech, he said that the economy would likely fall by 4.75% this year – much worse than the 3.5% decline predicted in April - while borrowing would be 3 billion GDP higher than the previous estimate.
"Missed Opportunity"
The Conservatives who will launch a new advertising campaign on Thursday warning of Labour's "debt crisis", said the plan to borrow 789 billion GBP over the next six years was unsustainable.
The ministers have failed to present a credible plan on how to repay, they added, "cynically" evading difficult choices until after the elections - to be held in June 2010.
“They didn’t present anything significant about what they were going to do,” said Philip Hammond, Chief Treasury Secretary of the Shadow Cabinet. “They had the opportunity to do so and they missed it.”
"The important announcements will be made after the elections, regardless of the winner"
Roger Bootle, Deloitte
Labour insists that schools, hospitals and the police will be protected from future reductions, contrary to Tory’s proposals that would lead to a faster and larger decrease in the deficit.
But the Lib Dems claimed that the money raised as a result of the increase in taxes and lower spending would be used to support next year’s spending and not reduce the deficit, arguing that the plans were “constructed on the wind.”
Nick Robinson, the BBC’s political editor, claimed that the schedule of announcements – such as rising inflation for some disability benefits next April, mentioned above – would leave the chancellor in the face of accusations of propaganda, with the money simply recovering the following year.
However, Labour insists that additional help is provided while people need it most and that the situation will be reviewed in September 2010.
“False budget”
While ministers didn’t release spending details beyond 2011, economists said it was clear that public spending restrictions would only begin to be felt in the medium term.
The well-respected Institute for Fiscal Studies stated that the Chancellor’s figures involve, in the future, substantial reductions in many areas, including potentially transportation, higher education, science and defense.
"The pain is actually only postponed," said its Director, Robert Chote.
“The most important announcements will be made after the elections, regardless of the winner,” said Roger Bootle, Deloitte’s Accountant Economic Advisor.
"This has been a fake pre-budget report. Markets realize this."
FBI Investigates "Arrests of Americans in Pakistan"
The FBI is investigating the arrest in Pakistan of five U.S. reporters suspected of extremist links.
The men were arrested during a raid at a house in Sarghoda in eastern Punjab province, the U.S. embassy in Pakistan told the BBC.
The FBI said it was investigating whether it was the same men who had been declared missing from their home in the U.S. state of Virginia last month.
The U.S. Department of Foreign Affairs said it was also looking for information about these people.
Three of them are portrayed as being of Pakistani descent, one of Egyptian heritage and the other of Yemeni origin.
“If they’re U.S. citizens, of course, we’d like to know what the accusations against them are and under what conditions they’re being held,” spokesman Ian Kelly said.
Katherine Schweit, an FBI spokeswoman, claimed that the agency became aware of the arrests and was in contact with the families of the missing students.
“We are working with the Pakistani authorities to determine their identity and the nature of their activities there, and whether it’s really the students who are missing,” she said.
The Pakistani embassy in Washington claimed that the men had been arrested in a house belonging to the uncle of one of them.
She said that the house was already being guarded by the local police and that no charges had yet been laid against the arrested men.
The Secretary of State Hillary Clinton declined to comment on the arrests, the Reuters news agency reported, but she said the U.S. needs to “collaborate further with Afghanistan and Pakistan in an attempt to uproot the infrastructure of terrorism that continues to recruit and train people.”
The five students had been reported missing from their homes by their families in Northern Virginia in late November.
Amnesty Condemns Iranian 'Abuses'
Over the past twenty years, human rights have been as unrespected as ever, according to the Amnesty International report.
The report presents "repetitions of violations" by the regime, before and after the campaign of the June presidential elections.
A man quoted in the report claimed that he was beaten and burned with cigarettes. Another said he had been threatened with rape.
Iran has denied previous criticisms of its non-compliance with human rights.
The authorities have stated that such criticism is politically motivated.
Thousands of people have been arrested and dozens have been killed in Iran, after President Mahmoud Ahmadinejad’s contested election led to the biggest public demonstrations since the 1979 Islamic Revolution.
Dozens have been sentenced to prison terms and prosecutors claim that at least five people have been sentenced to death.
Jon Leyne, the BBC correspondent in Tehran, now in London, says that at the beginning of the protests, Supreme Leader Ayatollah Ali Khamenei has acknowledged some of the allegations of abuse, ordering the closure of the Kahrizak Detention Center.
But since then, the authorities have not tolerated any criticism, says our correspondent.
“False Confession”
Amnesty International has reported the case of a 26-year-old computer student, Ebrahim Mehtari, who claimed to have been accused of “working with Facebook networks” and protesting the election results.
"They often hit me in the face," he was quoted as saying.
"I was burned with cigarettes, under the eyes, on the neck, on the head... They threatened to execute me and humiliated me."
After five days, he signed a false confession and he was released and left in the street, still bloody and half-conscious, Amnesty said.
In August, presidential candidate Mehdi Karroubi claimed that protesters detained after the election were tortured to death in prison and that others were raped.
The Iranian authorities denied the accusations of rape, but admitted that abuses had taken place.
Amnesty has also cited the case of a former detainee who claimed to have been locked up in a container with 75 others for more than eight weeks at a detention centre in Kahrizak.
Amnesty has agreed that the Iranian Parliament and the judiciary will establish a committee to investigate post-election unrest and the government's response, but it has stated that the mandate and powers of these bodies are unclear and that the results of the parliamentary committee have not been made public.
The group said at least 90 people had been arrested in the past three weeks to prevent further protests.
Ski slopes built in the city park
More than 100 tonnes of artificial snow has been used to transform the Bristol site into a competition track.
Piste in the Park attracted 16 semi-professional skiers and snowborders in competition for cash prizes.
At the top of Brandon Hill Park, a small springboard preceded a 100m long track.
John Hickman, a fourth-year student of medicine, said the view from the top of the track was beautiful but it was quite intimidating.
It's a magnificent view, with all the lights of the city twinkling below.
Here are some of the best skiers and snowborders in the country - real talents," he added.
Prizes have rewarded the best aerobatics - or figures made on the track.
After the party of Thursday evening, the snow will be abandoned to the melt.
Always them and us
Can Islam be French?
Pluralism and pragmatism in a secular state.
Roger Bootle, Deloitte
Some readers will be attracted by this new book, admirers of the author's latest book, "Why the French do not like the scarf" (2006), an elegant and well-documented study on a problem that has divided and preoccupied the country for about fifteen years and whose effects are still being felt today.
Mr. Bowen's latest book presents a much broader and more ambitious framework.
A good anthropologist, he doesn’t just want to know what politicians and the media are saying about Islam in France, but what’s happening in the country right now.
He spent many months in mosques, schools and institutes that now provide in France what Mr. Bozen calls "Muslim spaces" for the 5m-6m of Muslims.
He knows how to listen very well and reproduce the debates between teachers and students on the issues that concern them the most.
Is a Muslim having to get married in a mosque or at the town hall (or both)?
Should the evolution and rights of homosexuals be taught to young Muslims?
Can a Muslim woman marry a non-Muslim?
Can a Muslim use an interest-based banking system to get a loan?
It’s these practical problems, he says, which are the daily lot, rather than the political dramas that concern the media.
The author identifies a new generation of imams, teachers and intellectuals, all unknown to the general public, except perhaps Tariq Ramadan, an Arab Muslim born in Switzerland.
This new generation is trying to open the debate about how to be both a good Muslim and a good citizen in a modern secular society.
They don’t push the debate to the end.
Conservatives don't believe in the idea of a French or European Islam.
The intellectuals and activists who have been interviewed by Mr. Bowen are generally in agreement with their counterparts advocates of a puristic Sunni Islam associated with Saudi Arabia which today make up a small part of the Muslims of Europe.
Bowen believes that the values of the Muslims and the secularism of the French could be compatible.
But a compromise can only be reached with the efforts of each side.
He wonders how much the French politicians (and the so virulent guardian intellectual elite of secularism) are actually committed to pluralism.
According to him, Muslims were not spoiled, compared to Catholics, Protestants and Jews who also had to make historical compromises with secular republicanism.
Rather than growing pragmatism, it detects a "narrowness of key values".
Can Islam be French? After reading this book, one is tempted to say "yes, but not yet."
The darkest view in the world
The Arctic is changing faster and more dramatically than any other place on the planet.
The ice that composes it is melting at an alarming speed, taking with it a life that cannot survive elsewhere.
Oil, gas, expeditions, and fishing sail in new open waters, with diplomats, lawyers, and now writers in their wake.
In "On a thin layer of ice", Richard Ellis, writer and illustrator, paints a natural history of the symbol of the far north, the polar bear.
Knowing the history and complicated policies of whaling, he describes the long tradition of Arctic explorers who were proving themselves by chasing the polar bear.
The meeting of Admiral Nelson, then courageous aspirant of 14 years who had to fight only the tip of his musket, is certainly a myth, but the rest is true.
The young cubs, captured as they swam alongside their newly killed mother, were caught and sent to zoos and circuses.
At the beginning of the 20th century, a famous show brought together 75 bears at the same time.
Even today, the dimensions of a zoo are still usually a millionth of the surface necessary for an adult in freedom; the climate is rarely indicated: a polar bear living in Singapore has turned green due to algae growing in its thick fur.
Click here for more information!
Mr. Ellis tells the stories of other writers and historians and often looks back at the threat that hunters pose to species survival.
Although banned in Norway, America and Russia, killings continue in Greenland and Canada where helicopter hunters and Inuit Indians in motoski use powerful assault rifles to drive out their game.
Ironically, it is in the northernmost waters of these two hunting nations that ice will remain the longest.
In his new book, “After the Ice,” Alun Anderson, a former New Scientist editor, offers a clear and scary summary of Arctic science and a captivating insight into what the future may be there.
Not that the scientists have all the answers.
Neither atmospherists nor oceanographers can accurately recount the speed of changes.
It's not for lack of trying.
At the end of XIXth and early 20th, the pioneering journeys of Fridtjof Nansen gave the first information on the melting of the ice sheet.
Cold War submarines then mapped the relief changes.
Satellite surveillance and probes placed on icebergs are of great help, but most of the work is done by hardened men, encamping in the cold.
Mr. Anderson contemplates the extraordinary and tiny world of the tributary system of Arctic ice, formed by brackish water nets squeezed by frosting.
But from the bear mentioned above to the microscopic wonders contained in the ice, everything will be lost with the disappearance of summer ice, expected between 2013 and 2050.
"In a few months, it may be our turn"
Marines, among the 6 000 people who laid crowns on the tombs in Arlington
The 19-year-old De'Angello Robinson first class took a seven-hour bus trip to lay a simple wreath on the grave of a soldier he had never met.
For him and for the other Marines who travelled from Camp Johnson to Jacksonville, North Carolina to the Arlington National Cemetery Cemetery to decorate the tombs, this day was dedicated to greeting men and women who, one day, were in their place.
In a few months, from which we will be sent, we risk being in their place, Robinson said about the fallen soldiers.
If that happened to me, I'd want someone to do the same thing for me, that's why I'm just trying to show respect.
He was one of the 6 000 volunteers to have gathered Saturday morning to lay wreaths on the tombs of veterans, in different sections of Arlington Ceremony cemetery.
In 1992, Morrill Worcester, owner of Worcester Wreath Company in Harrington, Maine, launched the commemoration and others decided to decorate several hundred tombs in Arlington Ceremony Cemetery.
Now, to continue an 18-year tradition, Morrill and his wife Karen, make the trip every year, stopping in different cities on their way, to organize commemorations in honor of the military and the victims of terrorism.
The Worcesters also founded a non-profit association, Wreaths Across America, which aims to promote the event in all other states.
This year, the volunteers placed more than 16,000 wreaths on the graves of Arlington Cemetery around the Pentagon on the tombs of Fayetteville National Cemetery in Arkansas, Battery Park in New York City, and on the website of the United Airlines Flight 93 Memorial in Shanksville, Pennsylvania.
For the second year in a row, the Walmart Foundation has donated more than $150 000 to buy and transport the crowns.
Charleen Hunt, 70, of Westminster, said she made the trip to Arlington in honor of her husband, a deceased career soldier.
Although her husband is not buried in Arlington, Hunt said that for her, it was a way of thanking those who had sacrificed themselves.
“It’s a small gesture that civilians can make to support our troops,” she said.
It's like visiting a family grave.
Wrapped in several layers of warm clothing, the volunteers walked between the lawns and the extended hills of the cemetery.
Volunteers have chosen ancient tombs, dating from the First and Second World Wars, as well as from the Vietnam War, as they are not as often visited as the new tombs, organizers said.
The mood was optimistic, as the children were playing and the couples stood by the hand, carrying the fresh pine wreaths on the gray tombs.
Each round crown had a dark green color, with a small red butterfly tie on the top.
Neaby, there was silence on Section 60, where most of the war veterans in Iraq and Afghanistan are buried.
The United Service Organizations has donated 1 000 crowns to decorate the section, but the dark mood of today's wars filled the area.
Individuals were crying without hiding, others were praying and families and friends supported each other in the cold air.
Some read aloud the names carved in the stone while others leaned over to see the small black and white information cards, embedded in the damp earth for those whose tomble had not yet been completed.
Sandra Lockwood was one of the many mothers in tears, on her son's grave.
She departed from Zanesfield, Ohio and drove eight hours to gather at the grave of Marine Sergeant GunneryDavid Shane Spicer, who died in combat in July.
“No one should ever forget what we’re free of... my son paid for it,” she said.
"Long before I've disappeared, I want someone to remember him."
U.S. National Arrested by Cuban Authorities
The Cuban government arrested a U.S. national who distributed mobile phones and laptops in the country, U.S
The national, who has not yet been identified, works for Development Alternatives Inc., based in Bethesda.
The company works on projects for clients such as the U.S. Agency for International Development and World Bank.
Consular authorities and the U.S. Interest Section in Havana are trying to gain access to the detainee who was arrested on 5 December.
The precise accusations have not been made public, although under Cuban law, a Cuban citizen or a foreign visitor can be arrested under the pretext of being "dangerous".
All so-called revolutionary activities, including moderate protests and critical writings, lead to the risk of arrest.
Anti-government graffiti and discourses are seen as serious crimes.
Cuba has an ever-increasing community of bloggers, led by Yoani Sánchez, a popular commentator who often writes about how she is husband and she is being followed and harassed by government agents.
Sánchez has made several requests to leave the country to receive rewards, but permissions have always been denied.
The detention of a U.S. national may increase tensions between the communist government of the brothers in Cuba and the Obama administration, which is pursuing a “soft” approach in order to improve relations with the island.
The announcement of the arrest was first reported by The New York Times on Friday night.
The new U.S. policy insists that the Cuban government advance in concrete steps, such as releasing political prisoners and creating more space for the opposition, so that the United States can do the same.
Mobile phones and laptops are allowed in Cuba, although these are new and desired consumer goods in a country where the average salary of an official is $15 per month.
Only this year, the Cuban government has granted ordinary citizens the right to buy mobile phones; they are mainly used for texts or for a 15-minute conversation that corresponds to a salary day.
On the island, internet use is extremely limited.
It is available in luxury hotels and for foreign visitors in the country, as well as in some government buildings, such as universities.
Cubans who often wish to connect must communicate their name to the government.
Access to many websites is restricted.
Detaining an American in Cuba is rare.
Most of the few U.S. incarcerated in Cuba are behind bars for crimes such as drug trafficking, says Gloria Berbena, a press secretary to the U.S. Interests Section in Havana.
Berbena said she couldn't provide more information about the arrest.
Arrest and detention is a big mistake.
An activity that, in any other open company, would be allowed — distributing mobile phones free of charge — is a crime in Cuba, said Jose Miguel Vivanco, director of the Human Rights Watch’s Americas program, which recently released a harsh report on freedoms in Cuba, called “New Castro, Even Cuba,” referring to Raul Castro’s facility as the country’s leader to replace his sick older brother.
According to Vivanco, an accused is often arrested, tried and imprisoned on the same day.
He said that any solution would probably be political and that the Cuban government often provokes a negative reaction in the United States, at a time when the two countries are beginning to start a better dialogue.
Pakistani authorities prevent conspiracy to send men to Afghanistan
On Saturday, Pakistani authorities spotted an organization supposedly preparing a plot to send five men from Northern Virginia to Afghanistan in an attempt to kill U.S. troops, saying they hoped the case would dismantle a large network of terrorist recruiters scouring the internet in search of radicalized young men.
Investigators said they were chasing a hidden insurgent personality, known as Saifullah who invited these men to Pakistan after discovering them when one of them made comments approving the terrorist waves on the Internet YouTube video site.
Arriving in Pakistan, these men were guided by Saifullah who tried to help them reach the remote region of Pakistan's tribal belt, home of al-Qaeda and its terrorist training camps.
But a Pakistani intelligence official who was briefed on the case said on Saturday that Saifullah had failed to convince al-Qaeda commanders that these men were not participating in the CIA’s plot to infiltrate the terrorist network.
As a result, they were isolated for days in the eastern city of Sargodha, far from the forbidden mountains of the north-east that have become a refuge from terrorism.
They were regarded as undercover agents.
That is why they have been rejected, said a representative of the authorities who spoke on the condition that they remain anonymous because of the confidential nature of the case.
The representative of the authoritiators claimed that the men were determined and that they were still trying to obtain the necessary authorizations to obtain entry into al-Qaida camps when they were arrested by Pakistani law forces.
The case of these five men -- who remain in Pakistan and who are being questioned by the FBI -- emphasize the crucial role of recruiters in identifying potential and perhaps more important terrorists, in the determination of those who trust.
Since the attacks of September 11, the U.S. secret services have prioritized infiltrating men into the al-Qaeda network.
The organization's recruiters play the role of gatekeepers, refusing entry to those who do not show serious commitment and those who may turn out to be spies.
According to analysts, potential U.S. recruits are being closely studied by al-Qaeda.
But they are also very attractive to the group because of their potential access to U.S. targets and propagonary value.
However, Evan Kohlmann, an expert at the U.S.-based NEFA Foundation, said that terrorist groups also need to pay more and more attention, in recent years in the choice of the people they accept because the U.S. secret services have become experts when it comes to their recruitment methods.
If you’re trying to infiltrate someone into these groups, what better way than following their recruitment model that so many other people have already tracked? said Kohlmann.
The model is the one that is more and more on the Internet.
Tens of thousands of Protestants in Copenhagen call for "action" for the climate
Tens of thousands of Protestants marched through the streets here on Saturday calling for greater climate action from negotiators working in the city’s Bella Convention Center.
The number of protesters was estimated at 100 000 people, but 25 000 according to police estimates.
This event occurred relatively peacefully, although a small number of masked activists lit some explosives near a set of government buildings, downtown.
In that day when little happened during the United Nations-sponsored climate talks, thousands of activists marched around the city, deploying placards in English saying “There is no planet B” and one in Spanish, stating “Earth says it’s had enough.”
Several celebrities have joined the protests, including Denmark’s top model and photographer Helena Christensen, who said that traveling to her mother’s homeland, Peru, has opened his eyes to the heartbreaking issues facing the country because of the impact of climate change that is already occurring.”
“That’s one of the reasons why I decided to join in the great march — to spread the word and appeal to leaders from around the world to make fair, ambitious and engaged decisions,” she said.
It is not an easy task, but it must be undertaken, it is no longer possible to avoid it.
Polices prevented protesters from coming too close to the Bella centre and said they had arrested 19 people on the grounds that they were wearing a mask or were in possession of pocket knives.
Polices prevented protesters from coming too close to the Bella centre and said they had arrested 19 people on the grounds that they were wearing a mask or were in possession of pocket knives.
According to a passerby who asked to remain anonymous because of his involvement in climate talks, activists who wore masks and black clothes lit several explosives near Copenhagen’s main canal, which is surrounded by a few ministries.
They threw them near the buildings, he said, adding that they were first rockets, then followed by "a few big explosions".
Inside the Palais des Congrès, people were gathered around television screens to follow the course, throughout the afternoon.
But it seems that protesters have failed to awaken the consciences of key authorities such as Su Wei, China’s climate representative.
When asked if he thought that a demonstration would have a positive impact on international deliberations, he replied in English "In fact, it's something I wasn't aware of.
He then went on in Mandarin, stating "The event being important, I can't hear what's going on outside."
He observed that walking may seem harmful or useful depending on the prospects.
It shows the interest of the general public and the different sectors on the subject of climate change, he said.
On the other hand, "you can also say that they disrupt negotiations or harm the freedom of others."
Test results reveal stubborn differences between breeds
Last week’s federal report on mathematical results brought good news for Washington, D.C.
Although the District still remains far behind leading institutions, the report shows that Grade 4 and Grade 5 (cm1-cm2) are in much greater progress over the previous two years than in cities such as Atlanta, Chicago and New York.
But what emerges from the latest figures from the National Assessment of Educational Progress is the persistent gap between the results of African-Americans and whites, both locally and nationally.
The average results of white Grade 4 (cm1) children in WashingtonD.C. have increased, over the past two years, from 262 to 270 (on a scale of 500), but those of their African-American classmates have increased by only three points, from 209 to 212.
In fact, the pass gap increased between 2007 and 2009, from 53 to 58 points.
The progress of African-Americans in Grade 8 (4th) remains generally constant, falling only from one point, from 245 to 244.
The average white result was not included in the test results because the sample was not large enough.
An overview over a period of six years is not more encouraging.
In 2003, the gap between blacks and whites in Grade 4 (cm1), when the District's first NAEP report was published, was 60 points (262 to 202).
Although the results obtained by children in both groups increased during this period, the difference decreased only to 58 points.
Last week, some advocates in the District expressed concern that the increase celebrated by Group Director of Educational Institutions Michelle A. Rhee and the Mayor, Adrian M. Fenty (Democracy) were being propelled by white students who were already good.
That would mean that we raised the aggregate by looking at the top-of-the-scale, which is problematic and embarrassing,” said Jeff Smith, Director of D.C. Voice, a non-profit group that advocates for equality in district education.
“I don’t jump of joy for one or two points earned,” Kwame R. Brown (Apparent Democrat), a member of the D.C. Council and whose daughter is in Grade 4, told Eaton Elementary.
Often critical of Rhee’s management style, Brown said that the message of test results is that the city’s middle schools are in desperate need of attention.
Really, you always want to see the positive signs and I respect it.
But what's scary is that we don't spend enough time and energy on our middle schools.
Rhee said the District must continue to find better ways to meet the needs of struggling students.
This fall, for example, some teachers were trained to use a new method of reading, the Wilson Reading System, aimed at schoolchildren in elementary and early elementary school classes who did not integrate the basics of reading earlier in their academic journey and who are quite late compared to their classmates.
Every day, the mathematics program, for Kindergarten (kindergarten) classes at Grade 6 (6th) focuses on real-life games and experiences, as they were set up in the District by former official Clifford Janey, who is also to be credited for NAEP’s progress reported last week.
“I just think we need to continue to work to provide better interventions for these students at a lower level than in the classroom,” Rhee said.
Others believe that the NAEP results raise the question of whether Rhee can continue to elevate the overall results of the system while providing the money and resources for schools with poorer results in order to reduce the gap.
It's a dilemma for Michelle Rhee," said Bruce Fuller, a teaching trainer at the University of Berkeley in California who is studying city test results.
The conventional political solution is to dedicate your resources and management reforms to schools located in Washington’s poorest neighborhoods.
As a result, this targeting can have political repercussions that hinder this important program.
But the problem, of course, is that like the other superintendents of schools, she is trying to use the middle class of whites and blacks.
We are focused on the development of a system of good schools.
We need to strengthen our neighbourhood schools so that all families, regardless of where they come from, are assured that their children will be able to obtain an excellent public education.
Janey, now Superintendent of Newark’s schools, said in an interview this week that one of the essential elements for closing the pass gap was the duration of the traditional public school, now 180 days in the District of Washington, to compete with the Charter schools.
It takes about 200 days to have the strength to change, said Janey, who came to an agreement with his teachers’ union last year to get 185 days, which he considers a “starting point” to get another increase in the next contract.
Making higher education a promise for the future
The bus stopped at Gettysburg College with a long whistle.
Graciela Rodriguez, 12 years old, descends and blinks for a moment at the sight of white columns, brick facades and green lawns.
Graciela's parents had only been able to finish high school in El Salvador.
Until recently, Graciela herself, who was born in Silver Spring and lives in Riverdale, had never set foot on a university campus.
Also, as she and other Grade 8 students (4th) in the group explored Gettysburg where tuitions are $38 690 per year, her attitude was less that of an impressed new student than enthusiastic.
This is where I'm going to study! exclaimed Graciela who would like to study medicine, as the guide directed them towards the entrance of the Science Building.
“Youpi, really?” she said thoughtfully, “when she was told of the low ratio of professor of law – student.”
The University of Pennsylvania was the seventh that the children visited during their three-day discovery trip and now they had completely integrated the message: The question is not whether you are going to college:
The question is where.
Although this question still arises largely for the large number of American children born to Hispanic immigrant parents who have left high school where they became parents as teenagers, they are also hundreds of thousands to benefit from university education that will allow them to enter the middle class.
In fact, "a "second-generan" Hispanic out of five continues his studies in high school -- a noticeable achievement if we consider that so many of their immigrant parents, most of Mexico or Central America, entered the United States without having finished high school.
Their success is important, the researchers say, they pave the way for future generations who will play an important role in the country's workforce.
Investigators who study fighters claim that they have a natural affinity with the school and an innate need to succeed.
There are also many whose parents set high goals for their children and who find ways to compensate for their lack of familiarity with U.S. schools.
Mentoring programs also play a very important role in helping Graciela and millions of childs like she get to university -- in particular, if her efforts are regular and long-term.
When you look at children from low-income families with parents who don’t have the experience and skills to help them navigate the system, any intervention, at any time, won’t help them,” said Patricia Gándara, a researcher at Davis, University of California, who studied Latin American students.
We need to think about offering a network of support to these children, from kindergarten to high school.
The federal program that funded the visit of the universities of Graciela is a good example.
Known as GEAR UP (preparations you), it provides more than $300 million per year to local school systems to organize university preparation programs that begin when students of low-income families are in the middle school (college) and continue until the end of high school years.
Since 1999, the program has been attended by more than 10 million students, with more than 60 percent entering university, according to the U.S. Department of Education.
The Iraqi oil ministry secures agreements with 10 foreign oil companies
Despite concerns about violence and political instability, the Iraqi government has managed to attract significant oil companies in order to rebuild its infrastructure in difficulty during two auctions that ended Saturday.
The 10 agreements reached by the Iraqi Ministry of Oil with foreign oil companies suggest that the oil companies of China, Russia and Europe are about to play a major role in the rehabilitation of Iraq's oil industry, invalidated by decades of war and sanctions.
U.S. companies left with a stake in just two of the 10 fields auctioned.
Seven U.S. companies had paid to participate in the second auction that began Friday.
The only one who took an auction lost.
Two U.S. companies were awarded contracts for fields auctioned in June.
The weak portrayal of U.S. oil giants in the opening of the oil sector in Iraq surprised analysts.
Iraq has finally opened its doors, after six years of war, and instead of U.S. companies, are Asian and European companies leading the way, said Ruba Husari, editor of the Iraq Oil Forum, an internet news site.
It will take a long time before another offer is made in Iraq.
Security concerns, corroborated by massive coordinated bombings on Tuesday, and political instability as U.S. military troops withdrew have held back U.S. oil companies, preventing them from forcibly entering Iraq, which owns the world’s third-largest crude oil reserve, analysts say.
U.S. companies were, in some cases, disadvantaged because of their rivals, particularly the Chinese and other government-controlled energy companies, whose labor costs were significantly lower and who were more willing to take risks because they did not have to report to shareholders.
Exxon-Mobil and Occidental Petroleum Inc. were the only U.S. companies to obtain contracts with the Department.
The most important American companies, such as Chevron and ConocoPhillips, which maintained strong ties with the Iraqi Ministry of Petroleum and provided technical advice, left empty-handed.
The Russian companies Lukoil and Gazprom were the two main shareholders in two of the contracts awarded this weekend.
The state-owned Chinese National Petroleum Corp. has biddered more on contracts than other companies and has left with significant holdings in contracts for two main fields.
We all know that China is becoming a major economy, as well as a technological powerhouse,” said Assam Jihad, spokesman for the Ministry of Oil.
We are confident that the Chinese company will be on par with its competitors and will fulfill its commitments in Iraq.
Pre-selected companies to submit auctions made bids that were then compared to the price per barrel that the department agreed to pay to motivate production above current levels for each field.
One of al-Qaida's main organizers was reportedly killed in Pakistan
It appears that a U.S
If the information is confirmed, this would be the second deadly attack on one of the terrorist leaders this fall.
Saleh al-Somali was one of two Arab men suspected of having been killed by a pair of missiles thrown into their car on Tuesday near the city of Miran Shah in North Waziristan province, according to U.S. sources and Pakistani authorities in the region.
Local authorities claimed that the missiles had been fired by an unmanned aircraft of the type used by the CIA, inside Pakistan's anarchic tribal border.
They were driving a white car, heading towards the Afghan border, when the car was hit, said a member of Pakistan’s civilian secret services, speaking on the phone of Miran Shah.
Authority officials said suspicious local activists rushed to the scene and quickly confiscated what remained of the "totally shredded bodies".
It has been totally impossible for local authorities to verify the identity of the victims, but the two U.S. anti-terrorist representatives have given unspecified evidence that Somali was among the dead.
Somali was described as one of al-Qaeda’s Military Organizing Officials leading the group’s terrorist operations outside the Afghanistan-Pakistan region.
His role was to organize plots around the world, said one of the authorities who wished to remain anonymous, quoting the sensitive nature of air strikes inside Pakistani territory.
Given its central role, this probably included conspiracies of attacks against the United States and Europe.
He followed the strategic guidelines of al-Qaida leaders and translated them into action plans for possible terrorist attacks.
A second representative of the U.S. authority said that Somali had progressed rapidly in the ranks of al-Qaeda and had very good relations with other extremist groups in the region.
It may not be known to the general U.S. public, but it doesn’t diminish the threat it poses to us and our allies.
If his death is confirmed, Somali would be the second prominent figure of al-Qaeda or the second Taliban leader killed since September, when a similar attack killed Najmuddin Jalolov, leader of a faction of militants in the Tribal Belt, as well as three other operators.
The pace of attacks by CIA drones has decreased since the summer, from about six operations per month to two, according to figures put forward by the Long War Journal, a website moderated by a non-profit group.
This decline may be due to improved tactics by terrorist groups that have progressed to reduce their vulnerability while also ruthlessly killing suspected informants, the site said.
UN Special Envoy to Kabul Withdraws
UN Special Envoy to Afghanistan Kai Eide said Friday that he would resign his post in March at the end of a tumultuous term marred with allegations of widespread corruption during UN-backed presidential elections.
Eide's departure comes as the Obama administration has decided to send an additional 30 000 troops to Afghanistan.
The U.N. envoy said he supports this increase but expressed concern about the U.S. timetable for a withdrawal of troops that would begin in 18 months and that would force other NATO governments to withdraw their forces.
We need to accelerate the development of our security forces in Afghanistan, send the right signal to the Afghans and remind them that they can have confidence in the international community, Eide said in a Kabul telephone interview.
Our commitment must be in the long term.
The Norwegian diplomat has also urged the United States and other military powers to increase the number of international civil servants helping the political transition in Afghanistan.
The military increase must be copied by civilians, he said.
Eide said that he did not resign but that he would simply abide by his commitment to his family in March to spend only two years in Kabul.
He said he wanted to warn the United Nations.
Secretary-General Ban Ki-moon now knows that he has time to find a replacement.
“I just said it’s better to start looking for my replacement,” Eide said.
When I came here, there had been a two-month void between the departure of my predecessor and my arrival.
According to U.N. officials, Ban has already begun looking for a replacement.
Representatives say Staffan di Mistura's name has been cited; it is an Italian-Swiss citizen who recently chaired a U.N. mission in Baghdad and Jean-Marie Guéhenno, a Frenchman who previously led UN peacekeeping operations.
Eide's reputation in Afghanistan was tested after his former deputy, Peter W. Galbraith, accused him in September of favoring President Hamid Karzai during the presidential elections and concealing evidence of massive electoral fraud.
Eide denied these allegations, but he said Galbraith’s accusations — which was dismissed — “harmed the mission because there was already a healthy dose of skepticism regarding international observation of elections.”
Eide said he had proposed the appointment of a civilian representative to coordinate the relief of U.S. forces in Afghanistan.
He also urged UN officials to allow his successor to hire more staff from the U.S. and other Western countries than is attributed to the mission in Afghanistan, saying it would give them more confidence on how their money is spent.
Eide expressed frustration about the limits of its powers in Afghanistan, saying the UN’s heavy hiring regulations are hindering its opportunities to bring new talent.
“The UN rules are such that I’ve only been able to recruit one person since May,” he said.
It's disastrous and it can't last.
Gates: Iran must undergo additional sanctions
Defense Secretary Robert Gates said on Friday that major world powers would soon impose "significant additional sanctions" on Iran because of its inability to engage in talks on nuclear ambitions.
Gates, speaking outside a crowd of 300 U.S. soldiers in northern Iraq, during a week-long visit to war zones in Afghanistan and Iraq, dedramatized the possibility of military action against the Islamic Republic.
There is no good option in Iran, he said, in response to a soldier's question about the possibility of such an outcome.
One of the things that weighs me the most is that if I have learned one thing in Iraq in the last six years, it is indeed the unpredictable side inherent in the war.
The Obama administration considers a package of sanctions that would target Iran’s military and political elite, but Gates has signaled that some of these sanctions could also affect street Iranians.
He said that a "set of motives and punishments" might be needed to persuade the Iranian government that it would in fact be less secure with nuclear weapons" because "his people would suffer enormously" from these sanctions.
Friday, in a statement by White House spokesman Robert Gibbs, the administration joined European leaders in a warning that Iran will face "probable" consequences if it does not bring back its nuclear program in full compliance with the UN Security Council and its monitoring body, the International Atomic Energy Agency (IAEA).
Iran persists in saying that it wants to develop its nuclear experience only for a peaceful purpose.
In the Geneva talks on October 1, he said he would resume the dialogue on reducing his nuclear program and agreed to abandon a significant part of its storage of enriched uranium, in exchange for fuel desperately needed for a medical research reactor.
The administration pushed such an agreement, a way to build trust on both sides and gain time for negotiations.
Since then, Iran appears to have moved away from attempts to agree – in part, experts say, because Iranian leadership is divided on whether or not to engage with the United States.
Frankly, in my view, Iran's stiffening in the face of some of the international community's proposals for which they had actually agreed at the beginning of October, prompted the international community, including the Russians and the Chinese, to join on what they had not envisaged, in terms of significant additional sanctions against the Iranians, Gates said.
President Obama set December 31 as Iran’s deadline to respond to the proposals, before he turned to other options, including the continuation of what Secretary of State Hillary Rodham Clinton once called “increased sanctions.”
In a statement Thursday in New York, Iran's mission to the United Nations denounced what it calls "baseless allegations" by some members of the Security Council about Iran's nuclear activities and said it agreed to continue talks with the United States and five other major world powers, "in order to achieve a long-term satisfactory solution."
Gates, who was scheduled to return to Washington late Friday, met in the morning with Iraqi Prime Minister Nouri al-Maliki before flying to the rich Kurdish oil region to meet troop leaders in Kirkuk and Irbil.
Tensions persist between the Kurds and the Arab majority in Iraq, particularly with regard to borders, property rights and income sharing.
Gates urged both sides to reduce the potential conflict in order to avoid any delay in U.S. plans to reduce the number of U.S. troops, from 115 000 to 50 000 before the end of August.
Gates also wants to calm Kurds' anxieties about the outstanding withdrawal. U.S. authorities quoted Gates telling Massoud Barzani, President of the Kurdistan Regional Government: "We will preserve your security, prosperity and autonomy within a unified Iraq."
We won’t abandon you.”
In aging China, a change of direction
Wang Weijia and her husband grew up surrounded by propaganda posters preaching them, "The Mother Earth is too tired to accommodate more children" and "An extra baby, it's an extra grave."
They have learned so well the lesson that the authorities of the Government of Shanghai, alarmed by the insufficient birth rate of their city and by the ageing of the population, have radically changed direction this summer and have begun to encourage young couples to have more than one child; their reaction has been instantaneous and firm: never.
We’ve already given all our time and energy to one child.
We don't have any seconds left," said Wang, 31, a human resources manager who has an 8-month-old son.
More than 30 years after the introduction of the one-child policy in China, creating two generations of unique children notoriously with good cheeks and spoiled, nicknamed the "little emperors", a population crisis threatens the country.
According to the UN, the average birth rate fell by 1.8 children per couple compared to six when the policy was implemented.
The distribution of the population, while it is expected that the number of residents aged 60 and over will explode, from 16.7 percent of the population in 2020 to 31.1 percent in 2050. This figure is well above the overall average of about 20 percent.
This imbalance is even worse in rich coastal cities with a highly educated population, such as Shanghai.
Last year, people aged 60 and older accounted for nearly 22 percent of the population of residents registered in Shanghai, while the birth rate was less than one child per couple.
Xie Lingli, Director of the Shanghai Municipal Population and Family Planning Commission, said fertile couples must have babies in order to "help reduce the proportion of the ageing population and prevent a lack of manpower in the future."
Shanghai is about to become "as old - but not as rich, however - as developed countries such as Japan and Sweden," she said.
Gradual Attenuation
Drafted in the country's constitution in 1978, China's one-child policy is to this day, unquestionably, the most controversial obligation introduced by the communist government.
Couples who violate this policy may have to pay colossal fines - up to three times their annual income - and be discriminated against in the workplace.
The Chinese authorities have given the police the duty to help the country avoid a dangerous trend for its natural resources, while human rights lawyers have denounced the abuses in the execution of this policy.
In rural areas, some representatives of the authority have forced pregnant women of a second child to have an abortion.
In addition, many couples have resorted to selective abortions leading to an abnormally high-girl ratio.
In recent years, the authorities responsible for the population have gradually softened their position on the one-child policy.
In 2004, they granted more exceptions to the rule -- including for urban residents, members of ethnic minorities, and cases in which men and women are already single children -- and in 2007, they softened many of their harsh slogans.
Qiao Xiaochun, a professor at the Institute of Population Research at Peking University, said that central government authorities have recently discussed even more radical changes, such as allowing couples to have two children if one of the partners is an only child.
In July, Shanghai became China’s first city to launch an aggressive campaign to encourage births.
In no time, posters prompting families to have only one child have been replaced by copies of detailing regulations that have the right to have a second child and how to apply.
The city's government has dispatched family planning officials and volunteers to meet couples at their homes, and slip leaflets under their doors.
He also pledged to provide psychological and financial advice to those who would like to have more than one child.
According to the head of family planning, the response was disappointing.
Disappointing response
Although officials in a rumatic city on the outskirts of Shanghai say they have seen an increase in requests for records of couples wanting a second child after the launch of the campaign, the more urban district has reported that there is no change.
The municipality of Huinan, with a population of 115 000 inhabitants, for example, still receives only four to five requests per month.
Shanghai officials, disappointed, say that despite the campaign, the number of births in the city in 2010 is still projected to be only about 165,000 -- slightly above 2009 but less than 2008.
Feng Juying, Head of the Family Planning Committee in Shanghai’s Caolu Municipality, said financial considerations are probably the main reason many people don’t want to have more children.
“They want to provide for their first child,” she said.
Yang Jiawei, 27 and his wife, Liu Juanjuan, 26, said they would love having two children and that they are legally licensed there.
But like many Chineses, they only have the minimum health insurance and life insurance given by the government.
Without social protection, they say, the decision would be irresponsible.
Westerners mistakenly consider the one-child's policy to be a rights issue, said Yang, a construction engineer whose wife is seven months pregnant with the couple's first child.
That's right, we're being robbed the chance to have more than one child.
But the problem isn't just the law.
That's the money.
Other couples cite psychological reasons to explain their hesitation.
Wang, Head of Human Resources, said she only wanted an one child because she was one herself. "We were the center of the family and used everyone to take care of us."
We’re not used to taking care of someone else and we really don’t want to take care of others.”
Chen Zijian, a 42-year-old owner of a translation company, says so more crudely.
For couples working together, middle class, who lower the birth rate, he says, you have to be selfish to succeed.
The 20- or 30-year-old people have grown up seeing their parents fight in the early days of capitalist China and don’t want to have that lifestyle themselves, he says.
“Even only one child takes a lot of time from their parents,” he said.
A mother must give up at least two years of her social life.
Then there are space problems -- "You have to modify your apartment" -- and planning -- "When your child is only 9 months old, you have to write his resume to get a place in the best kindergarten."
Most of his friends are ready for this experience once, Chen says, but not two.
“Our generation is the first to benefit from a higher standard of living,” he said.
We don’t want to make too many sacrifices.
The United Nations Group has draft plans for emission reductions
The UN-sponsored climate conference — characterized so far by disorganized assignments and mutual recriminations — came back in the spotlight on Friday, due to the release of a document outlining ambitious greenhouse gas reductions over the next 40 years, with industrialized naties covering most of the burden in the coming times.
The text that could provide the basis for a final political agreement to regulate greenhouse gases, highlighted the remaining obstacles, as much as it has illuminated the path to be taken.
But it has been seen as an important step forward in negotiations that are short of time, with more than 100 leaders from all over the world arriving next week in Copenhagen.
Drafted by an ad hoc UN working group, the text does not say how much the rich countries would give to the poor to support global warming, both over time and in the long term.
And it offers a wide range of options for key issues, including how developed economies and new emerging economies could decrease their carbon production and what would be the upper limit of the global temperature rise that those responsible would be willing to tolerate.
It gives great flexibility to the process,” said John Coequyt, the Sierra Club’s Washington Representative.
Michael Zammit Cutajar, who wrote six pages of the document, studied a 180-page negotiating text to focus on what the UN’s Executive Secretary-General on Climate Change, Yvo de Boer, described as the “comprehensive image.”
It shows the guidelines of a possible agreement in which industrial nations would collectively reduce their emissions in 2020, from 25 to 45 per cent compared to 1990 levels, while the main developing countries, over the same period, would reduce theirs from 15 to 30 percent.
Together, countries would reduce emissions between 50 and 95 per cent by 2050.
The European Union also relaunched negotiations on Friday, promising to provide $3.6 billion per year for the next three years to help the poorest countries adapt to the impact of climate change -- to cope with floods and drought or to avoid deforestation.
However, on Friday, the same verbal altercations occurred, which have dominated the negotiations since last week.
Todd Stern, U.S. Representative on Climate Negotiations, has rejected any discussion that requires the reduction of greenhouse gases by industrialized countries, in comparison to the voluntary efforts made by the major emerging economies, if they were not financed by developed countries.
This change is the signal that the Obama administration is getting tougher on China than the Bush administration was two years ago.
The United States will not sign an agreement without the efforts and actions of the main developing countries, says Stern who also regretted that the text does not insist enough to ensure that reductions could be verified by outside observers.
Stern commented an hour after China's Deputy Foreign Minister He Yafei said the U.S. leading negotiator was either lacking "common sense" or was "extremely irresponsible" for saying earlier in the week that the U.S. wouldn't financially help China fight global warming.
With the future economic trajectories of the world's main powers at stake, flaws have emerged both within the Third World and between the industrial world and emerging economies.
The current battle is more about rescuing individual economies than saving the planet, with China and the United States quarreling over their respective obligations while poorer nations insist that the two dozens of most influential countries ignore the scientific imperatives of acting more courageously.
Ricardo Ulate, a delegate from Costa Rica, said it was not surprising that the main powers were fighting over who would have to suffer the costs of reducing greenhouse gases, even though vulnerable countries had become more aggressive in their attempt to make the major emitters responsible for their actions.
It’s really a game in which a new economic hegemony is developed, said Ulate, which is also Climate Advisor for Mexico and Central America for Conservation International.
Some of these countries more vulnerable to the impact of climate change have indicated that they will continue to put pressure on a legally obligating treaty in Copenhagen, although most of the main participants say that the negotiations will lead at a minimum to a political agreement.
The Alliance of Small Island States, which brings together 43 members, drafted a 24-page treaty proposal early Friday morning.
Artur Runge-Metzger, Head of Climate Unity of the European Commission, said that the action of small island states has "put political pressure on the full political process", in part by what they are now unified and that they ask emerging economies such as China and India to act.
Debates have become an urgent matter, as delegates focused on the fact that they had to solve most of the pending issues before heads of government reached an agreement.
Senior officials such as India’s Environment Minister Jairam Ramesh and China’s Deputy Minister got off their planes and rushed into the Bella Center building to attend closed-door meetings and information conferences, so they could make claims that will be considered in the following week.
The real image of the rally -- during which 13 000 people enter and leave the Palais des congrès every day -- guitar activists present nightly shows mocking the countries that they believe are betrayed and that proposals are written and distributed on paper rather than by emails -- is a real challenge.
Intensity is only increasing: almost all important ministers are here and no later than Wednesday, 60 heads of government will be in Copenhagen.
“We’re entering the big league,” said Carlos Manuel Rodriguez, Vice President for Global Policy at Conservation International.
The trucks are coming.
In Britain, the first train at 225 km/h will redefine Monday syndrome
At 5h13, Monday morning, a whistle will pierce at night to signal the departure of a train that will mark a transport revolution in Britain.
The country's first high-speed commuter train will reach 225 km/h as it runs from Ashford in Kent to London St Pancras, covering 93 km in 38 minutes.
Lord Adonis, the Minister of Transport, will be joined by Lady Kelly Holmes aboard the first Javelin commuter train outside London.
He hopes that the new service will enable Great Britain to integrate an international network of fast and reliable trains and will connect the major conurbations from the Midlands, the North and Scotland to London.
Lost small holes will become thriving suburban cities, the durations of business trips will be much shorter and the transition from plane and car to the train will reduce national carbon emissions while reducing traffic jams on the roads.
In theory, the three main political parties adhere to this project.
But the ten billion pounds needed to build a new rail network, the inevitable planning battle, and fears of dangers to the environment are likely to curb Britain’s high-speed lines.
According to The Times, High Speed Two (HS2), a company created in January to carry out a feasibility report for the government, will return its document to Lord Adonis on December 30.
This will allow it to obtain a detailed map of the route of the next step planned for the high-speed network.
The new line linking London to the West Midlands will be drawn within five meters of the urban area and sites where the impact on the environment can be controversial.
In the countryside, the plan will be drawn up to 25 meters from the final route.
It is also expected that HS2 will present three options for a larger high-speed network, going north.
Lord Adonis will respond in the spring.
Preferred options are highlighted in a Y-shaped configuration for which a high-speed line will go to the West Midlands.
The line will split in two to or near Birmingham, with one branch going west of the Pennines at Manchester and Scotland and another going north-east, towards Sheffield, Leeds and Newcastle upon Tyne.
There will be a single line across Scotland.
“It looks like it’s going to be the best performance option,” said a rail professional.
When this is over, the travel time between London and Edinburgh will be reduced to 2 hours 40 minutes.
The first branch, which won’t open until 2025, will allow all passengers to travel between London and Birmingham in 49 minutes, compared to a one-hour trip right now.
The proposals will allow the train to travel at 400km/h, making the world’s fastest British network.
The initial route is designed to allow trains of 400m in length and able to carry 1100 people.
Up to 18 trains per hour can operate on a line London - Birmingham.
A terminal that can accommodate 20000 passengers per hour would be required.
Given the space constraints, it is expected that an existing station will be expanded to receive the high-speed network.
Experts in the rail industry state that only St Pancras International or Euston offers this opportunity.
Lord Adonis will announce whether or not he thinks the project needs to come together, but longer public consultations and planning procedures mean that a final decision will not be made before a general election.
Even if it launches the machine, the network will have to be built part by party.
Trains will initially be forced to drive off high-speed lines on existing rails north of Birmingham, keeping travel times between London and Scotland longer than three hours.
This solution seems to be the best to ensure the passage from aircraft to trains, constituting a reference point for the rail revolution.
Taxpayers reimburse GBP 75,000 loan for Tory's orangery
Alan Duncan, the main member of the Tory parliament, paid the taxpayer thousands of pounds a year to reimburse for the cost of an orangery he built in his constituency house.
The leader of the party that was demoted by David Cameron for complaining that members of parliament were "maintained", increased his loan by GBP 75,000 to build an oak extension at his second home in Rutland.
He had been allowed to charge additional interest, amounting to hundreds of pounds per month, to the taxpayer.
The Commons authorities received the complaints at that time and were not questioned by Sir Thomas Legg, who carries out a control of members' expenses. Last night, Mr. Duncan said the complaints could not be "clearer or simpler".
Mr. Duncan, one of the wealthiest members of parliament, owns a two-storey house in a village in his constituency of Rutland - Melton.
Neighbouring houses have been sold for nearly a million pounds.
The ground floor includes a kitchen, a living room and a dining room, but the emoluments office states that Mr. Duncan needed more space.
Last year, he added a veranda at home, describing it in his plans as an orange grove.
Neighbors describe it as a "veranda for pleasure".
Mr. Duncan was not asked to provide an explanation for the loan increase during a recent investigation by the Commons Privileges and Standards Committee regarding its pre-borrowing provisions.
The Committee cleared him last month of any charges of non-compliance, following an investigation into his expenses.
In 2004, Mr. Duncan changed the legal certainty of borrowing 271,406 GBP from his property in London from his constituency, which he purchased in 1991, the year before becoming a member of parliament.
Documents released this week show that Mr. Duncan claimed about 1,400 GBPS per month of borrowed interest until March last year.
In April, his applications increased by more than 1,800 GBP per month.
The interest rate on Mr. Duncan's RBS loan was not changed at that time, suggesting that all increases were intended to finance the borrowing of GBP 75,000.
The RBS Variable Standard Interest Rate rose from 7.94 percent in December 2007 to 4 per cent in March this year, without moving ever since.
The last publication of the Commons authorities shows that Mr. Duncan continued to request 1,250 GBP in May.
Duncan lost his position as Shadow Cabinet Manager in September, after an under-coursed journalist allegedly filmed him stating that members of parliament had to survive "on budget" after the spending scandal.
"In fact, it's now national, you have to live "on budget" and you're being treated as "m...e"
He said: "I spent my money on my garden and asked for a small party, based on what was right.
And I could've asked damn more, but I didn't."
The member of parliament, a millionaire since his former post as an oil trader, began to have to explain his expenses, in May, after it emerged that he had asked for thousands of pounds for his garden, before agreeing with the expense bureau that this "could be considered excessive".
An activist dug a £-shaped hole in Mr Duncan's lawn, protesting after it was revealed that he had asked for 4,000 GBP for three years.
Asked about the increase in borrowing last night, Duncan said: "It was for capital improvements, approved both by the expense bureau and then by Legg."
He added: "There is no problem of evading or drafting or using the rules or anything.
It was quite clear, in the rules, allowed, etc.
It couldn’t be clearer or simpler.”
Loyal, all come and see Brighton's Advent calendar
Brighton’s beach cabins have found a new mode of promotion for the winter months, an interactive Advent calendar.
Beyond, an alternative church group that encourages spiritual exploration through creativity, invited 24 homeowners to decorate their cabin for each day of December, using Noel chaps as a theme.
The event began on December 1st, while visitors were allowed to visit the last cabin, from 17h30 to 18h30, every day, with pâtés and mulled wine served free of charge.
The Christmas songs chosen so far include "I Saw Three Ships, O Come, O Come, Emmanuel and The First Noël", for which the booth features the work of Janette Tozer, a local artist.
Martin Poole, 50, a TV marketing assistant from Hove, is an unpaid priest from the diocese of Chichester and director of Beyond.
He said: "We hope that religion is closer to people in a post-Christian society.
"As a Church, why are we waiting for people to enter into a strange old building? It seems to me that the Church should get closer to people and celebrate in a more captivating way.
"The idea of the Advent calendar of the beach cabin came just after a conversation with a few friends, over dinner.
Brighton is such a creative and vibrant place and we are trying to experiment and represent this through spirituality.”
Strong mobilization in Copenhagen and around the world for the climate
Tens of thousands demonstrated on Saturday to call for an ambitious and binding agreement on the fight against global warming, around the world and in Copenhagen, where the police carried out several hundred arrests.
At least thirty thousand people, according to the police, a hundred thousand according to the organizers, marched in the cold in the Danish capital, on the sidelines of the United Nations Climate Conference, tasked with reaching by Friday an agreement that can come into force on January 1, 2013.
Connie Hedegaard, Danish president of the climate conference, said the growing climate mobilization around the world, illustrated by these demonstrations, helped to make "the political price" of a very high failure in Copenhagen.
Incidents in the Danish capital occurred shortly after the procession left, when a group of 300 protesters, entirely dressed in black, smashed windows with cobblestones and hammers, an AFP journalist said.
Riot police immediately intervened unceasingly.
In total, the police announced the arrest of 600 to 700 people, mainly according to them members of the "Black Blocs", these ultra-violent groups who had distinguished themselves in particular at the NATO summit in Strasbourg, in eastern France, in April.
The Climate Justice Action (CJA), one of the organizers of the demonstration, denounced the conditions in which hundreds of activists were arrested "without distinction", stressing that a hundred of them "were still held in the street on Saturday night "handcuffed and kept in a sitting position despite the extreme cold".
Party of Parliament, the procession, rather good child, took the direction of the Bella Center, site of the negotiations.
The protesters stopped about 500 meters from the building, without trying to enter.
A scene had been erected to welcome the planned speakers, before a candlelight vigil with the participation of the former South African archbishop of Cape Town, Desmond Tutu.
The majority of the protesters had come by coach and train from Europe’s major cities, but many Asians, including some Chinese and Koreans, were also present as well as Africans.
Nearly 3,000 people, mostly in sky-blue waterproofs, had formed a first gathering in the morning in Copenhagen at the call of the Friends of the Earth, who intended to form "blue tides" for "climate justice".
Today, we’re taking to the streets to seek reparadiment of ecological debt in favor of the South,” said Lidy Nacpil, a Filipino activist from the Jubilee South Coalition in Copenhagen.
We can't keep telling each other: we've got some time," said Beninese singer Angélique Kidjo.
There are rivers that dry up in Africa, streams where you can walk like you've never done before.
For the first time in the history of climate diplomacy, born in 1992 with the adoption of the UN Convention, the anti-globalization movement has approached environmental organizations.
French MEP José Bové, a figure of alterglobalism, explained that he came to Copenhagen to "bind climate justice and social justice": "today, there is no cut between the fight against global warming and the fight for another world".
The Asia-Pacific region, which is home to many islands that are particularly vulnerable to warming, had kicked off protests.
Approximately 50,000 people, according to organizers, had taken to the streets in Australia.
In Manila, a few hundred people, students for the most, marched in red, sporting bandanas touting solar energy.
In Hong Kong or Jakarta, but also in Canada, rallies of a few hundred protesters have also been held to call for strong action against climate change.
In France, demonstrations organized by the "350" network brought together a few hundred people, especially in Paris, Marseille (south), Lille (north), Bordeaux (west) and Lyon (east).
A few dozen people gathered in Geneva.
Separatist Abkhazia has elected its president, Georgia denounces a "farce"
Abkhazia on Saturday elected its president, a little more than a year after Moscow’s recognition of Georgia’s pro-Russian separatist region as Tbilisi denounces a “farce.”
Some 131,000 people were called to participate in this first election after the Russian-Georgian war of August 2008 for the control of South Ossetia, another Georgian secessionist territory recognized by Russia.
The polling stations closed at 17H00 GMT.
The turnout was 58% a few hours before the close, according to officials of the Abkhaz Electoral Commission.
The first provisional results were to be announced in the night from Saturday to Sunday.
The five candidates, including the outgoing president, Sergei Bagapch, categorically reject the idea of reunification with Georgia, which condemned the holding of the vote.
These elections are a farce.
For his part, Georgian President Mikheil Saakashvili "regrets that Abkhazia has fallen under the total occupation of Russia," his spokeswoman, Manana Manjgaladze, said.
In addition to Russia, only Nicaragua and Venezuela have recognized the independence of this territory of 216,000 inhabitants, while the rest of the world considers it as part of Georgia illegally occupied by Russian troops.
After splitting from Georgia during the 1992-1993 conflict, which resulted in several thousand deaths, Abkhazia seeks to show through this election that it is ready to join the international community.
This process is a stage in our new life, our new era as an independent state, 54-year-old Svetlana Kvartchia told AFP, who claimed to have voted for Mr. Bagapch.
She was among the voters who went to the polling station at a school in Sukhumi, the capital, in a pretty white building with a few palm trees in front of the entrance.
The question of Russian domination is at the heart of the presidency in this sumptuous region, on the edge of the Black Sea.
Large panels showing Mr. Bagapch alongside Russian President and Prime Minister Dmitry Medvedev and Vladimir Putin are installed everywhere in this small territory of 216,000 inhabitants.
In Gali, in the west of the country, the head of the district, Beslan Archba, reserved a warm welcome to the Russian officials who came to monitor the conduct of the election.
We, the Abkhaz people, are grateful, he said during a small ceremony, referring to the support of Moscow, in particular the decision to recognize the independence of Abkhazia.
I hope that your country will soon be recognized by the United Nations, replied Alexei Ostrovsky, a member of the Russian parliament, raising a glass of vodka.
For his part, the opposition represented by former Vice-President Raoul Khadjimba and two businessmen, Zaous Ardzinba and Beslan Boutba, criticizes the agreements reached with Moscow last year, such as the one ceding control over the Abkhaz railways for 10 years.
The fifth candidate, Vitali Bganba, claims not to support either the current power or the opposition.
A majority of 50% plus a vote is needed to win the election.
If no candidate reaches this threshold, a second round must be organized within two weeks.
Climate: demonstrations around the world, incidents in Copenhagen
Tens of thousands of people marched around the world on Saturday calling for an ambitious and binding deal at the Copenhagen Climate Conference, where the police, on the alert, arrested nearly 700 people on the sidelines of an imposing demonstration.
Thirty thousand people, according to the police, a hundred thousand according to the organizers, marched in the cold in the afternoon in the Danish capital, which hosts until December 18 the delegates of 193 countries, in search of an agreement that will enter into force on January 1, 2013.
Party of Parliament, the procession, generally good child, has taken the leadership of the Bella Center, where the negotiations take place.
The protesters stopped about 500 meters from the center, without trying to enter it.
A scene had been erected to welcome the planned speakers, before a candlelight vigil with the participation of the former South African archbishop of Cape Town, Desmond Tutu.
Moments after the processibly departed, a group of a few hundred demonstrators, entirely dressed in black, armed with bricks and hammers, shattered windows, an AFP journalist said.
Riot police immediately encircled them and intervened unceasingly, throwing several of them ashore.
The police announced the arrest of 600 to 700 people, specifying that they were mainly members "of the Black Blocs", these ultra-violent groups who had distinguished themselves at the NATO summit in Strasbourg, in eastern France, in April.
In the late afternoon, a police officer was wounded in the jaw by a pavement and four cars burned near a squat during incidents that led to the arrest of about twenty people, according to the police.
We keep an eye on the small extremist groups, police spokesman Henrik Jakobsen warned, as law enforcement helicopters were in the skies.
The majority of protesters, who came by coach and train from the major cities of Germany, London, Amsterdam or Milan, were of European origin.
But many Asians, including some Chinese and Koreans, were also present as well as Africans.
Nearly 3,000 people, mostly in sky-blue waterproofs, had formed a first gathering in the morning in Copenhagen at the call of the Friends of the Earth, who intended to form "blue tides" for "climate justice".
Today, we’re taking to the streets to seek reparadiment of ecological debt in favor of the South,” said Lidy Nacpil, a Filipino activist with the Jubilee South Coalition in Copenhagen.
We can't keep telling each other: we've got some time," said Beninese singer Angélique Kidjo.
There are rivers drying out in Africa, there are streams where you can walk when you've never done it before.
For the first time in the history of climate diplomacy, born in 1992 with the adoption of the UN Convention, the anti-globalization movement has approached environmental organizations.
French MEP José Bové, a figure of alter-globalization, told AFP he came to Copenhagen to "link climate justice and social justice": "today, there is no cut between the fight against global warming and the fight for another world".
Oxfam has mobilized several personalities, including Danish model Helena Christensen, former U.N. refugee commissioner Mary Robinson, who were to address the crowd.
The Asia-Pacific region, which is home to many islands that are particularly vulnerable to warming, had kicked off protests.
Approximately 50,000 people, according to organizers, took to the streets in Australia.
In Manila, a few hundred people, mostly students, demonstrated, dressed in red and sporting bandanas touting solar energy in front of the town hall of the Philippine capital.
In Hong Kong or Jakarta, rallies of a few hundred protesters have also been held to call for action against climate change.
In France, demonstrations organized by the "350" network brought together a few hundred people, especially in Paris, Marseille (south), Lille (north), Bordeaux (west) and Lyon (east).
A few dozen people gathered in Geneva.
Geo-history emerges strengthened from the reform of the general high school, according to Chatel
National Education Minister Luc Chatel said in an interview to be published in the JDD on Sunday that history and geography emerged "strengthened" from the reform of the general high school, despite the abolition of their compulsory teaching in scientific terminale.
From now on, foremost students will all have four hours per week and the same program for a shared common culture, says Mr. Chatel.
For the minister, "we have to get out of hypocrisy" because, according to him, "the S sector is no longer a scientific sector" but "basically, it concentrates the best students".
The sectors ES and L cannot remain sub-sectors of the S, he continues, stressing again: "I have also not heard many historians and intellectuals indignant about the absence of history-geography in terminale in technological high schools".
Saturday, an Ifop poll published in L'Humanité revealed that nearly seven out of ten French (69%) disapproved of the government's plan to remove the compulsory teaching of history-geography in S. Many intellectuals and teachers also criticized this suppression, as well as the left and some right-wing elected officials.
On the other hand, the minister added that there was "no question" of removing the philosophy of the terminale S program.
Luc Chatel also defended the reform of teacher training, while 16 organizations (students and unions) called for a day of mobilization on Tuesday to protest against the project, which according to them sacrifices the pedagogical training of future teachers.
In Master 2 (M2), they will pass at the beginning of the year an eligibility test that will focus on their discipline, then at the end of the year an admission test that will focus on their ability to teach, explained the minister.
They will also follow internships in situation during the year of M2 and then will be a first year in front of students as trainee teachers.
If you add up all the internships offered before the competition, you already get more attendance in front of the students, i.e. more than 300 hours, he continued.
Finally, the minister indicates that he will propose "individual right to training, something that does not exist at national education".
It also reaffirms the plan to remove the school card "by 2012".
At Normale Sup, experts have 15 minutes to convince about the climate
Fifteen minutes to talk about the stakes of the Copenhagen summit: this is the challenge that Jean Jouzel, Vice-President of the Intergovernmental Group of Experts on Climate Evolution (IPCC) invited by the Ecole Normale Supérieure de la rue d'Ulm, took on Saturday morning.
Jean Jouzel, also Nobel Peace Prize winner, inaugurated the premiere of "Ernest" conferences, a day consisting of a series of expert interventions of fifteen minutes each, filmed and open to the public, said an AFP journalist.
We wanted to do something that catches people and makes them want to know more, explained on the sidelines of the Edouard Hannezo conference, 22, one of the organizers of this day who takes his name from that given by the students to the fish of the school basin.
We choose about twenty speakers from among the experts of their discipline and we select them for their charismatic side, he said.
Regarding global warming, "we can talk about the hours, but we can also talk about the key objectives in a few minutes," Jean Jouzel told AFP, who spoke without notes, but with the help of graphs, in front of about fifty people.
This is part of our role as a scientist, he stressed, hoping that listeners will remember that "the Copenhagen conference is based on an extremely serious scientific issue".
Videos from these lectures will be uploaded to the school's site from December 15.
The next day "Ernest" is scheduled in February, followed by others at a bimonthly pace.
Delay payments: anger of prison guards, planned actions
Prison Supervisor’s Unions on Friday called for “general mobilization” to demand the payment by the end-of-year holidays of premiums and overtimes whose payment has been postponed to January for budgetary reasons.
FO-penitentiary (2nd union of supervisors) asked staff who will not be on duty to demonstrate Tuesday morning in front of prisons and to "block them", to "denounce the theft of their dues".
The CGT-penitentiary (3rd trade union of the profession) calls them to "meet in general assemblies in the various prisons for a strong mobilization", judging "shameful and unacceptable" the non-payment in December of "overtime, night rewards, Sundays and public holidays" worked in October.
And the first union of supervisors, the Autonomous Federal Prison Union (Ufap/Unsa), "calls for the general mobilization (...) to raise discontent and anger".
The Directorate of Prison Administration and the Ministry of Justice explain that overtime has increased significantly in 2009 and have not all been budgeted, but ensure that residues will be paid in January.
La Chancellerie clarified at the end of the day that Michèle Alliot-Marie had sent a letter to unions of supervisors guaranteeing that this payment would be made next month.
“I am aware of the efforts made by staff for the operation and modernization of our prison system,” the Minister of Justice wrote.
I have asked the management of the prison administration to put in place without delay a set of measures aimed at making this situation resulting from a poor assessment of the number of overtime hours does not happen again in the future, adds Ms. Alliot-Marie.
The payment of December will therefore be meager, except for management staff benefiting from the famous premiums of functions and objectives, deplored the Ufap/Unsa, for whom "the budgetary constraints have good backs".
It is a pure scandal, indignated the interregional union FO of Marseille, announcing its decision to boycott until further notice "all meetings and convocations".
Staff won't come to work on their Sundays and public holidays if those of October are not regularized in December, same for overtime, also threatens FO Rhône-Alpes-Auvergne.
At a time when we increase our tasks and missions without increasing our workforce, it is more than regrettable to dare to attack the denaries of staff, believes the CGT-penitentiary of Bordeaux.
According to the unions, the situation is not the same in all regions, some of which are less misplaced than others.
But in any case the account is not there, noted the secretary general of Ufap, Jean-François Forget.
For Christmas, Obama says he offers "nicer stuff" than the ones he receives
U.S. President Barack Obama, who is about to spend his first family Christmas in the White House, told the "papess" of the small screen Oprah Winfrey that he had a simple rule when it comes to gifts: "I'm giving things cooler than those I receive."
As part of a short that will air Sunday night on ABC, the president and his wife Michelle offer an overview of how they intend to celebrate the end-of-year celebrations in their neighboring and prestigious home.
Even Bo, the family's four-legged little companion will be in the game.
Santa Claus also loves Bo, said Michelle Obama, adding that the dog would "of course" have a gift, according to excerpts from the show broadcast in advance.
And Michelle Bamba signed up against her husband’s assertions about the quality of the gifts he receives: “Certainly don’t, I gave you some cool gifts last year,” the president said.
Absolutely, was he forced to concede before humorously replicating by pointing to the First Lady's pearl necklace: "Who gave you that?".
During this one-hour show, with the casual atmosphere, with the famous facilitator who was a strong supporter of Barack Obama during the presidential campaign, the president and his wife also remembered their favorite gifts as children.
Barack Obama spoke about a ten-speed bike, but also a basketball that his Kenyan father that he rarely saw offered him at Christmas.
“I remember that time I met my dad coming to see us for Christmas and he gave me a basketball,” the president said.
It wasn't until much later in my life that I realized that it was he who gave me this balloon.
The First Lady remembered her side of her dollhouse.
“I really didn’t know how to organize a house so I had lined up all the furniture against the wall, rather than arranging it around the fireplace, but I loved this little dollhouse,” Michelle Obama said.
Johnny Hallyday reoperated, RTL claims he's in an artificial coma
Johnny Hallyday has been reoperated in Los Angeles due to significant "injuries" after an operation in France, but the situation is "under control" according to his entourage, while RTL claims that the singer has been placed in an artificial coma to avoid pain and complications.
The 66-year-old rocker was reoperated in the night from Wednesday to Thursday at Cedars-Sinai Hospital in Los Angeles after U.S. doctors found "lessions resulting from the operation" of the herniated discal suffered in Paris on November 26, his press service said in Paris, adding that these lesions had "necessitated a restorative operation".
However, the singer's press service was reassuring that the situation was "under control", while announcing that a new medical bulletin would be communicated at the latest within 48 hours.
According to RTL, partner of the singer’s latest tour, Johnny Hallyday was plunged into an artificial coma to avoid “pain and complications.”
It's only in 48 hours that we'll have a little more certainty about the patient's condition when they've opened an eye, RTL adds.
Asked by AFP, the press service has neither confirmed nor commented on the information.
RTL also claims that "according to some sources" no drain was laid after the first operation in Paris.
The rocker had been operated on at the Parc Monceau International Clinic for this herniated disc.
The intervention, scheduled for several days, had been practiced by the neurosurgeon Stéphane Delajoux , a very well-knowned doctor in the artistic community.
It wasn't available Thursday night to comment on RTL information.
As a result of the operation he performed on Johnny Hallyday, the singer was hospitalized Monday at Cedars-Sinai Hospital in Los Angeles, for an infection following the intervention.
Singer Eddy Mitchell, a relative of Johnny Hallyday, told RTL on Thursday that his friend was going through "a bad thing", but said he had "no worries" about the rocker, who is "a fighter".
He had called me just before leaving for Los Angeles, and I told him it was bullshit because flying twelve hours after such an operation, it's not playable," Mitchell said.
On Wednesday night, the singer's producer, Jean-Claude Camus, was reassuring, saying that Johnny Hallyday "reacted satisfactorily to antibiotics" and that the infection was "contained".
Mr. Camus had also said that he spoke with Laeticia, the singer's wife, and did not notice any particular concern.
But the singer’s health problems have only multiplied since July when he was hospitalized for nine days following a fall on his yacht.
Two months later, to everyone's surprise, Johnny Hallyday had revealed having had surgery on "little colon cancer", then a slight infection.
At the end of September, the singer resumed his long "Tour 66", presented as his farewell tour.
But since then, every health problem has been widely commented. Fans and journalists have noticed the difficulties of the singer moving on stage.
In mid-November, his entourage even had to deny another hospitalization.
Euro-2009/small basin: Bousquet's first on its fetish distance
The French Frédérick Bousquet, three-time medallist at the Worlds-2009 in the large pool, won an international title Thursday on a distance that he particularly likes, the 50 m freestyle, during the Euro-2009 in a small basin in Istanbul.
The first day of this competition where the combinations are allowed for the last time saw only three world records broken while we were waiting for a surge.
One has been upgraded by Hungary's Evelyn Verraszto on 200 m 4 swimmes and the other twims by Russia in men's 4x50 m 4-swim relay.
A regret for Bousquet, who looked at the world record of the 50 m free, of which he holds the brand in large basin (20.94).
On one of the two queen distances of swimming, the 50 m free is the 28-year-old Frenchman who won in 20 sec 53 in a final where ten swimmers took the start against eight usually, a first at the international level.
Bousquet outpaced Croatian Duje Draganja (20.70) and Russian Sergey Fesikov (20.84) but failed to lower the world mark (20.30) as he had envisioned.
We can always dream better and in this case, I had said that if the world record was within my reach, I was going to try it.
Like Bousquet, almost all swimmers were dressed in polyurethane suits, which will be banned from January 1st.
At the origin of 238 world records since February 2008, these suits end their epic in Istanbul, which had hosted the first in 1999, during the same competition.
The use of the suits did not serve this time the German Paul Biedermann, two-time world champion in the big basin this summer in Rome, world records at the key.
The young swimmer did not manage to lower the mark he holds on 400 m freestyle (3:32.77) but nevertheless won by signing the second fastest time of his career (3:34.55), ahead of Russia’s Nikita Lobintsev (3:35.75) and Denmark’s Mads Glaesner (3:36.82).
I didn’t give it my all about this race.
I knew I couldn't this time break a world record but I managed to defend my title and accomplished my mission.
Lobintsev will be a serious rival in the future, Biedermann said.
USA: a sarcophagus panel that belonged to Zola awarded 1.5 million USD
A roman sarcophagus panel that belonged in particular to the French writer Emile Zola was awarded on Thursday 1.5 million dollars, clearly above its estimate, announced the art dealer Sotheby's.
This front panel two meters long and 63 cm high, dating from the 3rd century A.D., was estimated between 150,000 and US$250,000.
The marble "representing four Dionysian scenes in a sophisticated architectural decor, surrounded by satyres and bacchantes, is a rare piece, there are only four or five in the world," Florent Heintz, vice-president of art auctions of Roman antiquity at Sotheby's told AFP.
It was sold among a hundred lots.
The sale is still ongoing but this lot is certainly the one on which the buyers have bid the most so far, told AFP a spokesman for Sotheby's, Dan Abernethy.
Emile Zola's discovery of the acquisition of this marble goes back a few days.
The panel remained for nearly 300 years in Rome in the famous collection of the Borghese family, then appeared successively by the French actress Cécile Sorel, who had made it an element of his bathtub in his Parisian mansion, and at Paul Reynaud, minister under the 3rd Republic in France and briefly president of the Council before the accession to power of Marshal Pétain.
Remained an area of shadow of about fifty years.
While searching the database of the Louvre Museum, Florent Heintz discovered that this panel was part of an "Emile Zola succession" opened in 1903, a year after the death of the author of "Thérèse Raquin" and the fresco of "Rougon-Macquart".
Attempted abortive departure from Zelaya from Honduras to Mexico
Mexico considered Thursday that the conditions were no longer met for the transfer to its territory of the ousted president of Honduras, Manuel Zelaya, who was denied a safe-conduct by the putschist government.
For the moment, after a phone conversation in the night with President Zelaya, everything seems to indicate that the possibilities of carrying out this transfer are not met, said Mexican Foreign Minister Patricia Espinosa.
Arrived Wednesday in El Salvador to bring Mr. Zelaya, the plane of the Mexican government went back to its place of origin, according to a source of the Directorate General of Migration.
The putschist government wanted to force Mr. Zelaya to "resign" and to seek political asylum abroad to grant him safe-conduct.
But the ousted president refused, because he wanted to travel to Mexico as a guest-appointed head of state.
The putschist government "has had another failure in wanting me to give up my office (...), they wanted me to resign," Zelaya told Globo radio on Thursday, on which he has often spoken since the five-month political crisis in the Central American.
I can stay here ten years, here I have my guitar, added by playing a few chords the ousted president, refugee at the Brazilian embassy in Tegucigalpa for two and a half months.
The Mexican embassy has applied to us for safe conduct, but unfortunately we cannot accept it because it does not contain an application for asylum, said Wednesday the interior minister of the putschist government, Oscar Raul Matute, on Honduran radio HRN.
“I am not seeking asylum in any country,” Manuel Zelaya said in an interview with Telesur, whose headquarters are in Caracas.
A possible departure from Honduras will have to be made as president of the Honduran people, he said.
Zelaya's rumors of Mr. Zelaya's departure mobilized supporters of the ousted president on Wednesday, who came to gather around the Brazilian embassy, where the military strengthened their siege.
Brazilian Foreign Minister Celso Amorim criticised the position of the putschist government.
This demonstrates the marginal nature of this government vis-à-vis international standards, he said.
Zelaya, who had moved closer to Venezuelan Socialist President Hugo Chavez, had been overthrown on June 28, on the day he held a popular consultation to prepare his re-election, against the opinion of the Supreme Court, the army and Congress.
Exiled to Costa Rica, he had returned by surprise on September 21 and had taken refuge in the Brazilian embassy.
If he goes out, he will be arrested to answer for the crime of "high treason" against him, according to the putschist government.
Putschist President Roberto Micheletti managed to hold a presidential election on November 29 to replace Mr Zelaya.
Porfirio Lobo was elected during the controversial election recognized by the United States but contested by Latin American countries such as Venezuela, Argentina or Brazil.
Three days later, Congress voted overwhelmingly against Zelaya's return to power until the end of his term on January 27.
Wall Street rises, U.S. trade balance fuels optimism
The New York Stock Exchange finished higher on Thursday, with U.S. trade balances testifying to the resumption of activity by both the United States and its partners: the Dow Jones gained 0.67% and the Nasdaq 0.33%.
According to final closing figures, the Dow Jones Industrial Average advanced 68.78 points to 10,405.83 points, and the technology-dominated Nasdaq by 7.13 points to 2,190.06 points.
The expanded Standard & Poor's 500 index won 0.58% (6.40 points) at 1,102.35 points.
We have good news coming from different parts of the economy, said Lindsey Piegza of FTN Financial.
But it remains anecdotal, she tempered, estimating that the market was focused on "thame-full glass".
According to Peter Cardillo of Avalon Partners, investors have mainly focused on U.S. trade balance figures, "which show that activity is improving in the fourth quarter."
The U.S. trade deficit fell in October by 7.6% compared to September to US$32.9 billion.
On the other hand, analysts were expecting a widening of the deficit.
Both imports and exports have increased.
The big news is the growth in trade volumes, particularly for goods exports, that reflects the growth of the economies of major U.S. trading partners, commented Christopher Cornell of research firm Economy.com.
On the other hand, new unemployment listings in the U.S. increased against all odds last week, reaching 474,000 applications.
But over the past four weeks, the average number of new job seekers "has fallen to the lowest level in a year," said Scott Marcouiller of Wells Fargo Advisors.
The bond market has fallen.
The 10-year Treasury yield rose to 3.482% from 3.423% on Tuesday night and the 30-year bond yield at 4.492% from 4.408% the day before.
Cuba: Hundreds of supporters of power conspire "Ladies in white"
A few hundred supporters of Cuba's power on Thursday conspired spouses of political detainees gathered in Havana for World Human Rights Day, and prevented another demonstration for which a dozen dissidents were arrested.
About forty "Ladies in White", wives or relatives of political prisoners, have been conspired in the street by supporters of Raul Castro's government as they march towards the center of the capital to demand the release of political prisoners, an AFP journalist said.
Down the Yankees!, "mercenaries!", "the street belongs to the revolutionaries!", "Vive Fidel and Raul!" chanted, with their fist lifted, the supporters of power to the dissidents.
They were escorted by plainclothes agents who came to "ensure their protection".
The day before, a similar demonstration had taken place against the "Ladies in White" in Havana, without any injuries or arrests.
But in the Havana neighborhood of Vedado, a dozen dissidents who were also preparing to demonstrate Thursday for International Human Rights Day were arrested by plainclothes polices, an AFP journalist said.
The police forcibly forciblized dissidents who were conspired by government supporters, the journalist said.
The arrests of dissidents under similar circumstances usually last only a few hours.
Recipients of the 2005 Sakharov Prize of the European Parliament, the "Ladies in White" had organized an unauthorized march to "demand the release of all political prisoners", 200 according to dissent, said one of their leaders, Laura Pollan, whose husband Hector Maceda is serving a 20-year prison sentence.
Mr. Maceda is part of the group of 75 Cuban dissidents arrested in March 2003, 53 of whom are locked up.
The Cuban authorities regard opponents as "mercenaries" or "agents" in the pay of their declared enemy, the United States.
Rarely, Cuban television reported on Thursday’s protest of pro-govermental activists against “counter-revolutionaries.”
Cuba, led by a one-party, the Communist Party, is regularly accused by European or American NGOs of flouting rights and freedoms, including expression.
A mad man kills an octogenarian with a knife and leaves 5 injured in Clichy-la-Garenne
A forged man armed with two knives killed an octogenarian and injured five others in downtown Clichy-la-Garenne (Hauts-de-Seine), an hour of influency, Thursday at the end of the day.
According to the first elements of the investigation, the 28-year-old attacker struck with two knives and for undetermined reasons, an octogenarian, in or in front of a pharmacy at 85 boulevard Jean-Jaurès, according to police sources and witnesses.
Seriously injured, the old man, born in 1929 according to an official of the police union Alliance, died in the hospital as a result of his injuries.
In or in front of this same pharmacy, he then attacked a couple, whose wife was pregnant.
The husband, in his 30s, intervened and was injured.
The first two victims, seriously injured, were admitted to the hospital where the octogenarian succumbed.
The days of the other wounded are in danger, with vital organs apparently being affected, according to police sources.
The pregnant woman then made discomfort and was reportedly hospitalized "in shock", according to a police source.
The armed man, who seemed under the influence of alcohol or drugs, according to a police source, then escaped into the street, always boulevard Jean Jaurès.
He would have injured two passers-by, apparently at random: a thirty-year-old and a forty-year-year-old, the latter seriously.
The scenario was still confusing in the evening.
He was armed with two butcher knives that he carried on the cross behind his back, explained on the spot an official of the Alliance union in Hauts-de-Seine, Arnaud Pressé.
Several passers-by, including young people, according to testimonies collected by AFP, tried to subdue the infancy.
I've seen a guy in my thirties with a green jacket.
Three kids tried to intercept it.
They didn't make it because the guy was strong, reported Alberto, waiter of a nearby bar.
A crowd fell on him, including a municipal police officer who broke his wrist, he added.
Still boulevard Jean-Jaurès, at the height of n°61, two municipal police officers, including their official, tried to arrest the armed man and were wounded with punches, according to police sources.
The events occurred in one of Clichy-la-Garenne's main arteries, where there are many shops.
Passers-by came to tell me that there was an injured person outside.
I went out, I saw a 70-year-old person on the ground, I put her in a safe position, and I saw a very important wound in the abdomen, Frédéric Allione, a 35-year-old pharmacist, told AFP.
Five minutes later arrived a second person in his 30s, wounded with stabbing.
It is the staff of the national police who arrested the man without identity documents.
The public prosecutor's office of Nanterre seized the investigation of the departmental judicial police service of Hauts-de-Seine (SDPJ 92).
The madman was hospitalized Thursday night.
He had not yet been heard as part of his custody.
A police source did not rule out that he was interned in a psychiatric hospital.
According to another police source, it took six police officers to subdue him at the Clichy police station where he was taken.
He was "in a state of overexcitement" as "all the fraunts of this species whose forces tenfold under the effect of action."
Polanski's lawyer again calls for abandonment of lawsuits
French-Polish filmmaker Roman Polanski's lawyer once again called Thursday in Los Angeles (California, western United States), the abandonment of lawsuits against his client, currently under house arrest in Switzerland for a case of more than 30 years.
Chad Hummel, the director's lawyer for the "Pianist", denounced before three judges of the Court of Appeal of the Second District of California serious procedural errors at the time of the events, in 1977.
I am calling for the dropping of the charges here and now, Hummel said, before adding that the behavior of the judge in charge of the case at the time, Laurence Rittenband, "was cold in the back."
According to him, Judge Rittenband — who has died since -- allegedly discussed, before handing down his sentence, with a prosecutor who told him that Roman Polanski deserved prison — which is a serious procedural misconduct.
This discussion had been revealed in the documentary "Roman Polanski: wanted and desired" (Roman Polanski, wanted and desired), from the very mouth of the prosecutor in question, David Wells.
But Mr. Wells had finally said at the end of September to have "lied" to the author of the documentary.
In the event that the court refuses to drop the proceedings, Mr. Hummel called for a trial, which would allow him to present the evidence of his argument.
The accusation, for its part, reiterated the argument already developed in the first instance, namely that no abandonment of the charges could be seriously studied until Roman Polanski appeared before the court.
The 76-year-old filmmaker fled the United States in 1978, prior to his sentencing, for "illegal sexual relations" with a 13-year-old girl.
He's never set foot in the United States since.
Los Angeles County Assistant Attorney Phyllis Asayama said Roman Polanski "must be there to appear at the hearing."
In this case, do we want to send the message, not only (Roman Polanski) but also to other defendants, that it is possible to run away?
For her, the filmmaker "asks a favor from the court, while despising his authority".
One of the three judges -- who now have 90 days to make their decision -- also stressed "that there were other alternatives to flight."
Mr. Hummel was not the only one to call for the prosecution against Roman Polanski.
Samantha Geimer's lawyer, the victim, also demanded, once again, an end to the prosecution.
Recalling that her client can no longer stand to be harassed at every twist of the Polanski case, she added: "Nobody in this room can say that the procedure has been fair.
Thirty-two years is enough.”
Roman Polanski was arrested on September 26 in Switzerland on an U.S. arrest warrant.
After more than two months behind bars, he was placed under house arrest in his cottage in Gstaad, awaiting possible extradition.
Wall Street ends without direction, hampered by the rise of the dollar
The New York Stock Exchange ended without direction on Friday, in a market torn between better-than-expected indicators in the United States and the rise of the dollar: the Dow Jones gained 0.63% but the Nasdaq lost 0.03%.
According to the definitive closing figures, the Dow Jones Industrial Average rose 65.67 points to 10,471.50 points, while the technology-dominated Nasdaq dropped 0.55 points to 2,190.31 points.
The expanded Standard & Poor's 500 increased by 0.37% (4.06 points) to 1,106.41 points.
Honestly, the market is very quiet, said Mace Blicksilver of Marblehead Asset Management.
It remains in narrow margins, the volume of exchanges has become very low and I think this will remain the case until the end of the year.
The trend was supported by the release of better-than-expected economic indicators, which "have contributed to improving the market's opinion on the strength of the economic recovery," summarized analysts at Charles Schwab.
In the U.S., retail sales rose 1.3% in November for the second month in a row and much more than expected.
U.S. consumer confidence rose sharply in December to 73.4 compared to 67.4 in the previous month, according to a preliminary estimate from the University of Michigan index.
However, the indices have been hampered by a further increase of the dollar, the highest in two months against the euro, which "pushes down the sectors sensitive to the economic situation", explained Owen Fitzpatrick, of Deutsche Bank.
Technological values have thus lost ground.
The bond market has fallen.
The 10-year Treasury yield rose to 3.540% from 3.482% on Wednesday night and the 30-year bond yield at 4.497% from 4.492% the day before.
Miss Model election again in South Africa
After a monty trip across South Africa, the 112 Miss Monde candidates are about to cross the last straight line Saturday to realize their dream: to be elected the most beautiful woman in the world in front of a billion viewers.
During this great beauty mass organized for the second consecutive year in the vicinity of Johannesburg, five of them have already won their tickets for the semi-final.
Miss Japan Eruza Sasaki, Perla Beltran Acosta of Mexico, Mariatu Kargbo of Sierra Leone, Kaiane Aldorino of Gilbratar and Yanbing Ma of Canada have been elected Miss Sportsman or Miss top model during their African stay.
It has been a real whirlwind.
Visiting beautiful towns such as Port Elizabeth, Durban and Cape Town was extraordinary.
“I’ve seen a lot of things and had so many experiences!,” exclaims Miss Sierra Leone.
This competition, which takes place for the ninth time in South Africa, is streamed live Saturday from Midrand, north of Johannesburg, from 17H00 (15H00 GMT).
I couldn't have dreamed of a better country to restore my crown.
That experience was fascinating.
“I’m glad I’ve lived it twice,” said Miss Monde Ksenia Sukhinova in the heat of the southern summer.
For the young Russian, her second stay in South Africa was marked by many exchanges with the candidates and the rediscovery "of the breathtaking landscapes in this country".
After a month of galas, rehearsals, meetings with the press and safari, the Miss are now focusing on the competition.
Indienne Pooja Chopra hopes to follow in the footsteps of Priyanka Chopra, elected in 2000 and present in this election, as well as those of Aishwarya Rai in 1994, both of whom became Bollywood stars.
It's every woman's dream of being crowned the most beautiful in the world but it's not just appearance.
We’re the ambassadors of our countries and I want to go home with the crown, says Miss India with a broad smile.
According to the organizers, music and dance will punctuate this 59th edition.
Candidates will parade in traditional costume and of course in swimsuits.
The show will be hosted by a TV host, China’s Angela Chow, former South African beauty queen Michelle McLean and Miss Monde committee manager Steve Douglas.
South Africa has shown that it knows how to welcome the world with warmth and pride that it is difficult to match, says Julia Morley, president of Miss Monde.
Candidates like last year did a safari, visited Soweto’s famous township, but this time by bike, and tasted the local cuisine.
Exceptionally, they also met with South African President Jacob Zuma who shook their hands.
“Not common for a head of state,” notes Miss Sierra Leone.
Their most beautiful memory will undoubtedly remain the draw in Cape Town (south-west) for the World Cup of June 2010, a way for the country to promote the event a little more.
I've been with people I never thought I'd get close.
I will undoubtedly come back for the 2010 World Cup, Enthuses Miss Italy, Alice Taticchi.
Johnny Hallyday's news "is very positive," according to his son David
Johnny Hallyday's news "is very positive," his son David said Friday on his arrival at the Los Angeles airport where he had to go to the bedside of his father hospitalized for several days.
The news is very positive.
It's very strong, it's going very well," David Hallyday told the press at his exit from the plane that brought him from Paris shortlest after 13h00 local (21H00 GMT).
The singer's son, looking relaxed, assured that he would go "very quickly" to Cedars-Sinai Hospital in Los Angeles, where Johnny Hallyday was admitted Monday for an infection following the operation of a herniated disc on November 26 in Paris.
The singer was "placed in an artificial coma that night in Los Angeles for a reason of comfort, to prevent him from suffering and to be able to administer his treatment," the press service said.
Johnny Hallyday had already been placed in an artificial coma by doctors at Cedars-Sinai Hospital in Los Angeles, where he had been admitted Monday for an infection following the operation of a herniated disc on November 26 in Paris.
The singer had to be reoperated in the night from Wednesday to Thursday due to significant lesions resulting from this intervention.
His wife Laeticia went Friday morning to the French high school in Los Angeles where their daughter Jade is in school to attend a school show, an AFP photographer said.
The head of orthopaedic and traumatological surgery at the Pitié-Salpêtrière hospital, Yves Catoné, announced that he would go Monday to Los Angeles with the singer.
The producer violently implicated Dr. Stéphane Delajoux, the neurosurgeon who operated the singer on November 26 at the Clinique internationale du parc Monceau in Paris.
We were told it was a massacre.
Los Angeles surgeons said they were outraged, Camus said.
“I’ve heard of a possible legal process to come,” he said.
Jean-Claude Camus estimated that Johnny Hallyday "left much too quickly for Los Angeles, with the authorization of the Parisian surgeon".
“I got this doctor in person online who assured me that there was no problem with this trip and it stunned me,” he added.
Shortly before on RTL, he had indicated that the singer had apparently "left without drain, without anything", after his Parisian operation.
Dr Stéphane Delajoux is very well known in the artistic community.
He had operated on actress Marie Trintignant in Vilnius, a few days before her death, in a clinic in Neuilly-sur-Seine where he practiced at the time.
In 2007, he had also operated on actress Charlotte Gainsbourg to absorb a hematoma following a cerebral hemorrhage.
But he is also a doctor with a sulphurous reputation, sentenced several times by the judiciary, in medical liability cases, as well as for tax evasion and fraud.
Olivier Metzner, the lawyer of the Order of Doctors, accused him of being "an unscrupulous man" prosecuted "regularly" before the Council of the Order, which is "far free from being recommended".
After several days of silence, Dr Stéphane Delajoux spoke Friday through his lawyer, David Koubbi.
He indicated that the intervention had "perfectly gone well" and that post-operative examinations were "normal".
The operation "was not hemorrhagic and therefore did not necessitate the installation of a drain," he added.
In any case, the tour of the singer, whose health problems have multiplied since this summer, seems in brackets.
Obviously, a resumption of the tour on January 8 (in Amiens, editor’s note) as planned, it seems very short, said Mr. Camus.
The association UDF raises the tone and summons the New Centre to no longer use its acronym
The association UDF, controlled by the MoDem of François Bayrou, reacted Friday to the attempt of OPA of the president of the New Center, Hervé Morin, on the acronym of the former Giscardian party, by putting it on notice to no longer use it on any medium, under penalty of legal proceedings.
Hervé Morin publicly announced his intention to retrieve the acronym UDF, judging that François Bayrou did not deserve to defend his political legacy after reaching out to the left.
The Minister of Defense, whose party is a partner of the UMP, had also planned to confirm this strategy Saturday before the National Council of the NC.
In support of his approach, he received the support of MP Hervé de Charette (ex-UMP passed to the NC) who claims ownership of the UDF brand for having been the first to deposit it in 2004 at the National Institute of Industrial Property.
But Friday night, the office of the association UDF, controlled by the friends of François Bayrou who do not intend to be dispossessed, came out of his silence to launch a formal notice to the minister and the deputy, summoning them not to use the acronym and to pass on to them the ownership of the brand, under penalty of legal proceedings.
You have multiplied, in recent days, statements indicating that you were going to propose to your political movement to +resume +resumpt » the acronym UDF, writes the association in a letter signed by 19 of the 24 members of the bureau, including François Bayrou and Marielle de Sarnez du MoDem, as well as Senator Jean Arthuis of the Centrist Alliance.
You also thought it appropriate to forward on your militant material and on the guard page of your site, the name of this movement, the +New center+, of the slogan +the UDF of today+, also note the signatories of the mail.
As you do not know, you have no right to the use of this acronym, explains the office of the UDF, which gives notice to the NC to remove "immediately the mention of the name of the UDF of any medium as well as (its) website".
It also asks Hervé de Charette "to pass on the ownership of (the) trademark to its legitimate assigns".
If you didn't, the courts would be seized to enforce the law and the legitimate rights of UDF members, the signatories warn.
In their letter, they point out that the UDF "has been a full-fledged political movement since 1978" and recalled that Mr. Morin and his friends had "made the choice" to leave it in 2007 "to create a competing movement, as others had done in 2002, to create the UMP".
According to them, the New Center has no right to use the acronym UDF (Union for French Democracy).
They also recall that the last congress of the UDF voted in 2007 its membership of the MoDem for three years and the creation of an office to defend "material and moral interests" of the training founded by Valéry Giscard d'Estaing.
As for Hervé de Charette, continues the office, "when he registered the trademark", he was "no longer a member of the UDF for three years", but at the UMP.
He takes outrageously and fraudulently from the failure to deposit the acronym UDF to perform it on his behalf.
This indelicacy is turning into a crime, they denounce, before ironizing on the strategy of the CN: "It seems to us that in politics, as in life, it is more worthy to make a name for oneself than to try to get out of anonymity by fraudulently taking a name to which you are not entitled."
Liliane Bettencourt case: François-Marie Banier will be judged for abuse of weakness
The court of Nanterre decided Friday to try the artist François-Marie Banier in order to determine whether, as the daughter of the billionaire Liliane Bettencourt accuses him, he took advantage of a state of weakness of the octogenarian to spolish her for a part of his fortune.
The 62-year-old photographer, known for his photos of stars, will be tried on April 15 and 16, 2010 for abuse of weakness, an offence punishable by a maximum sentence of three years' imprisonment and 375,000 euros of fine.
This decision means that there will indeed be a trial in this case worthy of a saga on one of the wealthiest families in France, in which mother and daughter tear each other apart while holding the reins of the L'Oréal cosmetics empire.
But the 15th Correctional Chamber has also ordered an 87-year-old Liliane Bettencourt medical expertise should be conducted collectively by three physicians.
L'Oréal's heiress and main shareholder has always refused to undergo such an examination, at least not under the conditions proposed by the Nanterre prosecutor's office during the investigation.
Physicians designated Friday will have to submit a report before March 10, 2010, including whether Liliane Bettencourt may have been in a state of weakness and at what times.
I think she'll submit.
“I’m going to talk to him about it,” said Ms. Bettencourt’s lawyer Georges Kiejman at the end of the hearing.
For his part, Françoise Bettencourt-Meyers' lawyer, Olivier Metzner, welcomed the court's decision.
It's great news (...) The expertise will demonstrate that he (Mr. Banier) manipulated this elderly lady that we have to protect, he said.
For two years, Liliane Bettencourt's only daughter has instituted criminal proceedings against François-Marie Banier, a photographer and touch-to-one artist, whom she accuses of having taken advantage for years of her mother's weakness to obtain a billion euros in donations, in the form of cheques, life insurance contracts or master paintings.
For her part, Liliane Bettencourt has always refuted having been manipulated by the photographer, whom she considers a long-time friend.
While the court had to examine the admissibility of the action taken by Ms. Bettencourt-Meyers, the pleadings of the lawyers sometimes turned into great family unpacking on Friday.
For example, Liliane Bettencourt's lawyer reported three letters sent by her client (in 1999, 2003 and 2005) to her notary, in which she said she was wary of Françoise Bettencourt-Meyers' husband.
In the letter dated 2003, Liliane Bettencourt added, according to the lawyer: "Could you explain to my daughter that I wrote to Banier once, twice a day for fifteen years.
Françoise, I've never been able to talk to her since her marriage."
For her part, the Nanterre Public Prosecutor's Office again considered that the plaintiff was not admissible to act, all the more so since a guardianship judge in recent days refused to grant her request to open legal protection procedure for her mother.
Liliane Bettencourt "has full capacity to take legal action, she is not under protection and does not have a guardian.
No one else has to act in his place," said Marie-Chrsitine Daubigney, the public prosecutor's representative.
The court finally decided that it would rule once the trial took place on the admissibility of Ms. Bettencourt-Meyers.
Managerial revolution or end of economic intelligence?
If nothing changes in the governance of major French companies, our fight for the development of economic intelligence has been futile...
For more than a decade, the attitude of the management of large companies towards its employees, recalls the position of companies vis-à-vis consumers before the marketing revolution: it is a question of making employees adhere to "values" determined from above as it once was, to impose on consumers products designed by the technicians of the factory.
By putting the customer at the heart of the company, the marketing revolution has put the strategies of conquering the markets in place... although the authoritarian temptation to impose products disconnected from customer preference factors returns regularly!
A demobilization of employees can lead to deadly demoralization.
Suicides at France Telecom, PSA or Renault are only the emerged part of a much more general phenomenon that particularly affects executives.
This demobilization seriously compromises the development of all the intellectual functions of the company, including R&D and, even more, economic intelligence.
Indeed, EI "requires a proactive approach and a forward-looking vision" from EI "requires a commitment on the part of employees that authoritarian management recipes totally thwart.
This is why we call for a managerial revolution that would involve putting employees at the center of the company’s concerns in the same way as the customer.
Relative usefulness of internal opinion polls
Will internal opinion polls – such as the questionnaire submitted by Orange to its employees – encourage this revolution in managerial practices?
Unfortunately, I'm afraid it's not.
- First of all because I could see that, in the face of a new situation, any structure finally, after many detours, to do primarily... what it has always done.!
- Secondly, because authoritarism – one of the pathologies of power – grows proportionally to the importance of its own failures.
- Finally, because most leaders have a simplistic view of human behavior.
Coming from the great engineering schools, they have a mechanistic interpretation of psychology.
They adopt, out of intellectual comfort, the old behaviourist theory, reactivated recently by the American "neurosciences" widely disseminated, without their knowledge, by the institutes of study and the media.
Thus, by suggesting that it is possible to wield the "levers" (sic) of the commitment of executives, a recent survey of Ipsos published in Le Figaro.fr reinforces the leaders in a position of mechanistic governance, unsuitable for the world of today.
Identify and measure mobilization factors
On the contrary, the managerial revolution necessary for the proper functioning of modern companies presupposes to adopt a finalistic vision of human behavior.
It consists in integrating, in any reasoning or practice of governance, a fundamental observation: for employees, the commitment for the company is only possible if it appears clearly as a way to realize part of their dreams, their ambitions, their projects.
Currently, only studies stemming from Profitional Analysis adopt this conception of employee motivations.
These studies have shown that they judge their company based on a predetermined doxa that allows them, through their perceived image of the company, to evaluate what it offers them to carry out their life projects.
Thanks to the identification and measurement of these mobilization factors, company executives can control, predict and anticipate their "social performance" and return their management of human resources "to the place".
They thus create a field conducive to the concerted development of a robust and effective economic intelligence.
Oslo's Speech: The "Just War" Is Worth Praising the Right in Obama
The U.S. right hailed Barack Obama’s speech the day before in Oslo on Friday, satisfied that the 2009 Nobel Peace Prize defended the concept of “just war” against U.S. enemies.
The Conservatives, who embrocaded the award to Mr. Obama on October 9, this time applauded the presidential speech, such as Sarah Palin, a former vice-presidency candidate in the 2008 election.
“I liked what he said,” Palin, the Republican Party’s right-wing aegeria, told USA Today, adding that she herself addressed the theme of the “sometimes necessary” war in her memoirs that have just been published.
The war is of course the last thing in my opinion that the Americans want to have to do, but it is necessary.
We have to stop these terrorists all over there, she said.
Mr. Obama, who inherited the two wars of his predecessor George W. Bush in Iraq and Afghanistan, worked Thursday in Oslo to justify the use of force, a way to explain his decision to send an additional 30,000 troops to Afghanistan nine days earlier.
Another opponent of Mr. Obama, Newt Gingrich, felt that the president had done in Oslo "a very good job".
I found that the speech was really very good, said the former president of the House of Representatives, who particularly appreciated the mention of "the existence of evil in the world" in the presidential remarks.
In some ways, I found this discourse quite historical, Gingrich, questioned during the radio show "The Takeaway".
The former elected official, considered by some as a possible candidate for the Republican nomination for the 2012 presidential election, said he was thrilled to have seen "a left-wing president" remind members of the Nobel Committee "that they would not be free and would not be able to present a peace prize if recourse to force did not exist".
For Michael Gerson, former pen of George W. Bush turned chronicler at the Washington Post, Mr. Obama gave "a very American speech".
In addition to presenting himself as a “citizen of the world” as “he’s always doing,” “he’s spoken like an American president,” reminding Europeans “that they’re living in a security bubble they didn’t create and they’re not doing much to preserve it,” Gerson said.
Among the few on the right to be unconvinced, a Bush-era falcon, former UN ambassador John Bolton, called the speech “dulty, ampulated and uninspired” on the National Review Online website.
To the left of the president, Democratic Representative Dennis Kucinich was alarmed that Barack Obama was justifying the use of force.
When one envelops the war in the notion of justice, one can easily justify the massacre of innocents, he warned.
However, the centre-left press remained commendatory for the president.
The Los Angeles Times, while continuing to believe that the Nobel was awarded prematurely to Mr. Obama, called his address "superproduction" even for an orator known for his talent.
Oslo's discourse "should serve as a roadmap to guide international decisions against conflict, poverty and oppression," the Californian daily newspaper said.
Mr. Obama "uttered the necessary speech," the New York Times said, judging him "both dark and edifying."
Routiers: three marathons for an agreement that saves Christmas supplies
After three sessions of marathon negotiations, the unions of the road sector and TLF, the only employers' organization to have remained until the end, reached Friday evening an agreement that puts an end to the slogan of strike and the threat to the supplies of Christmas.
During the signing session Friday evening, the Secretary of State for Transport, Dominique Bussereau, "rejoiced" in this agreement, after several sessions of negotiations, begun Wednesday morning.
After a second night of discussions the negotiators had left in the early morning Friday, with the hope of quickly signing the agreement text at the end of the morning.
Even if three employers' organizations (FNTR, Unostra, OTRE) had slammed the door.
But it took even more than six hours of a meeting started at 11am for everyone to agree and that at 18h00 the text was signed.
The conflict "would have been very detrimental to companies" and "it was to be feared that it would come to disrupt the holiday season," Bussereau said.
The text predicts an increase in wages of 4% for the lowest wages (in local and regional road transport), and 2.9% for the highest (international transports).
In hourly rates, for low-wages, "we go from 8.72 euros to 9.06.
It's not much, but we're leaving from a very far distance, explained Patrice Clos (FO).
We're going to order our activists that there's no blockades, no refueling center blockades.
But we can always have some recalcitrants who are not happy, he added.
Philippe Grillot, president of TLF, welcomed "a good agreement", even if it was "difficult to concede the increases on high wages given the international competition".
According to him, the increases will be in the order of 170 euros monthly for low wages and from 100 to 120 euros for the highest.
It is the agreement of common sense and respect for employees, said Maxime Dumont (CFDT), while Thierry Douine (CFTC) hailed "a historic agreement".
Richard Jaubert (CGT) referred to "an acceptable compromise".
The agreement also provides for the revaluation of travel expenses by 3%, and the maintenance of general conditions of road transport in the first half of 2010, which will make it possible in particular to address the issue of a 13th month and a modernization of the collective agreement.
Mr Bussereau recalled that "a reduction of 100 million euros in employer's expenses" has been the subject of a government amendment to the amending finance bill for 2009, voted in the night from Thursday to Friday by the National Assembly.
The government will ask the Senate to vote it "in the same term".
The signing of TLF allows the agreement to apply initially to companies belonging to this organization, and secondly, to the entire profession, in case of extension by the Minister of Labour.
It will take place "as soon as possible," Bussereau said.
The opposition of employers' organizations can only delay the process.
The FNTR denounced a "dupe market", because the 100 million euros "had already been promised to the sector to offset part of the carbon tax".
The Secretary of State for Transport forced us to negotiate urgently, not at the conclusion of a shared analysis, but in order not to thwart turkey eaters of the next New Year's Eve, deplored the Unostra.
For Otre, the agreement is likely "to dig the grave of a very large number of SMEs in the sector in the next 12 months".
RER A: disturbances this weekend, trade unions dissatisfied with RATP's proposals
The disturbances on the RER A, France's largest urban public transit line, will continue during the weekend, while a meeting on Friday on the 2nd day of strike between unions and RATP management left them hungry about their request for a bonus.
Saturday and Sunday, for this important weekend of purchases before Christmas, there will only be trains between 10h00 and 20h00, at the rate of one train out of two on the central section (between La Défense and Nation) and one train out of four outside the central section.
The interconnection with the SNCF will be suspended at Nanterre Préfecture, which will force passengers to change trains.
Galeries Lafayette, which expects 200,000 customers every day, were delighted that "RATP is making efforts to serve department stores".
The RER A crosses from west to east the Parisian agglomeration.
The RATP section runs from Saint-Germain-en-Laye and Nanterre to Boissy-Saint-Léger and Marne-La-Vallée.
On Friday, about one in two trains circulated during rush hours, and almost no train in off-peak hours, as expected.
As Thursday, there was no hustle on the docks, the Franciliens having organized themselves.
Thus in the early morning, the resort of Saint-Germain-en-Laye (Yvelines) was much less crowded than usual, especially due to the absence of middle and high school students.
In Boissy-Saint-Léger (Val-de-Marne), Jack Nbakina, a 29-year-old engineer, explained that he stood up "an hour thirty earlier, to be sure to arrive on time".
Saint-Lazare station did not experience saturation, said the SNCF, which had planned to supervise agents and police.
RER A operated by the SNCF from or to Cergy-Le-Haut arrived or departed from Saint-Lazare station to reach the metros.
New general assemblies of strikers will be held Saturday and Sunday, which will examine the results, judged "meager" by the unions, of a meeting with the management Friday afternoon.
The RER Director proposes to negotiate a specific protocol for the RER A on all aspects, working time, career development etc.
This is not what drivers want, who claim a due premium, according to Thierry Garon (South).
The RATP even puts its premium proposals in brackets, the CGT was outraged, stressing that the management "was not ruined to solve a problem while among truckers and SNCF it's done".
A new meeting is scheduled on Monday for "a broader examination of the conditions of practice of the profession", the RATP said.
This also told the unions that the company "mocked the users of the weekend".
In the morning, some 150 drivers had invaded an establishment committee at headquarters in Paris.
It was then that the management, which until then refused any discussion during the strike, had suggested "for the sake of appeasement" the meeting.
The conflict threatens to enlarge to the RER B on Tuesday, after Unsa and Indépendants filed a notice.
The unions ask for a premium of 120 euros (Unsa) to 150 euros including 30 euros variable (CGT, FO, CFDT, South, Independents) and complain about the deterioration of working conditions on this line to a million daily travelers.
RER drivers, all in the second part of career, receive from 2,200 to 2,700 euros gross as well as 600 to 650 euros of premiums, for staggered schedules, Sundays and public holidays.
Turkish justice dissolves pro-Kurdish party, far-reaching decision
Turkey’s Constitutional Court on Friday denounced the dissolution of the country’s main pro-Kurdish party, a decision that was immediately followed by angry protests in the Kurdish community, in the south-east of the country and in Istanbul.
This decision could greatly complicate the task of the government, which has just reached out to the Kurdish community in an attempt to end 25 years of conflict.
The Party for a Democratic Society (DTP) has been dissolved because it has become a "home of activities prejudicial to the independence of the state and its indivisible unity," Court President Hasim Kiliç told reporters after four days of deliberations.
The decision was taken unanimously by the 11 judges, while a seven-vote-qualified majority was required to pronounce the dissolution, Kiliç said, adding that 37 of the party's executives, including President Ahmet Türk and MP Aysel Tugluk, are banned from political life for five years.
The judge also announced the waiver of parliamentary immunity enjoyed by Mr. Türk and Ms. Tugluk and the forfeiture of the party's assets.
The DTP has 21 seats in Parliament (out of 550).
Its leaders had warned before the verdict that MPs would leave Parliament rather than sitting as parliamentarians without a label.
The decision follows proceedings launched in 2007 by the prosecutor of the Court of Cassation Abdurrahman Yalçinkaya, who accuses the DTP of obeying the "directives" of the Kurdistan Workers' Party (PKK), considered as a terrorist organization by Ankara and many countries.
Many observers believe that the DTP is the legal political showcase of PKK rebels.
The party assures, however, that it has "no organic ties" with the PKK but refuses to describe it as a terrorist organization and calls on the government to negotiate with it.
The court’s verdict comes at a time when the government has just reached out to the Kurdish community by proposing a series of measures strengthening its rights, in an attempt to dry up the Kurds’ support for the PKK and end the conflict.
DTP leaders had said a dissolution could lead to an increase in tensions in south-eastern Anatolia, where numerous demonstrations against the detention conditions of PKK leader Abdullah Öcalan have degenerated into clashes with police in recent weeks.
As soon as justice was announced, a thousand demonstrators gathered in front of the DTP premises in Diyarbakir, the large city in the south-east populated majority of Kurds.
The police fired tear gazes and used water cannons, as protesters threw molotov cocktails or stones, with slingshots.
A hundred people also demonstrated in Istanbul.
After the verdict, Mr Türk claimed that the decision of the judiciary will "deeper despair".
Turky can’t solve this issue (Kurdish, editor’s note) by banning a party, he told reporters.
The government, formed by a party resulting from the Islamist movement, also deplored the decision.
In principle, we are opposed to the closure of parties.
“It doesn’t solve anything, Energy Minister Taner Yildiz said.
This decision is a total torpedo of the democratic openness launched by the government, commented political analyst Ahmet Insel.
We can expect, of course, a reaction from the PKK, he added.
On Thursday, the PKK claimed responsibility for an attack that had killed seven soldiers three days earlier in northern Turkey, one of the deadliest in recent months.
The first snow can fall
What Wolfgang Brauburger wants is a very harsh winter.
But the head of the clearing and sandblasting service (FES) specifies that it is not to annoy the citizens.
It's simply so that agents can test the technique of vehicles and learn how to master them well.
And according to meterologists, this may well be for this weekend.
More than 300 agents and 120 vehicles stand ready to tackle snow and ice.
The FES here follows a very elaborate plan.
Brauburger explains that as soon as it snows on the city of Frankfurt and temperatures drop below zero, large sandblasting vehicles cover 20 main routes (called routes A).
This includes high-traffic streets, the main arteries, such as Neue Weg and Hanauer Landstraße.
Also included are firefights, public transport routes, pedestrian areas and sloping streets such as the Atzelbergstraße.
FES agents start their service at three o’clock in the morning.
If necessary, the 1040 kilometers of roads must be salted with a road fondant that adheres well, in 3 hours of maximum time.
This is the reason why the FES service starts at 3am "so that the roads are cleared when the traffic of daily migrations begins".
Where it gets more difficult, it's when the snow starts to fall at 7h30 in the morning.
This poses problems, says the head of the clearing and sandblasting service of the roads "because no motorist is ready to let dirty vehicles pass".
Then, if it no longer snows, we take care of the access roads and service roads - like the Rat-Beil-Straße.
Secondary roads and side streets come last.
Mr. Brauburger is well aware that this still involves a number of complaints.
At five o'clock in the morning, the FES pedestrian team sets out to give priority to the areas deemed dangerous and the crossings.
As for cycle paths, ESF services generally cannot deal with them.
Brauburger said that there is a special service in Frankfurt which monitors the condition of bridges between 22hours and 4h30.
As the ice forms very quickly on these surfaces, salting vehicles occur as soon as the temperature drops below 2 degrees.
It also happens that the police are using this special service in case of need.
The Cradle of Dinosaurs
The Tawa hallae resembled the other carnivorous dinosaurs: this biped was about 2 meters long for 1.50m high, its hird limbs were powerful, its elongated skull was provided with sharp and curved teeth, its short arms ended with sickle claws and it had a long tail.
If this species had lived a few million years later, it wouldn't have had anything exceptional.
But, as Sertling Nesbitt of the University of Texas at Austin reports and colleagues in Thursday’s Science magazine, the Tawa Hallae is one of the earliest dinosaurs with these morphological characteristics.
This animal lived 215 million years ago in New Mexico, which at the time was part of the emerged land of the supercontinent, Pangea and stood towards the equator.
Sertling Nesbitt says that the discovery of the Tawa illuminates the evolution of the first dinosaurs.
Indeed, it informs us on the ties of kinship, the distribution and the characteristics of them.
It was in 2006 that Sertling Nesbitt embarked on excavations with other paleontologists at the Gohst Ranch site in northern New Mexico
Randall Irmis of the Utah Museum of Natural History and member of the excavation team says that they sometimes found a dozen bones in one day which is absolutely unique for a sedimentary layer dating from the Triassic.
Scientists soon realized that some of these bones belonged to a new species.
Excavations have uncovered the remains of five specimens.
A discovery that confirms the theories concerning the beginnings of the evolution of dinosaurs.
The researchers baptized him by the name of Tawa after the word of the Hopi Indians for the god of the sun.
For Sterling Nesbitt, this is a quite exceptional discovery: dinosaur fossils from the Triassic are extremely rare and are limited to a few fragments of incomplete bones.
Not all paleontology experts share this enthusiasm.
For example Martin Sander of the University of Bonn does not find that this discovery is particularly spectacular, he points out, however, that it makes it possible to confirm certain theories concerning the beginnings of the evolution of dinosaurs.
According to the current state of knowledge, dinosaurs appeared on Earth about 230 million years ago.
However, most of the oldest fossils are fragmented and incomplete, which makes it very difficult to classify the very first dinosaurs.
Among other things, the experts wonder at what time the saurians have divided themselves into three major groups of evolution: theropods (carnivores) which include the tyrannosaurus and velociraptor appeared later and from which the birds, sauropods, quadrupeds in the long neck, and those related to them and ornithischian herbivores of which are part the species such.
According to the studies of S. Nesbitt and his colleagues, the Tawa is one of the earliest theropods.
This species presents an interesting relationship of both primitive and developed characteristics, says S. Nesbitt.
The researchers found clues that suggests that the Tawa hallae possessed small aerial bags in its bones at the level of the skull, as well as the birds that are descendants of theropods.
These small air pockets facilitate the breathing of birds in relation to mammals.
It seems that in the Tawa hallae, the vertebrae were also partially filled with air.
According to Randall Irmis, the Tawa is one of the oldest theropods exhibiting small aerial bags in its bones.
As the related lineage of the immense sauropods also possessed aerial sacs and a light backbone, it can be thought that their common ancestor already had this beneficial innovation.
Three successive waves of migration to North America.
As the most primitive known dinosaurs lived in South America, the researchers concluded that the saurians had appeared there.
Consequently, the three great lines of evolution were divided and distributed from South America to the rest of the world, i.e. all of the lands that formed the supercontinent Pangaea.
This seems to be confirmed by the fact that the team of paleontologists also discovered the remains of two other theropods that aren’t particularly related.
The three species cannot descend from a common ancestor that would have migrated, advance the researchers.
Alan Turner of the Museum of Natural History in New York and co-author of Science Studies magazine says there would have been at least three waves of migration to North America.
There are, of course, some contradictions.
Martin Sander, a paleontologist in Bonn, says there is an even older species, silesaure, which lived in today's Poland and belongs to a group related to dinosaurs.
According to him, this could give an erroneous vision because there are in South America a large number of dinosaur fossils that have been preserved while almost none are found on other continents.
On the other hand, how did dinosaurs manage to become the dominant tetrapods of the secondary era when they came from a relatively rare collateral line of the reptiles branch, remains a mystery today.
At the time of the Triassic, dinosaurs shared their habitat with many other reptiles, such as the ancestors of today's crocodiles and with many other saurians.
Towards the end of the Trias the dinosaurs spread throughout the planet thus favoring the development of many new species while others, which were related to them, disappeared between the Trias and the Jurassic.
Such a success, however, is not eternal.
To explain this transformation, Martin Sander sees two hypotheses.
There may have been a mass extinction that dinosaurs survived randomly while other reptiles would have disappeared.
Alternatively, the dinosaurs were better adapted, so they were able to eliminate competition in the field.
In any case, such success is not forever.
150 million years later, the fall of a favorite meteorite blooming of a group of animals that had also developed during the Triassic and that since that time was vegetating in the shadows: they were the mammals.
Solidarity from Vienna to Sao Paulo
The list is long and there are signatories from all over the world.
There are professors from the University of Toronto, the University of Cambridge, the University of Vienna, or Berkeley in California.
There are those of lecturers from the University of Naples, Quebec, Edinburgh, New York, Sao Paulo, Berlin or Bremen.
J.W. Goethe Frankfurt University's casino police's evacuation is making waves all over the planet.
278 professors and teachers from J.W. Goethe University but also from other higher education institutions in Germany, Europe and North and South America express their solidarity with the protesting students of the Westend Campus.
In a statement written in German and English they condemn the intervention of the police requested by the university's steering committee.
Last week, Werner Müller-Estler University President evacuated buildings occupied by striking students and teachers after walls and works of art were smeared with graffiti.
Five students were injured during the evacuation.
The signatories consider that the degradations did not justify "such violent intervention by the police commando against students and teachers who had organized their own courses".
Evacuation represents an "unacceptable attack" on freedom of research and teaching.
Focusing attention exclusively on acts of vandalism only serves to "delegitimize and criminalize the protest" and tries to forget the reasons for the mobilization.
The University's Steering Committee has thus evaded debates that would be essential.
The lecturers demand that the criminal proceedings instituted be immediately suspended.
The majority of the occupants did not take part in the degradations.
Legitimation of protests
The young people of the Verdi union of the service sector and the German Confederation of Trade Unions (DGB) are in solidarity with students.
“Protests are only the legitimate expression of growing concern for our own future,” said Alexander Klein, regional youth secretary for the Verdi union.
As for the Minister of Sciences of the Land of Hesse Eva Kühne-Hörmann (CDU) , she took cause for the action taken by the President of the University and the police.
We thank the University President.
Any occupation and deterioration of the premises is totally unacceptable, she told the Wiesbaden Regional Parliament last Thursday during the television show Aktuelle Stunde.
We understand the meaning of the protests but do not accept vandalism, expressed CDU Member of the Regional Parliament Peter Beuth.
SPD opposition parliamentary groups, the Greens and the Left (die Linke) did not share this opinion.
CSU takes the pretext of a few painted walls to criminalize the protests, said Janine Wissler of the party La Gauche (Linke)
Instead of thinking about the arguments and demands of the students, we evacuate them.
The La Gauche party has tabled a motion that summons the Minister of the Interior to submit a report on the conditions of the intervention of the police.
The New “Silicon-Valley”
For a long time she had the reputation of a secret city, forbidden to Western visitors.
Zelenograd, one of the ten districts of Moscow, was under the Soviet regime a place to search for secret armament, under strict surveillance.
Today with its 216 000 inhabitants 37 km from the center of Moscow, the municipality has the reputation of being the Soviet “Silicon Valley”.
Important places of production and research in micro- and nanoelectronics are located there.
As early as September 2008 representatives of Zelenograd have created links with Riedberg, a place of Frankfurt sciences – with university institutes and companies of the Frankfurt Innovation Centre (FIZ).
Reciprocal meetings and last Wednesday representatives of Zelenograd and Riedberg ratified a “Protocol of Close Cooperation”.
The signatories were Deputy Mayor Juri Rosljak and Prefect Anatoly Smimov of Zelenograd for the Russian delegation, Edwin Schwarz Project Section Manager for Frankfurt.
In 2010 representatives of FIZ and SMEs, whose strong point is scientific research, will go to Zelenograd for a seminar.
Scientific Exchange
It is a sustainable scientific exchange.
But also economic relations.
It will allow FIZ companies to enter the expanding Russian market.
On the other hand, the Russian scientific companies wish to obtain contracts in Germany.
Zelenograd is home to famous Soviet institutes and research societies.
There is the Moscow Institute of Electrical Engineering, the major firms Sitronics and Rosssijskaja-Elektronika.
The municipality was founded in 1958 as a "planned socialist city" for armament research.
Zelenograd (literal translation “Green City”) takes its name from the wooded hills in the heart of which the research centers are located.
Project S21: a brake for Stuttgart
We cannot place the breadth of the Stuttgart 21 project high enough.
For his supporters it is nothing less than “new heart of Europe”.
S21, it’s much more than the burial of a railway station, more than a colossal construction project that will plunge the heart of the capital of Baden-Württemberg, into a desperate chaos of construction sites for a decade.
Stuttgart 21 was born from the spirit of the 80s.
Head-of-the-line stations, with lanes that ended in the middle of the city, had the reputation at the time of being the relics of 19th-century transport technology that had the effect of a brake hoof of modern rail traffic because it once took a lot of time to pass the locomotives from the rear to the front.
Arrival, short stop, departure – that’s what you have to do.
That’s why head-to-end stations have to disappear: in Frankfurt and Munich.
And in Stuttgart too: a train station, which is about halfway on the Trans-European Rail Magistrate between Paris and Budapest / Bratislava hence the slogan "Heart of Europe".
The realization of project S21 will really begin, although the visions of the 80’s have not long been compatible with reality.
Even the most ambitious planners have acknowledged that the cost of burying the station in the respective capitals of Hesse and Bavaria was exorbitant.
In fact, head-of-the-line stations no longer really pose input and exit problems since there are modern, reversible trains with cars and pilot cars at both ends.
However, the realization of Stuttgart 21 is now launched next to the new route of the inter-city line towards Ulm.
Regional and communal policies have prevailed.
The railway company is the part because, it is the taxpayer who bears the cost of the risks of the largest infrastructure project of the Federal Republic and not the public enterprise.
From an urbanistic point of view, this project is relatively well justified.
We get an urban space that – according to the graphs of computer-generated planners – will be very green.
For the rail transport system the S21 is harmful.
The crossing station and the expansion of the section are designed for human trafficking to which we attributed – and this is typical of the 80’s – enormous growth.
At the time there was not yet competition from cheap flights.
Modern traffic policy should initially focus on tightening freight traffic because rail transport is much more environmentally friendly than by trucks on the road.
But the new route, with its inclinations, is not suitable for freight trains.
The S21, and this is much more serious concerns indirectly many other railway projects.
For example, six billion euros will be swallowed up in the Swabian project.
That is the official figure.
Experts believe that ten to fifteen billion are more realistic.
One thing is for sure, in the next decade this money will be lacking for other points of junction and engorgement in the network.
The list of the Ministry of Transport mentions a total of sixty major projects.
One of the most important is the widening of the section through the Rhine Valley by 180 good kilometers.
Since 1987 this line has been sloped.
It would be really urgent to press the mushroom thoroughly here.
But the S21 project has the effect of a braking shoe.
The route of the Rhine Valley is in the process of becoming the bottleneck of international cargo traffic, in the heart of Europe.
The government is considering the ruling on the airport
In the Wiesbaden Regional Parliament, the state government raised the possibility of appealing the judgment of the Administrative Court of Hesse concerning the prohibition of night air flights.
The Minister of the Economy Dieter Posch (FDP) has announced that the CDU/FDP coalition has tasked experts to examine from the legal point of view the judgment rendered by the Administrative Tribunal and which is a document comprising more than 400 pages.
The Kassel Court had justified in a written document that the Land should adapt the regulations concerning the prohibition of night flights in the event that the proposed construction of the north-west landing runway at Frankfurt airport would be carried out.
The authorization to land for 17 aircraft between 11 p.m. and 5 a.m., as envisioned by the state government, is not compatible with the legal protection of citizens against nocturnal air nuisances.
The coalition must have decided for January 4 if it wishes to make an appeal in cassation against the judgment rendered.
The opposition criticizes this delay and is trying to put pressure on the representative of the government so that he gives the appeal to the Federal Administrative Court in Leipzig.
The leader of the SPD parliamentary group Torsten Schäfer-Gümbel invites the CDU and the FDP to return to their promise made before the elections, which consisted in prohibiting night theft. Otherwise, this will be condidered as a total breach of a given word.
“It’s really difficult to be allowed to keep his word,” Green leader Tarek Al-Wazir said, referring to the FDP’s electoral slogan “We keep our promises.”
Minister Dieter Posch refutes Schäfer-Gümbel's reproaches, which claims that the Land government is still trying to allow night theft by using "avocastry and recoils".
It takes time to conduct an accurate legal review.
The coalition does not want to make a decision on the basis of a "fast first impression".
The decision must be legally reliable, says Stefan Müller (FDP).
The interpretation of the opposition with regard to its fraction's statements on the prohibition of night flights is completely erroneous.
U.S
The big American banks, very affected by the crisis, break their ties vis-à-vis the state.
The Bank of America announced on Wednesday that it had repaid 45 billion dollars (more than 30 billion Euros), corresponding to the aid granted as part of the Tarp bailout.
The U.S. television channel CNBC said during a report that Citigroup was also planning to repay the assistance received.
Banking institutions thus seek to get rid of the restrictions imposed, which accompanied the acceptance of sums, such as the limitation of bonuses allocated to managers as well as the payment of higher fees.
The Bank of America is launching a capital increase through the sale of securities to investors that has earned it 19 billion dollars in fresh money.
The rest of the sum comes from the bank's coffers, which are well-filled thanks to the sale of several branches of other sectors.
Last Wednesday evening, Kenneth Lewis the Chief Executive Officer of the Bank of America expressly thanked U.S. taxpayers: the injection of capital significantly helped to overcome a difficult period.
The banking had presummed its capabilities by acquiring investment bank Merrill Lynch at the height of financial turmoil.
The Citigroup is also planning to obtain money on the financial markets in order to free itself from the guardianship of the state.
The U.S. bank could thus launch as of Thursday a capital increase of 20 billion dollars, reported the CNBC chain citing the banking circles.
Citigroup’s spokesman did not wish to comment on this information.
In June the banks Goldman Sachs, JP Morgan Chase and Morgan Stanley had repaid the public funds they had benefited from.
Accelerated Election
In the end, everything happened faster than expected.
Barely two weeks for the dismissal of the current holder of the post of editor-in-chief Nikolaus Brender by the Board of Directors of the German chain ZDF, the successor is already appointed.
The committee wanted to vote last night to make the appointment.
Peter Frey, who manages ZDF’s Berlinese editorial office, was the big favorite.
However, the channel did not wish to confirm - officially - the vote so as not to anticipate on the procedure.
However, it seems that there has also been a desire not to further harm ZDF steward Markus Schächter in the event that the proposal is not accepted by the Board.
Frey, who is Schächter’s candidate, needed the vote of 9 out of 14.
The result of the vote was not yet known at the end of the drafting of this edition.
Two weeks ago Schächter proposed to the Board of Directors to extend Nikolaus Brender's contract by five years, but this had been rejected.
Only 7 members of the Council had declared themselves in favour of Brender's reappointment and 7 others had voted against. Brender's dismissal represented a stinking failure, although foreseeable for Schächter.
Indeed, the CDU, which is a majority on the Board of Directors, had already hinted several weeks before the vote, did not want to return Brender to its position.
Roland Koch, the Minister-President of the Land of Hesse, organized a league against the editor.
He had refused that the contract of the highly esteemed editor-in-chief would be extended by invoking the fact that ZDF's information broadcast listening rate had fallen under Brender.
This is the reason why protests were still taking place, almost until the vote, criticizing the growing political influence exerted on the ZDF.
At the end there were even 35 public law experts who had interfered in the debate and who believed that Koch's way of acting was unconstitutional.
But it won't have served anything.
Peter Frey: Who Is He?
Peter Frey was born August 4, 1957 in Bingen.
He studied political science, pedagogy and Romance (Spanish).
He then worked at the Südwestfunk (radio organ of Rhineland-Palatinate) and Frankfurter Rundschau (German daily newspaper of national scope).
In 1983, he became an editor of the ZDF, first of all to the news magazine "heute-journal" (journal of today).
Then his background continued: from 1988 to 1990 he was the assistant editor-in-chief of the time; then correspondent in Washington in 1991/992, then and until 1998 he was head and presenter of the "Morgenmagazins" (bi-monthly information program presented in the morning); until 2001 he was in charge of the foreign policy department of the ZDF then head of the Berlin editorial staff.
Following Brender's disgrace, when he had just been elected journalist of the year by the magazine Medium Magazin, the Greens announced that they would file a constitutional complaint
Koch’s opponent to the Board of Directors, Minister-President of Rhineland-Palatinate Kurt Beck, said last week that the Länders should transform the ZDF’s Intergovernmental Treaty so that steward’s staff proposals cannot be blocked by a partial political majority.
However, Beck does not intend to request the withdrawal of political figures who are members of the ZDF supervisory committees.
Peter Frey, Brender’s likely successor, had skillfully maneuvered with the Board of Directors by vehemently criticizing the commission in his decision to dismiss Brender.
Thanks to this proof of journalistic independence, Schächter was able to propose it for the position of editor-in-chief without losing face, just as the Board of Directors could elect him without being suspected of placing a political supporter in office.
In the arithmetic representations of the parties, Frey is ranked liberal on the left.
Great cleaning in university studies
Partial success for students: after one year of educational strike the Ministers of Education and Cultural Affairs of the Länder decided to be more conciliatory: during a meeting between the Ministers and the delegates of the Conference of Rectors, an agreement had been reached stating that the working conditions of Bachelor or Master students should be improved.
In the future, students should be able to complete their studies.
In particular, they agreed to lighten the weight of the examinations: a single examination must make it possible to close the chosen study module.
Moreover, the workload will have to be reconsidered to ensure "realistic and acceptable proportions".
Political authorities and higher education institutions wish to facilitate the recognition of examinations between universities and abroad.
The Länder have also committed themselves to organise more flexible common structural requirements for the Bachelor’s and Master’s sectors.
At the regional level, there must be no different regulations from one Land to another.
Less Examinations
Since the beginning of the student protests last June, the Länder Ministers of Education and rectors of universities have kept in their mouths the fashionable expression: "studiability".
But for the first time, this expression seems to take on a concrete meaning.
The Accreditation Board has decided to significantly reduce the number of certain examinations: "Each module should normally be able to be concluded by a single examination", according to a document of about thirty pages on the future conditions of admission to university cycles.
Reinhold Grimm, Chairman of the Accreditation Board, said that by clearly reducing the burden of examinations, it should be possible to stop skidding, which was significantly detrimental to studiability.
"Studiability" should, moreover, in the future be the decisive criterion for the organization of studies.
Modules as well as the background must be carefully examined to see if one cannot avoid this feeling of choking that we sometimes feel in the choice of courses.
Hamburg/Berlin - According to the latest RAID acquaintances on Kunduz, the opposition believes Defense Minister Karl-Theodor zu Guttenberg (CSU) is guilty of deliberate deception.
Students should "have the necessary opportunity and flexibility to enable and foster personal initiative".
The new regulations will concern access to the new university cycles but will also be adapted to existing cycles.
These cycles must be "reaccredited" every 5 years, which means, however, that it is only in 2015 that all cycles have been examined.
There are 10 competent accreditation agencies throughout the federal territory that depend on the accreditation councils created by the Länder.
In October, the Ministers of Education exerted some pressure on the Länders to weigh on the agencies so that the programmes were not too burdened and the examination regime not too strict.
In some Länder, universities and specialized colleges have already agreed with their regional ministries to take appropriate measures.
Until now, Rhineland-Palatinate is the only Land to have given its agreement to provide more resources for the development of the Bologna process.
With these funds, universities must organize tutorial programs and strengthen educational guidance offices as well as examination offices and the International Relations Department
The Länder and the universities provide for a budget of 10 million euros.
At the beginning of 2010, the government wishes to table in Parliament an amendment to the law on higher education institutions.
As a result, only one exam will have to pass his studies.
The organization of studies will have to be more flexible, in addition it must be able to guarantee the recognition of transcripts of other higher education institutions and access to the Master must be simplified.
By the end of next year, the "studiability" of all university cycles should have been examined.
Last Thursday, the government and the opposition once again debated the conditions of study in the country.
The decisions were appreciated, but there was criticism.
The Federal President of the Union of Socialist Students has indicated that he would hope that the organization of educational policy will no longer take place in the future "behanding the closed doors" of ministers and rectors of universities.
At the latest after the educational strike, students as concerned should be able to express themselves a little.
The future graduates of higher education have received the support of the President of Delegates of the Conference of Rectors (HRK) Margret Wintermantel: "the experience of students is very useful to us" she said Thursday during a meeting with the Ministers of Education of the Länder with whom the HRK is working on a joint reform project.
For the Union of Education and Sciences (GEW) the decisions made in Bonn do not go far enough. He calls for the law and the possibility for each Bachelor graduate to enroll in a Master's cycle.
Andreas Keller, member of the GEW’s Steering Committee, also asked that the Länder ensure today’s Bachelor’s students, that there will be plenty of places tomorrow for Master’s studies and as good study conditions as possible.
The Länder therefore have to hire more lecturers.
On the other hand, the Ministers of Education did not mention the possibility of easing access to the Master.
More than a footnote on Herta Müller
It is by these words “Arriving in Germany and it’s beautiful, almost more control...” that a work by the poet Werner Söllner begins, in which he thematizes in snippets of troubling sentences, the experiences of a dissident who arrived in Germany who gained freedom but at the same time lost his linguistic territory.
Werner Söllner, born in 1951 in Banat, Romania, was first a teacher of German and English in Bucharest and, later, director of collection at a publishing house for youth before moving to Frankfurt on the Main.
Söllner received various literary prizes; in 1993 he held the coveted chair of poetry at Goethe University in Frankfurt and since 2002 he has been director of the Hesse Literature Forum at Mousonturm’s artist’s house in Frankfurt.
He talked a lot about him in non-literarial circles last Tuesday, at a congress in Munich, whose theme was "German literature in Romania in the mirror and the distorting mirror of your Securitate files".
Visibly destabilized and embarrassed, Söllner made a statement in which he acknowledged before his fellow German-Romanian writers such as Richard Wagner, William Totok, Franz Hodjak, and Helmuth Frauendorfer whom he had collaborated as an informant for the Securitate political police.
Söllner was awarded the German Language Award in 1989 in conjunction with some of the participants.
Enrollment attempts began insidiously in 1971.
“I am,” concludes Söllner, “so someone who has not been able to sufficiently withstand the intimidation of the Securitate.”
The poet Werner Söllner is informator Walter (IM Walter).
Almost simultaneously with the honoring of the Nobel Prize for Herta Müller and his exemplary literary entanglements with the mechanisms of the power of the dictatorship, the literary scene is shaken and in particular the community of German authors from Romania, in a case that clearly shows the abyss and baseness of a political system, based on police surveillance and espionage.
Werner Söllner is, as far as we can say according to the current acquaintances, not only guilty but also a victim.
In his own Securitate file, IM Walter played a role, assures literature specialist Michael Markel after Söllner's confession and he refers to the positive influence of IM Walter who had interceded in his favour.
“He was able to pull him out of all the difficult situations at the time,” Markel said.
Markel felt the moral need to make that comment.
The form in which Söllner made his statement and in front of this audience was unexpected, however the admission of a former dissident of his collaboration with the Romanian secret services is in fact, not so surprising.
Germany is a pleasant reserve for Securitate spies had already written Herta Müller in July in the newspaper “Die Zeit”, after reading the 914 pages of his own file.
It can be assumed that the victims will have speculated and will sometimes have guessed what real names last hid the borrowing names Sorin, Voicu and also Walter.
Contrary to Germany where in the meantime the consultation of Stasi files is a reliable process, the attitude towards the legacy of the Securitate files is still very confusing.
A systematic clarification has been constantly thwarted and hindered in the past years.
It is only in 1999 that a national council corresponding to the Birthler institution was created to work on the assimilation of the archives of the Securitate (CNSAS) but the restitution of the files has rather dragged since.
Since 2005, there has been a law defining the management of archives.
That the influence of former members of the Securitate in Romania is still very great, is confirmed by the interview of a former official of the Securitate of Temesvar who, after the announcement of the presentation of the Nobel Prize to Herta Müller, expressed in a very dismissive way towards the laureate.
She had a psychosis, and in the end she had only been questioned once.
Infamy of power thought it could express itself here, without any genes, under the guise of freedom of expression.
Admittedly, many inescapable questions are associated with the Söllner case as happens so often in a context of hidden guilt.
Why now? Who has been plundered? Söllner’s weighted reaction, to Söllner’s admission, hopes that the rest of this chapter will be defined more by a thirst for knowledge than by accusations and a sense of shame.
On the genesis of Herta Müller’s work, who received the Nobel Prize yesterday, it’s more than a footnote.
Tumults in the Champions League
One had the impression that a representative was coming there, pulling four pins straight from a nice Christmas party, on which the sports goods manufacturer had spread his goods when the new coach VFB Christian Gross climbed at midnight minus a quarter in a Mercedes C-class, which he had just called, an orange plastic bag from the supplier by hand.
The man, whose bald skull attracted attention, allowed himself to be driven into the city that was still foreign to him.
Otherwise our fiftieth-year-old could have taken the wheel himself – he hadn’t drank alcohol and he wasn’t happy either.
He had a pleasant evening but tomorrow we had to return to work.
'Cause it's going to start now.
The words of the Swiss that we had just engaged three days earlier were clear: “We reached the eighth of the final”, that is to say in the league of champions thanks to our 3:1 against Unirea Urziceni of Romania “but we must not be blinded by success. It will be a brutal fight so as not to fall again.”
The "big job" awaits us Sunday.
There, it’s about rehearsinging to Mainz the results of the Champions League on a daily basis.
And it will be necessary to overcome the weaknesses that have resurfaced after the three expeditious goals of Marica (5.), Träsch (8.) and Pogrebnyak (11.)
Draught at random
The eighth final of the Champions League (match from 16./17. + 23./24 February/ return match 9./10. + 16./17. March) will be drawn on December 18 in Nyon.
The joy of the fulminant entry into office of Babbel’s successor was therefore moderate.
And the joy wasn't moderate only in the one who had brought luck, who had passed on to destabilized Stuttgart professionals, unwavering motivation and the pleasure of playing.
I’ve been to the players and I told them they had to focus on their strengths, I advised them to be more courageous, explained the “plumber of souls” – Gross – the recipe for success that sounded so well that didn’t make any more effects with Healer Babbel.
He sparked a certain magic, confessed Jens Lehmann - supporter of Babbel - "in football when there is a new impetus sometimes happens things that one would never have thought possible".
And Sami Khedira, who, after weeks of break for injury, advanced to the rank of the best player thanks to his dynamism, tried to describe the “phenomenon” observed by a proverbial “fresh wind” and the working method of the new leader: “He is someone who takes action and requires discipline.
That’s what we need in the current situation.”
But apparently Babbel lacked it.
The VfB Stuttgart has not yet passed, warns the manager Horst Heldt
For the time being, VfB Stuttgart especially needs tenacity.
Like Gross, the players and the manager are careful not to overestimate the victory against a totally overwhelmed and weakened defense of Romanian guests.
They had also won in Glasgow before having four days later a bad awakening in Leverkusen with 0:4 , Khedira objected.
Horst Heldt, the manager, pointed out that they had achieved one of the objectives of the season but that there were others who were still far away.
We haven't passed the difficult course yet.
That Heldt on his 40th birthday doesn’t have the heart to party, even after the first home win in three months, is due to the events of the weekend that a television interview with goalkeeper Jens Lehmann bounced back just before the start of the game.
There are decisions that others make and not Jens, Heldt thwarted, in response to Lehmann’s sharp criticism.
He had accused the direction of weakness of style and said that she had decided to change coaches under pressure from pubertary fans.
Lehmann had also judged as a sign of lack of sovereignty and jealousy meant that his day of free after the matches had been suppressed.
Lehmann’s statements were “marked by pure selfishness” buried Heldt and announced consequences for the goalkeeper.
They were no longer at risk of becoming friends for life these two forties.
In any cast, they agreed for everything else, i.e. without the group’s previous work by the coach replaced, the strong sporting and combative representation would not have been possible.
Babbel and his assistants were “for a big part in success” retorted Heldt and Lehmann in chorus – and even the man with a plastic sack agreed.
World Heavyweight Championship
Warm fight in Bern: Kevin Johnson the challenger provoked constantly verbally and gestually the world champion Vitali Klitschko.
But after twelve difficult rounds the Ukrainian defends his title of world heavyweight champion according to the WBC federation version.
Unanimously the judges imposed himself on points.
Hamburg – Vitali Klitschko retains his title of world heavyweight champion.
The WBC federation announced that the 38-year-old champion on Saturday night had earned a points victory at the unaminity of the judges against the U.S. Kevin Johnson (120:10).
For the third time Klitschko defends with honor his title since his comeback on stage in October 2008.
Previously the Ukrainian had already won the title of world champion of the WBO and WBC federations twice.
Unfortunately he did not pass the long-awaited KO.
Klitschko, who emerged 39 times victorious in 41 professional fights, had to confess twice his defeat due to injuries.
Challenger Johnson had to accept his first loss in the 24th fight.
In the Arena of Bern, in front of 17,000 spectators, the great Klitschko especially wanted to dictate the fight, from the height of its 2.02 meters, by its left.
Johnson, eleven centimeters smaller who, before the duel, had announced the end of the Klitschko era, managed to avoid the blows of the champion, thus preventing Klitschko to bring into play his dangerous against right.
Johnson, too passive in action for a challenger, tried especially to pass the rounds.
Although he continued to provoke Klitschko in words and gestures his blows very rarely reached the champion.
He is still the second boxer after Timo Hoffman in the year 2000, having exceeded with Klitschko the distance of the twelve rounds.
The challenger had made a lot of noise in the Swiss capital before the fight.
He insulted Klitschko calling him a “naughty zombie” who “isn’t really capable.”
During the weigh-in on Friday, the champion couldn’t help but provoke Johnson by dropping his sunglasses from his nose “to be able to see his eyes.”
Fourth victory since Klitschko's comeback.
Klitschko won again in his fourth fight since his comeback on October 11, 2008
After an almost four-year break, at the time he had beaten Nigerien Samuel Peter in Berlin and regained "his" WBC belt that he had previously given up without a fight.
This year, defending his title of champion, he won the fight against Juan Carlos Gomez of Cuba and in September against Chris Arreola of the USA.
“I just took a hit in this fight and am in perfect shape,” Klitschko said to explain his quick return to the ring.
His pass is to try to reunite in the family the four most important titles.
His brother Wladimir is champion of WBO and IBF federations.
But it is the British David Haye who, having detonated the great Nikolai Walujew - 2.13 meters - in November, wears the WBA belt.
This summer, Haye cancelled a match against Vitali Klitschko which was scheduled to take place in September.
It seems that his promotion company plans to play this match now at the Wembley Stadium in London.
The Kunduz case
Counter-to-face opposition attack: the leader of the parliamentary group of the Greens, Jürgen Trittin, accused Defense Minister Guttenberg of lying in the case of the Kunduz Affair.
The SPD and La Gauche (die Linke) parties ask the chancellor for explanations.
Hamburg/Berlin - After reviewing the latest information on the Kunduz air attack, the opposition criticized Defense Minister Karl-Theodor zu Guttenberg (CSU) for deliberatively disguissing certain facts.
The Minister has intentionally "formulated untruth" on the deadly strike of two tanker trucks last September, said the leader of the parliamentary group of the Greens, Jürgen Trittin, on Saturday during the broadcast of information of the channel ARD "Tagesschau" (the newspaper of 20h).
Clearly, that means he lied.
The order to kill was given in total disregard of the rules of the International Security Assistance Force in Afghanistan (ISAF).
It is, in essence, the content of the report available to NATO that has been read by Mr. Guttenberg, Trittin said.
Last Friday, according to new revelations from the media, the objective of lafrappe was in reality, not to destroy the stolen trucks, but was intended to eliminate two important Taliban leaders.
According to NATO's investigation report, 142 people had been killed or injured in the U.S. air attack launched at the request of German Colonel Goerg Klein, Kunduz Military Commander.
The Greens, the SPD and the Left asked Chancellor Angela Merkel (CDU) on Saturday, to speak to the Federal Parliament as soon as possible and to reveal what Guttenberg really knew about the incident.
Jürgen Trittin and his colleague from the Green Party Renate Künast are of the opinion that Angela Merkel must make it clear whether the targeted massacre strategy is part of Afghanistan’s federal policy if the chancellery, the army and the intelligence services have endorsed this new strategy.
The government spokesman categorically rejects these criticisms.
The chancery has in no way influenced the army's interventions in Afghanistan, he said.
On Saturday, there were more and more voices to express doubts about the real reasons for the dismissal of Inspector General Wolfgang Schneiderhan and Secretary of State Peter Wichert, as presented by the ministry.
According to information from the SPIEGEL newspaper, the information they had given to Guttenberg regarding the attack on tanker trucks was both correct and complete.
Officially, they would have concealed the existence of the NATO report and concealed from the Minister some embarrassing details.
Introducing the two senior officials, however, suggests that they have informed the Ministry of Defence of the existence of several reports, including a two-page document of Colonel Klein as well as a report from the military police.
The "Frankfurter Allgemeiner Sonntagszeitung" reports that Wichert had meanwhile requested in a letter a clarification of the facts.
So far, he has not received an answer
Saturday, the Ministry did not wish to comment on these charges and referred to the commission of inquiry of the Federal Parliament which would form the following Wednesday in order to shed light on the facts in question.
The Worlds of the Dream
Some see, all awake, images and hear voices that no one else sees or hears.
It is not only in the mentally ill, or under the effect of the drug, that the limits between imagination and reality are merged.
As long as these phenomena occur during sleep, everything is fine.
When we are lying in our darkened rooms, we can unhinderedly indulge in the wildest dreams and fantasies.
However he who, awake, experiences a dream state, hears and sees things that others do not perceive, quickly has the reputation in our Western world to be crazy or drugged, or at least to be an original.
Such images and voices do not only appear in psychial patients or under the effect of drugs.
It is our imagination: it is the same neural activity that allows us to conceive houses, paint paintings or write novels, but which sometimes encourages the limits between dreams, madness and reality to blend.
A state of conscious awakening, taking the form of a dream, can present itself in the most diverse forms, in the best of cases this is called visions, at the worst of madness.
But even if the trigger and effect stand out, the visions of the mystic Hildegard von Bingen, the drug experiments of the New Age prophet Carlos Castaneda, the second states of the shamans, psychoses and hallucinations are attributable to very similar processes.
There are hallucinations or delusions when balance, neurotransmitting substances in the brain – primarily dopamine, norepinephrine, serotonin and achetylcholine – ruptures and the interaction between different parts of the brain is disrupted.
Complex hallucinations and delusional thoughts very often occur during schizophrenic psychosis.
The Earth’s force of attraction has no more power over the person.
Since antiquity, philosophers and physicians have always studied the relationship between madness and dreams.
For Aristotle, hallucinations were a form of an awakened dream; Wilhelm Griesinger, one of the founders of modern psychiatry, observed in 1861 "a great resemblance between madness and the dream", for Sigmund Freud the dream was even "a psychosis with all the irregularities, imaginations, delusions and sensory illusions that compose it".
It’s autumn 2001 when Henning T turns into a cartoon character.
His body is in two dimensions with screaming colors.
The earthly attraction has no more power over it, it hovers at the edge of the ceiling.
He's feeling a hit.
Behind him winged, grimacing devil's creatures, bloody mouth, showing the teeth.
They pursue him, kick him with heavy flamboyant boots, throw him against the walls, he feels pain throughout the body.
Thunderous voices, wicked, scream, coming out of the walls and radio.
He can no longer support those voices who insult and humiliate him.
Henning T. pulls two cables from its stereo, makes it a loop and attaches it to a hook attached to the ceiling.
He climbs onto a baffle and passes the buckle around his neck.
He's jumping.
The buck breaks away.
Let it serve as a lesson for you, shout the voices in a choir to snatch the eardrum. "Go to a clinic and get treatment!"
Today Henning T. is 40 years old, he has experienced six acute psychoses, the last dated from 2 years, the first of almost 20 years.
It was triggered by LSD.
Diagnosis: schizo-affective psychosis, paranoid, in addition it is manic-depressive, it takes drugs against depression, mania, schizophrenia.
He's learned to live with his illness, he's been married for three years.
During psychosis I experienced terrible fears and deep despair.
Illusions are a cruel reality.
Approximately one percent of the population suffers at least once in their schizophrenia life; in psychiatric clinics this is the most common disease after depression.
In a schizophrenia crisis, the people concerned can no longer make the difference between the inner world and the outside world, they find themselves in a state of hyper-excitency, which make them suffer from hallucinations, megalomania, relational madness or persecution.
It’s very common to hear voices—voices that command, comment, or dialogue.
Optical hallucinations aren't uncommon either.
The person concerned can no longer make a distinction between the important and the insignificant, the brain can no longer filter the disruptive signals, the conscious is drowned under sensations.
Like the dream, psychosis opens the locks to a tide of ideas and fantasies from the deeper layers of consciousness.
The perception of reality in psychosis is centered on the subject, according to Professor Thomas Fuchs, Head of the Department of Psychiatry at Heidelberg University (Allgemeine Psychiatry im Universitätsklinikum).
Schizophrenics is located in the center of the situation, all to a meaning centered on it.
At the same time he finds himself in a passive role, he is like in a dream, overcome by events and cannot act on them actively.
This is why schizophrenics often feel threatened, controlled, manipulated.
In general they do not have the possibility of adjusting reality, illusions are a cruel reality, waking up is excluded.
Experience once in your life with a hallucination.
Unlike the dream, according to Fuchs, "the sensory and spatial structure of reality that is perceived during psychosis, is fundamentally preserved."
Delusional thoughts and hallucinations are based on reality, the alienation of the experience takes place in a real setting.
Why does our brains make this kind of cabrioles, is always an enigma for researchers.
Exceptional situations can lead everyone to momentarily lose the perception of reality, according to Fuchs.
However, only a small number of people will develop psychosis.
There appears to be a genetic predisposition to schizophrenia, but studies on twins have shown that genes are not the only determining factors.
The researchers assume that the people concerned have tiny brain damage.
It is assumed that neural maturation of the brain is disrupted at an early stage of development for example by a viral infection of the mother during pregnancy, Fuchs explains.
But what remains unexplained is how the anomaly of the neural system occurs and moreover the fact that it does not manifest for many years.
Environmental factors also play a role – stress or a traumatic experience can be a trigger for psychosis, if there is predisposition.
Similarly, hallucinogenic drugs can trigger psychoses because they act on neurotransmitters.
When our brain heats up.
What’s crazy, psychology professor Erich Kasten de Lübeck tells us, is that psychotics suffer from their hallucinations and take expensive drugs to get rid of them while others buy dear drugs to get them.
Pleasant or unpleasant perception of illusions is often just a matter of point of view.
Kasten has been bringing together reports on hallucinations and illusory image phenomena for ten years: last year he published his results in a book entitled "The Unreal World in Our Head".
Hallucinations are indeed part of the concomitant phenomena typical of psychoses but also of an impressive number of many other pathologies.
They include psychological disorders such as borderline or depression but also migraine, epilepsy, multiple sclerosis, Alzheimer’s disease or cranial trauma.
Surprisingly, under certain conditions even the brains of healthy people are prone to hallucinations.
The hallucinations, according to Kasten, consist of fragments of memories that are normally inhibited.
They take shape when the areas of the brain that process sensory perceptions, are hyperactive or not sufficiently refrenous; when our brain heats up but also when there is lack of stimulation.
Drugs can trigger this condition just like brain injury or psychosis.
But also the lack of stimulus, stress, heartbreak or other exceptional mental states.
In recent studies, one-third of participants admit to having experienced a hallucination at least once in their life.
In summer 2005 Sarah K. is 16 years old, she lives in a small town of Mecklenburg-Previous Pomerania and goes to high school.
A very normal girl to actually say.
If there weren’t images and voices.
During the recreation, it happens that a young boy, slim, with long chestnut hairs, speaks to him.
When she answers her comrades look at her, astonished – no one else sees or hears this boy.
They think this shy girl is a lunatic dreamer but Sarah K doesn't dream, she's sure.
She’s awake, hears and sees this boy just as she sees and hears the girl sitting next to her on the bench.
She can even perceive her presence momentarily.
It’s a pleasant feeling, she finds this young friend.
Am I crazy? she wonders sometimes.
But she knows well that this younger and the other visions aren’t real and that they will disappear again.
At night the apparitions are generally frightening.
Visions are often heavy.
When she’s in class, she can’t take the class because the teacher is just presenting a new student in her eyes and ears while he’s invisible to others and the teacher is visibly explaining equations on the board.
At night the apparitions are generally frightening.
When, in the darkness of her room, she waits for sleep that arrives too rarely and far too late, she often cannot make the distinction if she is awake or dreams already, if the images she sees, the grimacing faces or the girl with bloody notches on her arms and legs are in front of her eyes open or closed.
Leave me alone, she screams at these creatures.
She doesn’t talk about it to her parents or her friends, they’ll think she’s crazy.
Autumn 2009. Sarah K. is now 20 years old.
She graduated last year and she lives with her friend.
A year ago the hallucinations ceased.
Since she came into contact via the internet with psychologist Kasten she knows very well that she is not crazy.
Madness is also a work of art of desperation.
When someone is socially isolated or doesn’t have enough stimulations, there’s a high likelihood that they’ll have hallucinations after a while.
I guess the brain isn’t busy enough, makes sure there’s animation.
Sarah K., the only daughter, was afraid of other children when she was little and often played by herself.
Her hallucinations began when she learned at the age of 13 that the man she called dad, since she was old enough, wasn’t her real father.
These are the same cerebral areas that are active for visual and acoustic hallucinations, and for real impressions and noises, so it is very difficult for people like Sarah K to differentiate between illusion and reality. "Each experience", according to Kasten "takes place in the brain, reality is formed from there.
Hallucinations are also images that form in the brain, which is why they seem so real.”
For Kasten, nocturnal dreams are also a form of hallucination.
The hallucinations of healthy people do not differ fundamentally from those in acute psychoses.
Unlike schizophrenics, the mentally healthy person is able to recognize that fantasies aren’t real images.
That’s why Kasten advises his patients to cherish hallucinations as a special talent.
There’s only one step between dream and hallucination.
In our consciousness-focused world, unfortunately, we quickly tend to take imaginative experiences for pathologies, according to Michael Schmidt-Degenhard, professor at Heidelberg and chief physician of the psychiatry and psychotherapy clinic at the Florence Nightingale Hospital in Düsseldorf.
Psychosis is a serious disease, painful but it also contains something positive, creative - madness is a work of art of despair.
Schmidt-Degenhard studied the so-called oniroid form of experience, a particular form of the modification of consciousness awakened by the dream.
Oniroid experiences are very complex semi-dreams that are perceived as real by those who live them and that it cannot differentiate from the dream even when it is in a state of awakening.
The state of oniroid consciousness manifests itself during trauma or in situations of extreme pathologies such as for patients with polyradiculopathy who are fully conscious, experience motor paralysis.
According to Schmidt-Degenhard, when a human being is threatened by a loss of himself and the world, due to a total and lasting inability to move, he replaces this real situation that undermines his life, by his own imaginary world that is dissociated from reality.
It’s a state of mental boundaries between dream and hallucination.
As it is not possible to overcome the situation in reality, a change of world takes place but the real situation is represented in the imagination.
In fact, it is an intense form of means and interpretation, in an intolerable situation in itself, a capacity of organization that is given to us men, so as not to sink into the nothingness of the loss of consciousness.
It’s all real later in memories.
The Zürcher Peter Cornelius Classen, an art historian, also describes his oniroid experiences in his book "Changing of the Heart" as an action to safeguard the soul.
He’s 49 years old, at a time when his conscious leaves on a multi-day trip after the operation of a heart valve followed by a transplant; meanwhile, his body is doomed to stand still in resuscitation.
Claussen plunges into a world that he has created from scratch, visiting foreign places, traveling back in time. He sees himself as an old man in a railway sanatorium, a robot at his bedside watching over his health.
He lives in the Middle Ages among nobles who make blood-based narcotics.
He is abducted by a Greek on a motorcycle who takes him to his country, surfs with his hospital bed in the Alps and meets a Korean mobyss.
He completely absorbs himself into this imaginary world, everything is real even later in his memories.
According to Claussen, these inner journeys stand out fundamentally from every dream.
“Dream is quite a different state,” he writes, describing what he experienced.
Contrary to dreams, the memories coming from mental journeys are extremely clear and very detailed even years later.
It can even be said that they are much more distinct and more intensive than the real experience.
The intensity of this experience breaks the horizon of our other daily experiences, according to psychiatrist Schmidt-Degenhard.
Ultimately, oniroid delusions, psychoses, hallucinations and psychedelic experiences show us how fragile our inner representations of external reality are even in a waking state.
Any change in the state of consciousness, summarizes psychologist Kasten "can cause a deformity of what we call reality."
An experience that can also be quite enriching.
The first snow can fall
Burda
At the age of 27, the children will take over from the publishing house.
Kallen’s appointment as CEO of Burda editions is an intermediate solution: in the longer term, Hubert Burda wants his two children to take over the press group, he told Spiegel newspaper.
The holding company must remain 100% of a family business.
Hamburg - In the long term, publisher Hubert Burda sees his company in the hands of his children
The two children will inheritate at the age of 27 and are firmly committed to joining the company, Hubert Burda, 69, told the Spiegel.
All the details have been settled in advance.
The question of whether they will be active in the company will arise afterwards.
You will also have to see if they are capable.
You can't impose your children on 7500 employees without knowing if they have the ability to take care of them.
It would demolish the children as well as the company.
Burda said last Thursday that he was stepping down from the President and Chief Executive Officer in January and that his successor was Paul-Bernhard Kallen.
Kallen, 52, has been a member of the Board of Directors since 1999.
He was primarily in charge of the technology sector.
Subsequently he took care of the Treasury sector, i.e. he was responsible for the capital of the company and after Jürgen Todenhöfer's departure he also took the lead in marketing, the foreign sector, printing and finances.
Thus for the first time it is not a member of the family who is at the head of the Burda editions.
Burda's children are 19 and 17 years old respectively, his son Felix, who is now 42 years old, died a few years ago.
Kallen's appointment also serves to make the connection of the next 10 or 15 years"
Burda specifies that the holding company must remain 100% in the hands of the family.
The ruins have always taken place in the families that have allowed themselves to be dragged by a bulimia of capital, which have made huge unfortunate speculations thinking that they could climb ever higher.
That was never my strategy.
“I’ve never dreamed of universal power,” Burda said.
I absolutely don't see myself as the country's preceptor role as Springer could and I don't intend to be part of these international leagues like Time, these companies have taken on far too much importance.
Integral beauty
Everything that you can desire, in terms of beauty and well-being, at your fingertips.
This is the objective that El Corte Inglés, which has just inaugurated in the shopping center of Callao (Madrid), a multidisciplinary space of more than 1000 m2, devoted to the care and attention of the body.
The spa, located on the eighth floor, houses prestigious brands such as Hedonai, specialist in laser hair removal, Spaxion, with its relaxation rooms and aesthetic treatments, Luis & Tachi, hairdressers, Marina d’Or, which has a gym, or L’Occitane and its natural cosmetics, among others.
There is also a food department, with in particular a tea room, which offers more than 130 different kinds.
Spaxion offers you, among other things, to enjoy a relaxing massage with your companion, while contemplating the exceptional views of the capital, or to rent for two hours the relaxed area in order to organize your own “Beauty Party” with eight of your friends and to relax thanks to a session of jacuzzi, massages while drinking a glass of champagne.
In total, there are 250 m2 distributed in 12 rooms, where you can benefit from the most innovative facials, bodies and hydrotherapy.
Such as, for example, cavitation to combat cellulite or vibrating platforms.
The gym also has these devices, as well as virtual “trainers” who guide customers’ steps, offering them a fully personalized treatment.
It all starts with the introduction of your personal code on a touch screen, located in front of the device.
Several videos then show us how to properly carry out our exercise program.
Its director assures that 15 minutes (warm-up and stretching included), is enough to work all the muscles of the body.
And, if, after having made our body work, we want to go through the hairdressing salon, Luis & Tachi has a room where we propose rituals of hair repair, brightness or nutrition, all personalized.
Innovation and exclusivity seem to have been the watchwords of this urban spa, which will be open every day of the year from 10am to 10pm.
