Endesa and Barcelona have the electric car
The electricity company Endesa, in collaboration with the Ayuntamiento in Barcelona, has created the “Oficina LIVE”.
It is a project to develop and promote the electric vehicle in the Catalan capital.
The “Oficina LIVE” (Logistique pour l’Implantation du Véhicule Électrique) will define and coordinate the roadmap for the implementation of the electric car in the city and put in place agreements concerning mobility, carrying out procedures, coordinating, presenting subsidies, etc.
Moreover, it will develop the MOVELE project, promote the electric car, gather the opinion and work of the sectors involved.
Ultimately, the idea is to launch what will become the transportation of the future.
3 “electric cities”
But Barcelona is not the only city that will be involved in the installation of the electric car in Spain.
Madrid and Sevilla join the project.
These are the three cities chosen by the government, within the framework of the MOVELE Plan, in order to launch the implementation of the electric car.
The electricity company Endesa has already signed a framework agreement with the Generalitat of Catalonia.
Its objective is to promote research and technological development activities in the Autonomous Community of Catalonia.
These include activities related to energy, and in particular to energy efficiency.
This is not the only initiative Endesa participates in the promotion of the electric car.
The company participates in standardisation groups on a European scale and has in progress a set of complementary projects such as G4V, ELVIRE or CENIT VERDE.
Zapatero foresees “a future of tranquility” for the engine sector
The President of the government, José Luis Rodríguez Zapatero, foresees “a future of tranquility” for the Spanish automotive industry, which, he recalled, is the country’s main export sector.
During the presentation of the Economic Report of the President of the Government for 2009, Zapatero recalled the measures launched by the executive during the year to support the automotive sector.
“Without an Automotive Competitivity Plan, today we would have a landscape of abandonment, closure of some of our main production sites,” said the President of the government.
“Through this plan and the decision of brands to stay in Spain, in a very competitive sector, we can envision a future of tranquility for the future of this industry, which is our main export sector,” he added.
In this sense, he recalled that the Competitiveness Plan, included in the Integral Plan for the Automotive Sector (PIA), devoted 800 million euros to the projects of 19 manufacturers and 137 ancillary companies, which mobilized an investment of more than 3 000 million euros.
The chief executive explained that this investment was intended both for the development of new models and for the modernization of existing products and processes.
Zapatero has also focused on government demand support measures, referring to Plan 2000E, implemented in collaboration with the Autonomous Communities and automotive companies.
According to the President, this plan reduced the average emissions of new vehicles marketed on the Spanish market by 6% and contributed to a “strong increase” in registrations, which rose by “40%” in November.
20 000 m2 of classic cars
Madrid is home to the first edition of the International Exhibition of Collector’s Cars, a meeting space for professionals and enthusiasts of the world of the engine.
This initiative will take place from 5 to 7 February 2010 at the Pabellón de Cristal de la Casa de Campo de Madrid.
It is an exhibition area of 20 000 m2, where more than 200 national and international exhibitors will be present.
We will not only be able to find motorcycles and classic cars, but we will also find articles of all kinds related to the world of the engine.
Thus, among the exhibitors of the pavilion, we will be able to find accessories, spare parts, components, textile accessories, complements, magazines, miniatures, etc.
Hundreds of articles related to the world of the collectible engine that will delight collectors and the curious.
Participating companies include Clásicos de Mos and Good Old Times, car sales specialists, Juan Lumbreras, catering, Coupe-Francisco Pueche, auctor sales and catering, or Cochera Clásicos, a specialist at Mercedes Benz.
Outdoor aptitude test
But the commercial activity of this show will also include parallel activities: gatherings, sports exhibitions, aptitude tests, presentations and competitions.
Aptitude tests with classic cars and motorcycles will also take place on a converted circuit outside the exhibition centre.
Mazda3 i-Stop : A sustainable sporting spirit
Mazda intends to prove that sportsmanship is not angry with respect for the environment.
For this purpose, it has just integrated on the Mazda3 the engine shut-off and starting system, dubbed i-Stop, a device that, at least for the moment, is only available on the 150 horsepower gasoline 2.0 engine.
This second generation of Mazda3 is illustrated by the good rating it has obtained as a whole.
The details, the finishing of the vehicle, the habitability, the behavior, the mechanics, the i-Stop system...
All of these aspects are remarkable about the new Mazda3.
Starting with the driver’s station, it turns out that the front seats have excellent ergonomics and fasteners.
The touch of the steering wheel is also particularly pleasant.
Continuing with the set formed by the control panel and console, we see how Mazda continued in the Japanese trend to create a futuristic interior design, with many buttons and controls, as well as bright colors in red and blue.
This style reminds us, in part, that of the Honda Civic.
That’s why we can say that both the Mazda3 and the Civic, both Japanese, contrast with the sobriety of German products, and more particularly with Volkswagen’s Golf, which everyone wants to surpass.
Pursuing with the cabin, this compact Japanese car has more than sufficient livability to allow four adults to travel with a high level of comfort.
In addition to all this is a large chest with regular shapes.
The automatic engine shut-off and starting system, at least for now, is only available on the two-liter and 150-horsepower gasoline-powered engine with a six-speed manual change.
This engine is a real happiness. It is illustrated by its finesse, its low noise, its services and the mastery of its consumption.
Indeed, although the most requested Mazda3 are the diesel models, those who do not make a large number of kilometers per year should not exclude this i-Stop with gasoline engine, both for its comfort of speed and for its controlled consumption.
Regarding the level of equipment, the model chosen for the test is the Sportsman, i.e. the one with the largest number of standard elements.
Among standard equipment, Lane Change Assistant (RVM) that removes blind spot, traction and stability controls, cruise control, photosensitive interior mirror, large-scale on-screen edge computer, parking sensors, independent air conditioning, rain and light sensor, bluetooth and audio bluetooth, as well as 17-inch alignment wheels, are particularly noteworthy.
Moreover, as if this equipment were not enough, this version had the Premium Pack which, for 1 500 euros more, includes, among other elements, adaptable bi-xenon headlights and a BOSE audio system with disc loader.
Concluding
The Mazda3 2.0 i-Stop is one of the best compact cars in its class.
It is illustrated in particular by the quality of the materials used, its behavior and its high level of equipment.
In addition, those who do not make many kilometers per year will be able to opt for the i-Stop version associated only with the 150-horsepower 2.0 gasoline engine.
The only drawback of this model is its high price, which is between 21 770 euros for the Luxury finish and 23 270 euros for the model tested, the Sportive.
In return for this large sum, the Mazda3 will offer us every day a great dose of satisfaction.
