L.A. covers the red carpet of sensuality embodied by Penélope Cruz, Nicole Kidman and Fergie.
The stars have dazzled us more than ever, dressed in models as elegant as sexy for the release of Nine. (Photos: Gtresonline).
Our “Pe”, as sophisticated as usual, had opted for a dark “lady”.
FonPeek's Promise
Peek is extremely limited, it is a device that only allows you to look at your email, without offering the opportunity even to see certain types of attachments or browse the Internet.
He took advantage of his five minutes of glory in the United States, when Time magazine designated him as one of the products of the year.
In truth, it’s more difficult to see it on the street, but the idea seems interesting, especially for young people.
Unlike smartphones, Peek offers a very low price and monthly subscription, less than half of what it costs, for example, the iPhone’s data subscription.
Given its success, the company has launched two new devices: one designed exclusively for Twitter, the other with a free lifetime data service, for six times more expensive than the Peek.
FonPeek, presented yesterday at The Web event, is the European version of the invention and its third incarnation.
It is just as limited as his American older brother, but has a surprise.
The price of his monthly subscription, about 12 euros, includes roaming throughout Europe.
Those traveling with a smartphone already know what it means to give up the data when crossing a border or desperately looking for a cafeteria on the Wifi (or a connection of Fon) to be able to consult his mail.
The idea of roaming at a fixed price is incredibly seductive and it’s been working for a long time.
Indeed, some operators have special chords and travel options, but as far as I’m concerned, I would need to have done a thesis in physics to understand them and good glasses to read fine print.
The European Commission’s repeated calls to order for affordable roaming tariffs across Europe have had a barely perceptible effect.
Outside our country of origin, we hesitate to use the data, and we are not wrong.
That a small device like the FonPeek (it’s always curious that it’s called Fon when it doesn’t have Wi-Fi) offers this type of rates, means that operators are starting to think differently and studying different proposals allowing them to make their data network profitable.
Kindle is another good example, it includes uploading books and other internet searches.
No subscription and no galley.
It’s a connected device and you pay when you buy and not every month.
And that’s what I hope to see one day for the FonPeek: a purchase option with lifetime access as Peek already offers in the United States.
Not that I want to buy it.
I'll look at my mail on my phone, thanks.
I just want it because I think it’s a more just formula for the consumer.
It would be interesting to see a computer presenting the same system.
Michelle's Favorite Color
Yellow is, no doubt, the favorite color of the First Lady of the United States.
If she went to take office of Barack Obama in the presidency of the United States with a dress and a coat of this color, she has, in fact, chosen the same to accompany her in the second moment, without a doubt, the most important of her life: the presentation in Oslo of the Nobel Peace Prize.
Michelle Obama wore a very similar model on these two occasions: in addition to their resembling color, a yellow pulling towards the green with brilliant reflections, both times, the outfit consisted of three pieces: the dress, the jacket and the coat, in the same fabric, and was decorated with a large embroidery or a necklace.
So at a first glance, it seems that the First Lady of the United States follows the wake of the Princess of Asturias and has chosen to deliver the same outfits.
But the fabric in which the two sets are carved makes them very different.
If for taking office it was a thick lace, on the other hand, she wore a printed velvet in Oslo.
The accessories are also different: medium-heeled green shoes for the first opportunity, and a similar model, but golden, this time.
The hairstyle has also changed: the hair is now raised as she wore them released the previous time.
75% of working mothers have had professional problems because of their motherhood
Discrimination at work has increased in recent years, especially for women of the younger generations who want to be mothers.
This is revealed by the study “Fecondity and professional career of women in Spain”, carried out by CSIC in collaboration with the Instituto de la Mujer.
The survey, carried out on 9,737 women aged 15 to 75, leaves no room for doubt: 75% of working mothers have had their professional careers limited because of motherhood.
The study indicates that there are many differences in the onset of couple life and the number of children, whether or not the woman is working and the type of job occupied.
Those who do not have activity outside the home, work part-time or have unstable work, get into a relationship earlier, have their first pregnancy earlier and have more children.
On the other hand, women with stable employment, mainly in the public sector, are pushing back entry into couple life and first pregnancy, and have fewer children.
Women between the ages of 35 and 49 with fixed work have their first child, on average, 3.7 years after settling as a couple. This figure rises to 4.1 among those working in the public sector. “These data show that women with stable jobs needed more time in their lives to obtain it, which led them to postpone the union and the arrival of children.
In addition, the type of employment exercised largely influences motherhood, but less the date of the beginning of life together,” explains CSIC researcher Margarita Delgado.
The level of education is another factor that widens the gap in the age of marriage of women and their first child.
According to the survey, the higher the level of training, the more they get married and have their first child late.
There is also the difference among women of the same generation.
For example, in the group between the ages of 35 and 49, those with elementary and higher education were mothers for the first time at 25.1 and 32.1 on average.
“Another distinction also appears between women, depending on their level of education.
As those who have completed elementary education get married and have their first child before finding a stable job, those with an average or higher education reverse this program and give priority to obtaining stable work as a precondition for marriage and the arrival of a child,” says Delgado.
Diane Kruger is worth it too
L'Oréal Paris has just added a new name to its jealous list of ambassadors: Diane Kruger.
Starting next year, the German actress will embody the image of several products of the brand, singing the universal motto “Because I’m worth it”.
The protagonist of Merry Christmas, one of the most elegant actresses of the international scene, joins other celebrities registered with L’Oréal Paris, such as Linda Evangelista, Jane Fonda, Andie MacDowell, Eva Longoria and Spain’s Penelope Cruz.
Before her, the last to enter this select club were actresses Evangeline Lilly (Lost) and Freida Pinto (Slumdog Millonaire).
Among men, the cosmetics brand also has luxury ambassadors: Patrick Dempsey (Dre Grey, anatomy lessons) and Matthew Fox (Evangeline’s companion in Lost).
