The ace poker of Manuel Vincent
Manuel Vincent offers us in Poquer de ases (Alfaguara, illustrations by Fernando Vicente) about thirty "X-rays" of writers who have gone through his literary life (and sometimes his other life).
He does not do this according to the predictable and apprehended genres (biographical summary, life and work), but by looking for something similar to the ignition core of the character created.
The journey is exciting and Vincent does not spare fear or illuminations.
Delicious pleasure, this book is also a moving invitation to reading and proofreading.
I'll leave you here some snapshots.
“In its pages, I discovered that the Mediterranean was not a sea, but a spiritual impulse, almost physical, the very one I felt without naming it: pleasure against fate, morality without guilt and innocence without any god.”
On Samuel Beckett: “Nihiliste, an allegorical Christian, he wrote what he had in his blood, not in his head, between powerlessness and ignorance, with a dazzling, meaningless poetic humour, like the blade of the knife that almost killed him.”
On Graham Greene: “Our man was inspired by this woman of a passion that lasted thirteen years, in whose flesh he combined the emotion of adultery with the pleasure of remorse, a spiritual privilege that consisted of reaching heaven by the path of perdition.”
On James Joyce: “L’Ulysse was published in Paris in 1922 by Sylvia Beach. It is one of the eight thousand-metre peaks of universal literature, which must be climbed through the northern face, on which the best climbers still let go.”
On William Faulkner: “He was a strange guy.
For himself, he sometimes said that he was the heir of a county landowner and other times that he was the son of a black and a crocodile.
Both of those dreams were of greatness.”
On Louis-Ferdinand Céline: “This writer collected the demented cry pushed from a bridge by this character of Munch to bring his echo to the depths of the night of the 20th century, in his literary form, and he did it in the flight of himself and invoking posteriority with a violent discourse to the maggots.”
On Dorothy Parker: “One day she knelled and prayed, ‘Beloved God, I beg you to make me stop writing like a woman.’”
On Joseph Conrad: “On his tomb were carved the verses of Spenser: “Sleep after the effort, / after the torment the port, / the rest after the war, / after life, tired the death delights.”
On Virginia Wolf: “In her luggage she also carried her depressions.
Her husband accepted very normally that she would tell him that Edward VII was spying on him between the azaleas or that the birds were singing in Greek.
You’ve never seen a man so patient and in love with a neurotics whose literary talent went beyond madness.”
On Pío Baroja: “What did Martín-Santos do but try to hide Joyce from Baroja?
What did Benet try to shoot Baroja by Faulkner?
What did it do if it wasn't to aspire this man's glory so that he'd serve as his pedestal?"
Loose aggression
Last week, my mother, who is about 70 years old, was walking in our neighborhood of the city of Fuenlabrada, when a group of young people between 15 and 18 years old passed by her and our dog, a meled-blood, rescued from the SPA and weighing no more than six kilograms, and without saying a word, they kicked her who left the dog seriously injured.
Our indignation is immense because we do not see what is fun to attack for free a small defenseless animal which, moreover, was on a leash.
We must add to this the indignation of a lady who walked quietly and who, when she insulted these individuals, came to fear for her physical integrity, since an energumen that attacks an animal is capable of doing the same thing to a woman, a child, an older or, ultimately, anyone weaker than himself.
The episode took place around 5 p.m
The dog groaned all day.
Of course, of course, we have lodged a complaint with the police station of the district even if we doubt that it serves much and in order not to touch the sensibilities, we just want to clarify that the cowardly assassins were Spanish.
Indignation led us to denounce this fact.
We don’t understand what’s going on with young people so that they find it fun to mistreat for free a helpless animal that gives us affection, friendship and keeps us company.
We don’t know where the right education and respect for animals and people went.
Michelin and Fesvial, sets for wearing the helmet
The manufacturer of Michelin tires, in collaboration with the Spanish Foundation for Road Safety (Fesvial), has launched a campaign to encourage the wearing of helmets, mainly among the youngest motorcyclists, as indicated today by both structures.
The objective of this initiative is to raise awareness of the importance of wearing the helmet, since it is the only element of protection to avoid head injuries, or even death, in the event of a two-wheeled accident.
Michelin indicated that this campaign was in line with its commitment to road safety, which “goes beyond the design and manufacture of ever safer products” and affirmed that it contributes to this objective through other educational and informative actions.
This new campaign to encourage the wearing of the two-wheeled helmet, under the motto “To be or not to be”, will include a first phase during which posters will be distributed in more than 500 motorcycle garages, in order to guarantee the widest distribution.
Finally, Michelin stressed that road safety “is a constant concern” for the company, because as a manufacturer of tyres for two-wheeled vehicles, one of its main objectives is to develop ever safer products.
The sale of light commercial vehicles is increasing
After almost two years, the market for light commercial vehicles is starting to see some light at the end of the tunnel.
The 9,425 vehicles registered in November in Spain represent an increase of 12.6% compared to 2008 and, more importantly, they represent the first increase after 22 consecutive months of decline, according to figures from the trade union of manufacturers Anfac and Ganvam, that of dealers.
The good news should, however, be nuanced, since the benchmark used for the comparison in November 2008 was particularly bad, with a decrease of 60% compared to the previous year.
As a result, despite last month’s rebound, the registrations accumulated during these 11 months are 40% below those of the previous fiscal year.
Since January, if we add up all the vehicles that fall into the category of light commercial vehicles (derivatives of passenger cars, pick-ups, vans, light vans and light-chassis trucks) 95 853 have been sold.
The big clouds that most affect their demand have not dissipated, because although they are also bought by individuals, most purchases are made by small or medium-sized companies, which pay in almost 100% of cases thanks to external financing.
Thus, in addition to the restrictions implied by the limitation of bank loans, we must add other factors such as the increase in the unemployment rate or the persistence of poor economic conditions.
Faced with this situation and with the difference of passenger vehicles, aid for the purchase of the 2000E served little.
In addition to individuals, the self-employed and SMEs who wish to acquire a light commercial vehicle of less than 3.5 tons with a level of CO2 emissions that does not exceed 160 grams per kilometer, can also benefit, but the number of operations speaks for itself.
Yesterday, at Ganvam, it was again recalled the need for the administration to pay particular attention to these vehicles and also to industrial vehicles (trucks and buses).
The dealer association foresees a year 2010 similar to that which ends, for which it calculates a drop in sales (of passenger vehicles and 4x4) of 19%.
Emergency Braking Assistance Is Now Mandatory
As has taken place with the ABS anti-lock system and as will take place with ESP stability control, emergency braking assistance systems will be a mandatory element on European cars.
They are already mandatory on newly registered passenger cars and light commercial vehicles, while for the rest of vehicles a waiting period has been set until the end of February 2011.
The measure, imposed by the European Union, aims to strengthen the protection of pedestrians, even if, because of its mode of action, it can also help to avoid many rear shocks.
The Emergency Braking Assistant acts by increasing the pressure on the brakes in the event of an emergency detected by the system thanks to the kick given by the driver.
However, the latter does not always maintain pressure until the end.
According to a study conducted by car component manufacturer Bosch, one-third of the drivers involved in an accident were unable to press the brake before the collision, and half of them did not take full advantage of its strength.
Up to 1,100 Accidents
The intervention of the aid, on edge, requires the maximum of the brake system, reducing the distance that the car needs to stop.
According to an EU study, if all cars were equipped with this type, up to 1,100 fatal accidents in which pedestrians are involved could be avoided each year.
This reduction would be even greater with the popularization of so-called intelligent emergency braking systems: it is calculated that one in four claims with injuries would be avoided.
In this case, the vehicle is equipped with a system that, thanks to a radar or a camera, detects obstacles and warns the driver, in addition to acting slightly on the brakes.
If the driver reacts, he helps him by increasing the pressure on the pedal, if necessary.
If it does not react, and although the collision is inevitable, the device exerts the maximum effort on the brakes in order to minimize the damage.
General Motors postpones plan for Opel until January
As workers at the Opel plant in Figueruelas (Zaragoza) were warned by Nick Reilly, the new president of General Motors (GM) Europe, that production of the new Meriva would be launched during the first week of April – probably 8 – according to Javier Ortega, the North American manufacturer warned that it will need more time to conclude Opel’s contingency plan.
Nick Reilly, the new president of GM Europe, expects that each point in the plan has been approved in advance by the party concerned.
And this may imply that the final document, which will first be submitted to the workers and then to the governments of the countries where the factories are located, will be postponed until early 2010.
On the other hand, at GM, it is assured that a decision on the sale of Saab should be made in the coming days.
It is therefore not surprising that the Swedish government has tightened pressure on the U.S. manufacturer to make a decision as soon as possible.
GM admits to being in contact with possible new buyers.
One of them could be Geely, which also aspires to acquire Volvo, but Dutch sports car manufacturer Skyper’s candidacy, backed by Russian group Converg, is gaining importance.
In the event that there is no agreement with any of them, GM could sell part of Saab to China’s Baic.
The decision to close Saab at the end of the year if it is not sold is maintained.
At the wheel of the Porsche Boxster Spyder
To design their last and precious jewel, the engineers of Porsche left the Boxster S and managed to thin it from nothing less than 80 kilos, which leaves the scale at only 1 275 kilos.
This led them to do without, in the first place, the automatic roof which was replaced by a manual sheeted roof, making it possible to reduce by 21 kilos, to use aluminum doors such as those of the GT3, 15 other kilos less, to remove the air conditioner, 13 kilos, to reduce the fuel tank from 64 to 54 liters, 7 kilos of gained, to mount sports tickets, still 12 kilos,
Finally, they renounced the CDR-30 audio system, gaining another six kilograms and replaced the door handles with fabric handles, saving the remaining kilo.
Although the model also exists with the dual-clutched PDK transmission, the test was performed on a six-speed manual gearbox model, mainly on roads on the California secondary network, in the mountains, with particularly tortuous turns.
His behaviour can only be described as irreproachable.
The decrease in weight does not prevent the car from driving on the ground with plumb.
On the contrary: it has been a long time since we tested such a small and lightweight model which, at the same time, guarantees so much safety in corners, including at high speed.
He is a small athlete, able to warm up his muscles at low speed for the necessary time, until we ask him to sprint, brake, recover and push his engine again...
That’s all we wanted. Thanks to its direction, as communicative as precise, the car will do what our hands will indicate on the steering wheel.
And she'll do it with surgical precision.
Sound of motor at will
The sound sound V6 boxer, with 320 horsepower (10 more than the Boxster S) is particularly successful and can be reinforced with the “sport” option or thanks to a system that modifies the roar of the engine, already used on the Panamera.
Acceleration capacity is brutal, whether at a standstill or during recoveries.
Suspensions are firm but not hard or uncomfortable.
The pavement of the roads that were covered with the Boyxster Spyder was quite damaged, but the car managed the potholes well.
The electronic assistants barely made an act of presence throughout the journey, while on some corners we found ice in the early morning and gravel, later in the morning.
In short, an ultra-light, most entertaining car to drive and which is much more than a simple modification of the Boxster S.
The car will be put up for sale in February 2010, at a price of 70 831 euros.
" Bag Girl "
An intimate friend of Karl Lagerfeld, icon of the Coco Cocoon line and occasional starlet of the fashion shows of the house, the singer displays her face as bad girl with as much capacity as her Chanel bags.
More than a “Bad Girl” or an “It Girl,” it’s a “Bag Girl.”
Baskonia offers a victory in Israel
The Caja Laboral obtained a brilliant triumph at 91-82 during his visit to the arduous field of Maccabi Electra, despite the many absences suffered by the team of Dusko Ivanovic. She is thus at the top of his group, after the great show of Mirza Teletovic who, with eight three-point baskets was the best of his team (29 evaluation).
Vitoria’s team was able to fill the important absences of Herrmann, Oleson, Huertas or Micov with a large dose of commitment and collective work, even if they had to wait until the end to win.
Moreover, the great skill from the perimeter, with 16 baskets with three points scored, largely determined the final victory.
The match began quickly for the blues and red, who took the advantage from the beginning.
The fact that the first four baskets of Vitoria’s team were marked from the perimeter, foreshadowed that they had jumped on the Israeli field with warm wrists.
English and Teletovic were the main craftsmen of the 18-20 final of the first quarter.
The second quarter was in the same tone, but Alava’s team chose Barac’s inner play and Eliyahu’s work, which was received by cheers and applause back to his former home, to increase their advantage at the break (34-43).
Teletovic was already beginning to stand out as the best player of his team, with 13 points scored, although Splitter’s “minus six” evaluation already hinted that this night was not going to be the best of the Brazilian.
But in this match, despite the fact that they were on the right track at the break, nothing was played, far from it, and Avala’s team wanted to remind Vitoria’s that we don’t win so easily on their pitch, and thanks to “Elias’ hand”, they managed to get into the game.
Eidson and Pnini contributed decisively that the yolks took the advantage over the light (50-49).
Other difficulties
Ivanovic’s players, injured but far from dead, needed a few skillful actions to take the air, and it is at this time that the machine gun of Mirza Teletovic which, with three baskets with three consecutive points and the help of English and Ribas put the counter back to a reassuring score (54-67), and that the locals managed to accentuate at the end
Avala’s team went out to play it all for everything in the final period and English and San Emeterio (60-75) advised that they weren’t going to bore anyone taking what they’d deserved for the first thirty minutes.
Nevertheless, Pnini y Eidson, the best of their team, did not resign themselves and tried to support her from the line of three points (70-77).
Despite this, and with Splitter eliminated for fouls, Ivanovic’s players didn’t rush, defended well, and although the Israelis got closer in the last minute (80-85), the game was already played and the victory escaped Tel Aviv for the first time of the season.
Injuries prevent Unicaja from fighting battle
Unicaja lost loudly in Greece against Olympiacos and ceded the head of Group B of the Euroleague, in a match marked by the absences of Unicaja players, especially in the indoor game (Freeland, Lima and Archibald), who facilitated the Greek victory.
The Malaga team was not frightened by the atmosphere of the Pavilion of Peace and Friendship or by the size of the rival, arguably one of the most powerful in Europe and with a big budget.
The game of the first quarter, made the Andalusians dream, an aggressive defense and three baskets with three consecutive points, two from leader Omar Cook and one from Guillem Rubio who dazzled the 12-18 local team, in the ninth minute.
Olympiacos was surprised by Unicaja’s intensity, although the local team’s arsenal with the inner play of Bourousis, Vujcic and Schortsanitis made it possible to reverse the 22-20 score, in the thirteenth minute, when an action, a start of the fight, between North American leaders Beverley and Williams resulted in the disqualification.
The Greek team began to function by showing its superiority in both sides, as the injury outing of English pivot Joel Freeland and Carlos Jiménez’s three personal faults weighed on Unicaja.
Interior players and Lithuanian winger Linas Kleizas, with their three-point baskets, widened the 44-32 advantage in the nineteenth minute.
An impossible rise
The third period was particularly offensive for the Olympiacos which, despite the 19 losses, was superior and even managed to match the difference of the match played at Malaga 66-48, in the twenty-seventh minute, causing the head of the group.
Unicaja were trying to overcome absences and the last exit of Scottish pivot Robert Archibald, injured during the game.
Despite all these problems, Unicaja, thanks to its organization, Cook’s three-point baskets, and Lewis’ support, left some hope with 66-57, in the thirty-first minute.
But victory was practically impossible because the Olympiacos was relentless when the players put pressure and showed their superiority in centimeters in the area.
The Croatian pivot Nikola Vujcic, managed to raise the score to 81-62, in the thirty-seventh minute, and Unicaja submitted, accepting the victory of Olympiacos.
How much should you get paid based on your Facebook profile?
Facebook users can know the salary they would deserve based on their profile, thanks to a new Trovit app, the search engine with the largest number of classified real estate ads, jobs and vehicles in Spain.
The tool “How much should you get paid?” calculates the salaries of users from more than 140 000 job offers that Trovit has in Spain, from a form available on Facebook, where you have to register your profession, your years of experience, your age and the city where you want to work.
For a week that the application has been operating, the search engine has detected that, as a rule, the Spanish worker “feels poorly remunerated,” Trovit’s product manager Albert Ribera said in a statement.
According to the data available to Trovit from its search engines in the United Kingdom and in France, Spaniards are less paid.
A British computer programmer collects 45 226 euros gross annual and one French, 31 059, while a Spanish receives 24 000 euros.
In the same way, a driver collects 34 247 euros gross annually in England, 25 751 in France and 16 420 in Spain.
According to Trovit, the less well paid work in Spain is that of server, with 11 592 euros net annual, followed by that of seller, with 14 725 euros, and that of worker, with 15 667 euros.
The most sought-after jobs on the Internet in the last three months are drivers, part-time jobs and social workers.
Brazil sets a date for the departure of Zelaya
The Brazilian government has set a deadline for Manuel Zelaya's stay at his Tegucigalpa embassy.
The inverted president will have to abandon the diplomatic legation no later than 27 January 2010, the date of the official end of his mandate.
Francisco Catunda, Chargé d’affaires of the Brazilian Embassy in the capital of Honduras, confirmed to TV Globo that “Mel” knows that he must leave the embassy, where he has been a refugee since September 21, before that date.
"He is aware that he must leave on January 27, at the end of his mandate, he must look for another destination."
The channel also cited the former agent, who confirmed his intention to leave the embassy before the end of the established deadline: “My position is to leave as soon as possible, logically with the support of the Brazilian government,” Zelaya said by phone.
Following the November 29 elections (during which Porfirio Lobo was elected), Zelaya assured that he would remain in the Brazilian embassy until January.
However, in the same week he tried to seek refuge in Brazil.
The operation finally failed, since Zelaya refused the condition of political exile that the de facto government chaired by Roberto Micheletti intended to impose.
Thank you very much
After rosy days since last spring, during which asset inflation was the prevailing rating in the markets, uncertainty arises again, which is not surprising since it is their natural state, in an environment where agents make decisions about a future inherently uncertain.
The remedy used by central banks to stabilize markets has been to monetize debt through the direct purchase of assets and indirectly by donating abundant liquidity at rates close to zero, so that financial institutions buy assets to mobilize.
Central banks have already confirmed that direct purchases of assets have been virtually finalized and, without questioning their non-conformist liquidity policy, are starting to put a bit of sand in the cockroaches, as the ECB did, with their auction a year before next week.
As a result, investors doubt the ability of the global economy to maintain economic recovery without heterodox measures, and we are witnessing a reversal of risk premium reduction.
Stock market volatility and corporate bond spreads are increasing again, although they remain far removed from the paranormal levels they reached at the beginning of the year.
States have been forced to socialize private risk, and public debt is affected again.
There is once again a flight towards quality and the United States, Japan and Germany have been the shelters chosen by investors, provoking an increase in differences between the countries of the eurozone and Germany.
Spain was barely affected by this episode of instability, but then Standard & Poor'sa threatened with a further downward revision of the qualification of the Kingdom of Spain.
On the other hand, a little uncertainty is not bad to silence the advocates of moral risk, who were again trying to provoke a case of conscience among those responsible for global economic policy who had curbed the destructive effects derived from the limitation of credit on the destruction of employment, when what would have to done is to decree a new day of Thanksgiving.
However, Dubai’s situation resembles a tribal struggle between the emirs to restore its place to power in the emirates and we have already seen the case of Greece.
A government that conceals a public deficit, elections, and a new government that discovers the pot with roses.
Prodi did so in Italy after Berlusconi and Socrates in Portugal after Durão Barroso.
It would be perfectly irrational for Ecofin to save Hungary from non-compliance with its external payments at the beginning of the year and allow a eurozone country not to meet its commitments.
What, on the other hand, makes logical, is that the Ecofin has encouraged the Greek government to transparency and to make its citizens co-responsible for the need for extraordinary measures to maintain the stability of its public finances.
If, as we hope, we are faced with an episode of normalization of transient risk premiums, after a few excesses in high positions, this would be predictable.
In the Spanish case, as long as our differences in public debt increase commensurate with European corporate bonds, we do not have to worry.
If the European gaps diminished and ours continued to increase, then it would be an idiosyncratic Spanish risk.
When our predictive indicators require most study services to anticipate their recovery models and to revise their forecasts upwards for 2011, along with the OECD and the European Commission, which return to the charge with a Japanese model for the Spanish economy, it does not augure much hope for their warning.
Once again, it seems to be an excellent opportunity to buy Spanish assets, even if for the moment “caution is a mother of safety”.
Connected Sectors
Iberdrola President Ignacio Sánchez Galán was the first to speak yesterday before the subcommittee of the Congress of Deputies, where Spain’s energy strategy for the next two decades is being discussed these days.
Following his speech, which was followed by the president of Gas Natural-Fenosa, Rafael Villaseca, Mr. Galán sketched for the press what he had mentioned before the subcommittee, eight-closed.
For the president of Iberdrola, Spain needs to invest 50 000 million euros until 2020 and 60 000 million more between this date and 2030, in the event that the nuclear power plants currently in operation would remain open.
If this is not the case, the necessary investments would not amount to 60 000 million but to 95 000.
However, Iberdola considers that for the next decade, investments should not be devoted to increasing production capacity, but distribution networks and international interconnections.
As of 2020, the question changes, and we will have to increase power.
Its bet focuses on both gas and coal-fired power plants, with carbon dioxide recovery mechanisms and, above all, on renewable energies.
In this area, it considers that 18 000 MW more are needed, along with other “support energies”.
Galán, who believes that the Sustainable Economy Bill that the government has just approved is in line with what his company demands, did not wish to attack the royal decree to encourage the consumption of national coal, which contradicts policies to fight global warming.
According to him, there is “a clear long-term bet” in this sense, despite the “punctual” measures, as the president of Iberdrola called them.
In any case, Galán believes that decisions to invest, although they are located in the long term, need to be taken, as they take some time to reach maturity and that a stable remunerative framework is needed to address them.
Ferrovial wins Aer Lingus Handling Market in Ten Airports
The Swiss company Swissport, a subsidiary of Ferrovial for airport services on land, will act as a privileged partner of the Irish airline Aer Lingus, for freight services, at ten European airports where it operates.
Paris, London, Frankfurt and Brussels are one of the most important.
The agreement, whose economic terms have not been revealed, has a duration of five years.
On the other hand, Ferrovial, Junta de Castilla-La Mancha and the University of Alcalá de Henares yesterday signed a collaboration protocol to create the Intelligent Infrastructure Innovation Center.
The alliance expects an investment of 20 million until 2012, of which 50% will come from Ferrovial.
One of the centre’s tasks will be to conduct energy efficiency research on highways and airports.
Acciona competes in its first highway in Australia in Hochtief and Bouygues
Acciona is fighting for its first-time concession highway in Australia, a market on which it is already working as an energy company and water manager.
The project at stake is an estimated tunnel of 1 700 million Australian dollars (approximately 1,047 million euros).
The French company Bouygues and the German company Hochtief are its rivals.
A 5-kilometer-long tunnel in the Australian city of Brisbane, on the East Coast, is the option to allow Acciona to settle in the country as a public works company and motorway concessionaire.
The town hall of the city wishes to be able to dispose of it by the end of 2014.
After having chosen the public / private collaboration (PPP for its initials in English) to carry out the project, as part of a business plan under the supervision of Erns & Young, the local administration already has a list of finalists who will be responsible for the construction site and operation of the toll.
Awaiting the presentation of their offers next May, the planned investment amounts to 1 700 million Australian dollars (about 1 047 million euros) and in the last phase compete with three consortia, with a significant presence of European manufacturers.
The Transcity group is led by Acciona, the only smooth Spanish company, and its associates are BMD and Ghella.
The first is one of Australia’s largest builders, while the second, installed in Italy, is one of Europe’s leading specialists in underground works.
Against Acciona’s team, the Northern Direct consortium is built, with the presence of the British company Laing O’Rourke, the Australian infrastructure manager Transfield and the French builder group Bouygues.
The third and final consortium that passed the event is LBRJV, whose capital is divided between the Australian Leighton (a subsidiary of the German manufacturer Hochtief, who in turn has the participation of ACS), another local company, Baulderstone (owned by the German group Bilfinger Berger) and the Welsh company specializing in public works, Razel.
Australia’s infrastructure market promises major projects and privatizations, but entering it is a great challenge, if not through the acquisition of a local construction company.
A strategy followed by the companies Hochtief and Bilfinger, already mentioned, in addition to their Asian references, and which still remains an unvalidated step for the Spanish giants of the sector.
ACS has just conquered a place in this market through its 30% stake in Hochtief, owner of Leighton.
Millionaire Assets
Acciona relies on a geographical area of the utmost importance for the sector, on which it already has a wide presence through its activity as an energy supplier and in the desalination sector.
Among other assets, it has the largest wind farm, that of Waubra (192 MW) in Victoria, and manages the group of companies responsible for erecting and managing Adelaide’s desalinator, with a budget of 700 million.
It now intends to consolidate these two areas and take advantage of synergies to enter the construction and infrastructure management markets.
The underground toll lane, promoted by the Brisbane City Infrastructure Bureau and the so-called Northern Link, is set to connect the Western Toowong Highway to Kelvin Grove (see map), by 4 kilometers of tunnel. This is the largest existing project to reduce traffic congestion in Brisbane.
The schedule set by the administration indicates that the auction will take place in the summer of 2010 and that work will begin in December of next year.
Despite the fact that this toll track will be open to traffic at the end of 2014, it is not expected that the project will be completed before 2016.
Tunnel began to be designed in 2005.
It will consist of a two-way, an electronic toll system and a complex ventilation system.
The Government of Queensland will bring 500 million Australian dollars (308 million euros) for its financing.
Brisbane is already offering work to Hochtief
Australia continues to make Hochtief, which yesterday mentioned getting a contract with the government valued at 154 million.
The company owned by ACS will equip, through its local subsidiary Leighton, the infrastructure of the national high-speed fiber optic network.
This intervention will allow 400 000 people to have quick access to the Internet and will provide work to the German company for 18 months.
On the other hand, the company ceded the 27-storey King George Tower construction project in the city of Brisbane. The work has not yet begun and Hochtief has concluded the sale for 129 million.
In the United Kingdom, more specifically in Manchester, it will share with Laing O'Rourke the construction and management of two school groups for 25 years.
The initial investment will be 75 million euros.
FCC is awarded a tunnel in Slovenia for 64 million
FCC, through its subsidiary Alpine, is awarded the construction of a 2.1-kilometre-long tunnel for a motorway in Slovenia, amounting to 64.5 million euros.
The company obtained this contract a few days after being in charge of the expansion of the Bosruck tunnel (Austria), for a sum of 130 million.
Alpine consolidates its experience in the construction of tunnels.
The start-up of the project is planned for early 2010 and its realization will take 32 months.
Sra. Rushmore was awarded Renfe's advertisement
Renfe has entrusted the advertising and marketing services of the next two years to the agency Sra. Rushmore, for a budget of about three million euros per year, said the railway operator.
The contract mentions the possibility of two extensions of 12 months each. Sra. Rushmore has been chosen from 16 agencies that have appeared at Renfe’s call for tenders, of which four have been finalists, including TBWA, which has been responsible for creativity over the past three years.
From now on, Sra. Rushmore will be responsible for the design and production of the campaigns.
A fine of 63 million to France Télécom to curb the competition
The French competition regulator has imposed a fine of 63 million euros on France Télécom for its anti-competitive conduct in the Caribbean region belonging to the country.
According to this institution, the operator has limited competition on fixed and mobile telephony through the signing of exclusive agreements, tariffs and consumer loyalty programmes on the margins of regulation.
With this sanction, the old French monopoly and still a state-owned company, accumulates more than 450 million euros in fines since 1994, because of the obstacles it puts to its competitors.
The sanction imposed yesterday has also been increased because the authority that oversees the competition considers that France Telecom has committed similar infringements in the past and is therefore recidivist.
An application store
On the other hand, the mobile telephony subsidiary of the French company has followed the wake of other operators and has opened an Internet application store, with the aim to increase revenues that do not come from the transmission of voices.
Initially, Orange’s application store will be active in France and the United Kingdom.
It will have to wait until 2010 for it to arrive in other countries such as Spain.
Their participation in the Champions League has contributed 476.4 million to Real Madrid and Barça since its inception
The Champions League has become a source of income for clubs, since its birth in 1992.
Indeed, Real Madrid and F.C. Barcelona won 467.4 million, placing them in third and fifth place of the clubs that pocketed the most money in this competition.
The Manchester United are at the top of the list.
The Manchester United are at the top of the list.
Winning the UEFA Champions League not only brings fame; it is also one of the main sources of income for big clubs.
Indeed, the competition allocated 5 362.5 million euros to the 105 teams (including 12 Spaniards) who have participated in the event since the creation of the tournament, during the 1992-1993 season, according to UEFA information.
Real Madrid and F.C. Barcelona were well served in this distribution.
The white club was the third of the continent to receive the most money for its participation, and the only one, with A.C. Milan, having won the trophy three times.
The Catalan club, currently holding the title and the last winner of the former European Cup in 1992, is fifth on the list.
At the top of the list are the Manchester United and Bayern of Munich, the two teams which, in addition, have charged the most money for their participation in the last Champions League.
Even though they lost in front of Barca.
On the other hand, Spain is only the third country, with regard to the money reported by the Champions League, although it has won more titles than any other (five, while Italy has won four, England three and Germany two).
Distribution
This apparent paradox can be explained by UEFA’s complete procedure for the allocation of money.
For this year, the organization chaired by Michel Platini plans to win 1 090 million thanks to the Champions League.
From this sum, UEFA will devote 413.1 million to fixed payments: each of the 20 clubs that participated in the classification matches (among which Atlético de Madrid) received 2.1 million.
In addition, those who managed to participate received only 3.8 million to be there, in addition to 550 00 euros for each match played.
There is also a bonus in case of victory (800 000 euros) and tie (400 000 for each team). Those who arrive in the eighth finals receive three million each, the eight teams that arrive in the quarter-finals receive 3.3 million.
The last four teams will each receive 4 million.
And the champion will win 9 million, against the 5.2 received by the other finalist.
Thus, for fixed payments, a club will earn at least 7.1 million, and a maximum of 31.2 million.
On the other hand, Barca pocketed 2.5 million for winning the European Super Cup in August.
But in addition, there are the variable payments, to which 337.8 million are spent.
This money is distributed according to the proportional value of each televised market.
The Spanish market is one of the most important, and has four designated places.
The champion of the previous year pockets 40% of the money allocated, 30% for the second, 20% for the third and 10% for the fourth.
The other side will be paid based on the number of matches played during the current year’s edition.
Nueva Rumasa offers 61 million euros for 29.9% of Sos.
The Nueva Rumasa group listed yesterday the details of the offer to buy shares by which it intends to acquire a maximum of 25 to 29.9% of the capital of Sos.
A percentage that does not require him to launch a takeover bid (OPA) for all securities.
Concretely, Nueva Rumasa offers 1,5025 euros per share, which will be disbursed in 10 years and will be remunerated at a rate of 1% per year for this postponement.
Yesterday, the shares of Sos finished at 1.85 euros; Nueva Rumasa’s offer therefore evaluates the company 18% below the market.
According to Nueva Rumasa, the 1.50 euro price reflects the actual valuation of Sos.
The offer is conditioned by the appointment of eight of the 15 advisors.
Jerez’s group gives a 15-day period for the shareholders to indicate their interest in the sale of the securities.
The CNMV assured that it had not been informed of this offer, although the Ruiz Mateos company claimed that it had made contact with the regulator.
If it took place, the disbursement would amount to 611.7 million, while the capital of Sos is 254.27 million.
In the meantime, Sos continues the restructuring of his management.
In an attempt to remain outside possible offers to buy and from the judicial back and forth of its former directors, brothers Jesús and Jaime Salazar, she announced the election of María Luis Jordá as Director of Internal Auditing.
A decision that makes her the first woman to hold a leadership position in the food business.
It is a newly created position that will depend directly on the chairman of the group.
And it is not free of controversy, since the company remains overwhelmed in the legal battle for the supposed embezzlement of more than 230 million Sos to Salazar companies.
Moreover, the possibility that in the coming months Sos reveals new embezzlement of funds cannot be ruled out.
Regarding credit to the Salazars, former advisers Ildefonso Ortega and Ángel Fernández Noriega (representatives of the CCM and Unicaja) said yesterday before the National Audience that they had been informed of the embezzlement of 212 million without knowing the destination.
CNMV Authorizes Paternina’s OPA by Inversora Mer
The National Securities Market Commission (CNMV) yesterday authorised the OPA of exclusion presented by Inversora Mer on Federico Paternina on October 9.
The monitoring body explained that it was authorizing this transaction because it understood that its terms were adjusting to current standards and that it considered “sufficient” the content of the explanatory slip presented, following the amendments applied on December 2.
The CNMV clarified that the offer concerns the acquisition of 550 008 shares of Federico Paternina at a price of 7.65 euros per share, which represents 8.95% of the share capital, composed of 6,142,786 shares admitted to trading on the Madrid and Bilbao stock exchanges.
He further explained that 5 592 778 million shares have been fixed, and that they could in no way be transmitted before the finalization of the offer, which he indicated that the price had been fixed by Federico Paternina, in accordance with the provisions of the standards in force.
New tools for car expertise
In these days of bad weather and accidents, car garages are not the only winners.
Every day, thousands of automobiles that have suffered a disaster are brought back to a garage, where an expert has to assess the damage and the cost of the repair.
Each claim is different, and calculating the cost of parts and labour time can be a long and complex job.
Calculate by hand the cost of a repair between 20 000 and 30 000 euros, including parts and labor can take two or three hours.
On the other hand, with the assistance of the latest IT tools, it would be a matter of minutes.
It is for this reason that almost 100% of experts use this type of systems.
Providers of services and solutions for claims assessment and maintenance are dedicated to the collection of information provided by manufacturers, and then process it and offer it to their customers in the form of a useful tool.
In Spain, Audatex, from the North American group Solera, is the leading company; 80% of the estimates are made with its products.
The other competing companies in the field of evaluation tools and the development of information on the sector are GtMotive and Eurotax.
Working with the data provided by manufacturers is far from simple.
According to Eduardo Velázquez, commercial director of Audatex, the company in Spain with 63 manufacturers and importers, and each of them provides them with information in a different format.
Moreover, the amount of data to be processed continues to increase, so that over the past five years, more versions, variants and engines have been launched than in the previous fifteen years.
When a new car goes out on the market, these companies go to work.
This is a very arduous job, because you have to catalogue each of the thousand parts of the vehicle and enter its price.
In addition, an “intelligent” graphic image of each model on which you can click on the different parts of a passenger car.
If it’s a massively sold model, the work can be ready in a week if the process progresses very quickly.
If it is a low-selling car, it can take three or four months. Currently, Audatex has complete information on 99.2% of cars marketed in Spain.
Among the most exclusive models, for example, they have information on Porsches, but not Ferrari or Lamborghini.
With regard to vehicles in circulation, the figure exceeds 99%.
To carry out documentation and database development work, Audatex has eight centres spread around the world (in Spain, France, Germany, the United States, Brazil, Mexico, China and Japan), and each specializes in certain brands.
The national headquarters is located in Alcobendas (Madrid).
There, more than one hundred technicians (usually mechanical experts) work simultaneously on three computer screens.
Audatex invests 90 million every year to develop these databases.
Every month, 2.5 million data is recorded, and customers receive updates every two days.
The development of this huge flow of information allows these companies to give them different uses and to offer various products.
The most common application concerns repair and maintenance, but depending on the countries, there are also tools for the management of breakage parts or to calculate the venal or residual value of a car.
The only company that has a guide that provides information on the value of used cars in Spain is Eurotax.
Audatex doesn’t offer it in our country, but it does it in the United States.
In view of Audatex’s products, it’s easy to get an idea of what kind of support it can provide to professionals in the sector.
This company offers ten different tools, the flagship being AudaPlus, the standard claims valuation solution for experts.
It includes cars, motorcycles, light and heavy industrial vehicles.
In addition to the cost of the parts, it takes into account the repair times estimated by the manufacturer or the painting scales.
This tool is bought according to its use, for each estimate.
The highest cost is 5.75 euros, for customers who use it little, at most ten times a month.
Then, each insurance company, which carries out thousands of experts, negotiates its price with Audatex.
Other products, such as AudaVIN, allow the total identification of a vehicle and the equipment with which it came out of the assembly line, only thanks to its chassis number.
AudaGlass focuses on vehicle lenses, and AudaSubastas is a server open to the professional sector on the Internet that makes it possible to bid on vehicles declared wrecks.
Its statistical service is very useful to identify trends, perform geo-demographic analyses or know common problems by brand or model.
It is possible to study, for example, how many models of a given brand went to the garage for a breakdown or how many suffered severe claims for a given period.
Gt Motive is the only Spanish company dedicated to the development of such tools.
It markets, among other things, Gt Estimate, which is also intended for experts and provides digital images, as well as access to the manufacturer’s official parts, prices and times.
Gt Motive is an Einsa Group company, founded in 1971 as a provider of services and solutions for the assessment of claims, breakdowns or maintenance.
The Evaluation WG Guide is a historical reference product on the market.
The company works with 24,000 users in garages, 3,100 experts and 53 insurance companies.
Its business volume has increased at a rate of 20% per year over the last three years, and made a turnover of 10.8 million euros in 2008.
At the beginning of last year, she embarked on her process of internationalization, with the establishment of an office in Paris.
Audatex was born in Germany in 1966 and moved to Spain in 1979.
According to the company’s sources, 62% of its turnover is realized in Europe and the rest of the world, and the remaining 38% in North and South America. It works with 900 insurance companies and 33 000 garages, of which 3 000 are in Spain.
Solera Group’s global turnover (Audatex, Sidexa, Informex ABZ, Hollander and IMS) will amount to US$557 million, 3.3% more than in 2008.
In our country, the company will receive 19.1 million euros, or 7.2% more than last year.
In 2005, the Solera group was listed on the stock exchange 1 000 million dollars, today its shares are valued at a total of 2 400 million dollars.
Since 2007, it has been sided on the New York Stock Exchange and is part of the Standard & Poor's benchmark index.
Auction on the Internet of crashed cars
Last October, the Solera group bought Autoonline, an Internet platform for the sale of cars, motorcycles and screened special vehicles.
The transaction involved a cost of 59.5 million euros for 85% of the company’s capital, which could expand next year to the remaining 15%.
The objective of the acquisition was to offer more value-added services to its customers, with the ability to access a huge international market.
This valuation and sales service is aimed exclusively at professionals in the sector, such as insurance companies, experts, rental companies, garages, breakages and professional sellers who must be registered before starting operations.
This platform was created in 1996 in Germany and manages more than 650 000 operations throughout Europe, including 500 000 in Germany.
Among its main markets are Spain, Greece, Poland and Turkey.
There are currently more than 1500 sellers and 4,0000 experts registered on the site.
It manages 3 000 ads per day and on the Spanish market, 100 new advertisements are published every day.
According to Spanish law, a car whose chassis number has been removed can no longer circulate, but on the other hand, some parts that are not damaged can be used as spare parts.
Collectible cars can be re-recorded, although they have been removed, after intricate steps.
Garage Quotation in Four Steps
With the Auda-Taller tool, Audatex guarantees that the user obtains a quote in just four steps: identify the vehicle, look for the spare part, create a quote and generate it.
Ease of use is an essential element of these systems, especially to convince older professionals who, to a greater or lesser extent, are reluctant to use new management techniques.
The database consists of 1,034 vehicles, including 666 passengers, 109 all-terrains, 137 motorcycles, 78 vans and 44 trucks.
Naturally, to use it, a computer and an internet connection are required.
Once the model to be repaired is identified thanks to its chassis number, a real graphic image of the model in question appears and then the necessary part is selected.
The user can include variables such as labor prices, surcharges or discounts, and then they can print the quote to hand it over to the customer.
AudaTaller is not an estimation tool like AudaPlus, but a catalogue of references.
The main difference between these two systems is that one intended for garages does not include estimated labor times.
The cost of using this tool for the garage is 350 euros per year, which can be credited.
Currently, Audatex is working to ensure that these images are in three dimensions.
This is an innovation that mechanics see with a very good eye, since it will allow, for example, to select a door and rotate it to see the internal face.
